</ol></nav>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1. </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <span id="S1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">M</span> be a loopless matroid of positive rank <math id="S1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>. In <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite>,
Feichtner and Yuzvinsky introduced the Chow ring <math id="S1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{\operatorname{CH}}(\textrm{M})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>CH</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext>M</mtext><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{\operatorname{CH}}(\textrm{M})</annotation></semantics></math>
as the Chow ring of the toric variety associated to the Bergman fan of <span id="S1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">M</span>.
This toric variety has a boundary complex of torus-equivariant subvarieties isomorphic to the order complex <math id="S1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{\Delta}(\textrm{M})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext>M</mtext><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{\Delta}(\textrm{M})</annotation></semantics></math> of the lattice of flats of <span id="S1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">M</span>. The <em id="S1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Chow polynomial</em> and <em id="S1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">chain polynomial</em> of <span id="S1.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">M</span>
are, respectively, the Hilbert–Poincaré polynomial of <math id="S1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{\operatorname{CH}}(\textrm{M})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>CH</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext>M</mtext><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{\operatorname{CH}}(\textrm{M})</annotation></semantics></math>
and the face polynomial of this boundary complex:</p>
<table id="S1.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{\textrm{H}}(\textrm{M};t)=\sum_{i=0}^{r-1}\dim\underline{\operatorname{CH}}^{i}(\textrm{M})t^{i},\qquad f(\textrm{M};t)\coloneqq\sum_{i=0}^{r}f_{i-1}(\underline{\Delta}(\textrm{M}))t^{i}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mtext>H</mtext><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext>M</mtext><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mrow><msup><munder accentunder="true"><mi>CH</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext>M</mtext><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext>M</mtext><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">≔</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi>r</mi></munderover><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext>M</mtext><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{\textrm{H}}(\textrm{M};t)=\sum_{i=0}^{r-1}\dim\underline{\operatorname{CH}}^{i}(\textrm{M})t^{i},\qquad f(\textrm{M};t)\coloneqq\sum_{i=0}^{r}f_{i-1}(\underline{\Delta}(\textrm{M}))t^{i}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Later in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite>, Braden,
Huh, Matherne, Proudfoot and Wang introduced the toric variety of the augmented Bergman fan of <span id="S1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">M</span>, and defined the augmented Chow ring <math id="S1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{CH}(\textrm{M})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>CH</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext>M</mtext><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{CH}(\textrm{M})</annotation></semantics></math> as its Chow ring. Its Hilbert–Poincaré
polynomial is the <em id="S1.p1.2.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">augmented Chow polynomial</em>:</p>
<table id="S1.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textrm{H}(\textrm{M};t)=\sum_{i=0}^{r}\dim\operatorname{CH}^{i}(\textrm{M})t^{i}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>H</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext>M</mtext><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi>r</mi></munderover><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>CH</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext>M</mtext><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textrm{H}(\textrm{M};t)=\sum_{i=0}^{r}\dim\operatorname{CH}^{i}(\textrm{M})t^{i}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">These polynomial invariants of the toric varieties of matroids only depend on the underlying matroids, and have been proven to enjoy many positivity properties, coming from combinatorial Hodge theory, and the structural properties of the boundary complexes.
In the seminal paper by Adiprasito, Huh and Katz <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite>, the Chow ring of a matroid, despite being the Chow ring of a possibly incomplete toric variety, satisfies the Kähler package, similar to the cohomology ring of a smooth projective variety. Furthermore, Braden, Huh, Matherne, Proudfoot and Wang proved the corresponding statements for the augmented Chow ring
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>, Theorem 1.6]</cite>. In particular, as a result of Poincaré duality and the hard Lefschetz theorem, the Hilbert–Poincaré series <math id="S1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{\textrm{H}}(\textrm{M};t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mtext>H</mtext><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext>M</mtext><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{\textrm{H}}(\textrm{M};t)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textrm{H}(\textrm{M};t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>H</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext>M</mtext><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textrm{H}(\textrm{M};t)</annotation></semantics></math> have symmetric, unimodal
coefficients.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">For the chain polynomials, the boundary complex <math id="S1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{\Delta}(\textrm{M})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext>M</mtext><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{\Delta}(\textrm{M})</annotation></semantics></math> is well-known to be vertex decomposable, and hence shellable. This implies that the sequence of face numbers <math id="S1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{i}(\underline{\Delta}(\textrm{M}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext>M</mtext><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{i}(\underline{\Delta}(\textrm{M}))</annotation></semantics></math> is strongly flawless, and that the <math id="S1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math>-vector of <math id="S1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{\Delta}(\textrm{M})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext>M</mtext><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{\Delta}(\textrm{M})</annotation></semantics></math> is nonnegative <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite>. Coron, Ferroni and Li studied structural properties of the nested set complexes in the most general setting of arbitrary matroids with building sets <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite>. They proved that
reduced nested set complexes of matroids with arbitrary building sets
are vertex decomposable, hence shellable, and admit convex ear
decompositions <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>, Corollary 1.2 and Theorem 1.3]</cite>.
The existence of convex ear decompositions, for the maximal building sets, implies that the <math id="S1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math>-polynomial of the order complex <math id="S1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{\Delta}(\textrm{M})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext>M</mtext><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{\Delta}(\textrm{M})</annotation></semantics></math>, written as <math id="S1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h(\textrm{M};t)=\sum_{i=0}^{d}h_{i}t^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext>M</mtext><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi>d</mi></msubsup><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h(\textrm{M};t)=\sum_{i=0}^{d}h_{i}t^{i}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d=r-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=r-1</annotation></semantics></math> is strongly flawless: for every <math id="S1.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\leqslant i\leqslant\lfloor d/2\rfloor" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>⩽</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>⩽</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌋</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\leqslant i\leqslant\lfloor d/2\rfloor</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
<table id="S1.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{0}\leqslant h_{1}\leqslant\cdots\leqslant h_{\lfloor d/2\rfloor},\qquad h_{i}\leqslant h_{d-i}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>⩽</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.1389em">⩽</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.1389em">⋯</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em">⩽</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌋</mo></mrow></msub></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>⩽</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{0}\leqslant h_{1}\leqslant\cdots\leqslant h_{\lfloor d/2\rfloor},\qquad h_{i}\leqslant h_{d-i}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p3.2" class="ltx_p">In particular, it is top-heavy <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>, Corollary 1.4]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">However, stronger positivity properties of the Hilbert–Poincaré series <math id="S1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{\textrm{H}}(\textrm{M};t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mtext>H</mtext><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext>M</mtext><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{\textrm{H}}(\textrm{M};t)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textrm{H}(\textrm{M};t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>H</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext>M</mtext><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textrm{H}(\textrm{M};t)</annotation></semantics></math> need to come beyond the Kähler package; and similarly for the face enumeration polynomials <math id="S1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(\textrm{M};t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext>M</mtext><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(\textrm{M};t)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h(\textrm{M};t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext>M</mtext><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h(\textrm{M};t)</annotation></semantics></math>, they must come beyond the structural properties mentioned above. For instance, Ferroni, Matherne, Stevens and Vecchi proved <math id="S1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math>-positivity for both polynomials <math id="S1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{\textrm{H}}(\textrm{M};t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mtext>H</mtext><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext>M</mtext><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{\textrm{H}}(\textrm{M};t)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textrm{H}(\textrm{M},t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>H</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext>M</mtext><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textrm{H}(\textrm{M},t)</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite> as an immediate application of the semi-small decomposition of the Chow ring and the augmented Chow ring <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Real-rootedness of a polynomial with nonnegative real coefficients is widely regarded as the strongest positivity property. It is equivalent to the total nonnegativity of the infinite Töeplitz matrix associated to the polynomial, and implies log-concavity without internal zeros by a classical result of Newton <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a>]</cite>. For palindromic polynomials, it implies <math id="S1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math>-positivity. We refer to the beautiful handbook by Brändén on the relationships among these positivity properties <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Along this line, Huh and Stevens, and independently Ferroni and Schröter, proposed the following conjecture for the Chow polynomials of matroids.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmthm1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_conjecture">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmthm1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Conjecture 1.1</span></span><span id="S1.Thmthm1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">43</a>, Conjecture 4.1.3]</cite>
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>, Conjecture 8.18]</cite>)</h6>
<div id="S1.Thmthm1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmthm1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For any loopless matroid <span id="S1.Thmthm1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">M</span>, the polynomial <math id="S1.Thmthm1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{\textrm{H}}(\textrm{M};t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mtext>H</mtext><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext>M</mtext><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{\textrm{H}}(\textrm{M};t)</annotation></semantics></math> is real-rooted.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Huh and Stevens proposed the augmented analogue
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">43</a>, Conjecture 4.3.3]</cite>; see also
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>, Conjecture 1.6]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmthm2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_conjecture">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmthm2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Conjecture 1.2</span></span><span id="S1.Thmthm2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Huh–Stevens)</h6>
<div id="S1.Thmthm2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmthm2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For any loopless matroid <span id="S1.Thmthm2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">M</span>, the polynomial <math id="S1.Thmthm2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textrm{H}(\textrm{M};t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>H</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext>M</mtext><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textrm{H}(\textrm{M};t)</annotation></semantics></math> is real-rooted.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p8.1" class="ltx_p">Around the same time, Athanasiadis and Kalampogia-Evangelinou conjectured the following analogous statement
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>, Conjecture 1.2]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmthm3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_conjecture">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmthm3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Conjecture 1.3</span></span><span id="S1.Thmthm3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Athanasiadis–Kalampogia-Evangelinou)</h6>
<div id="S1.Thmthm3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmthm3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For any loopless matroid <span id="S1.Thmthm3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">M</span>, the polynomial <math id="S1.Thmthm3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(\textrm{M};t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext>M</mtext><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(\textrm{M};t)</annotation></semantics></math> is real-rooted.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p9.1" class="ltx_p">These conjectures have stimulated much research activity in recent years, and they are proven to hold for several prominant classes of matroids. For chain polynomials, Athanasiadis and Kalampogia-Evangelinou proved real-rootedness of chain polynomials
for subspace lattices, partition lattices and their type <math id="S1.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> analogues,
as well as the uniform matroids and near-pencil cases
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>, Theorem 1.3]</cite>.
Brändén and Leite established it for perfect matroid designs,
Dowling geometries and paving matroids <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>,
and for all matroids of rank at most <math id="S1.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math>
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>, Theorem 6.1]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p10.1" class="ltx_p">For Chow polynomials and the augmented Chow polynomials, their real-rootedness for uniform matroids were first proved <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>, Theorem 1.10]</cite> and by Brändén and Vecchi <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>, Theorem 1.1]</cite>.
Soon after, Hoster and Stump generalized both results to finite graded simplicial
posets with nonnegative <math id="S1.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math>-vectors <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>, Theorem 1.1]</cite>.
Brändén and Vecchi also proved real-rootedness of Chow and
augmented Chow polynomials for totally nonnegative posets and their duals, and
for paving matroids <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>, Theorem 6.1 and Corollary 7.2]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p11.1" class="ltx_p">Concurrently, Coron, Ferroni and Li introduced UMEL-shellable posets, which are posets admitting an EL-labeling satisfying uniformity and monotonicity conditions. They proved real-rootedness of these polynomials for UMEL-shellable posets <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>, Theorems 1.2 and 1.5]</cite>. For matroids, UMEL-shellable posets include the lattice of flats of prominent families of matroids such as uniform matroids, affine and projective
geometries, braid matroids of types A and B, all Dowling geometries, and more generally rank uniform supersolvable matroids, together with their truncations and rank-selections <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>, Theorem 1.6, Theorem 1.8 and Proposition 7.1]</cite>.
In recent work on matroid analogues of Gal’s conjecture, Coron, Ferroni and Li further proved that <math id="S1.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{\textrm{H}}(\textrm{M};t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mtext>H</mtext><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext>M</mtext><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{\textrm{H}}(\textrm{M};t)</annotation></semantics></math> is
real-rooted for every matroid of rank at most <math id="S1.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>6</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">6</annotation></semantics></math>
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>, Corollary 8.5]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p12.1" class="ltx_p">The main goal of this paper is to construct explicit infinite families of counterexamples to all the conjectures above. They share a common source which comes from the failure of log-concavity of flag <math id="S1.p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math>-vectors. This failure can also be rephrased into the failure of log-concavity of multichain counts of matroids. The <em id="S1.p12.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">multichain counts</em> of a loopless matroid <span id="S1.p12.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">M</span> are defined by the function <math id="S1.p12.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Z}_{\geqslant 0}\to\mathbb{Z}_{\geqslant 0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>⩾</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>⩾</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Z}_{\geqslant 0}\to\mathbb{Z}_{\geqslant 0}</annotation></semantics></math></p>
<table id="S1.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="Z_{\textrm{M}}(k)\coloneqq\#\{\widehat{0}=F_{0}\leqslant F_{1}\leqslant\cdots\leqslant F_{k}=\widehat{1}\in\mathcal{L}(\textrm{M})\}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mtext>M</mtext></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≔</mo><mi>#</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mover accent="true"><mn>0</mn><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>⩽</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.1389em">⩽</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.1389em">⋯</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em">⩽</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mover accent="true"><mn>1</mn><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext>M</mtext><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{\textrm{M}}(k)\coloneqq\#\{\widehat{0}=F_{0}\leqslant F_{1}\leqslant\cdots\leqslant F_{k}=\widehat{1}\in\mathcal{L}(\textrm{M})\},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p12.2" class="ltx_p">which counts the multichains of <math id="S1.p12.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}(\textrm{M})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext>M</mtext><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}(\textrm{M})</annotation></semantics></math> by size. See <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">42</a>, Section 3.12]</cite>. It turns out that, the failure of log-concavity of the multichain counts, induces failures of log-concavity of the polynomial invariants in question; this idea appeared implicitly in the recent work of Luis Ferroni <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>]</cite> and in an analogous setting in Ehrhart theory <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>, Conjecture 1.2]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmintrothm1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_introthm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmintrothm1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem A</span></span></h6>
<div id="Thmintrothm1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmintrothm1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmintrothm1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <span id="Thmintrothm1.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_upright">M</span> be a loopless matroid. If there is an integer <math id="Thmintrothm1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j\geqslant 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>⩾</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j\geqslant 2</annotation></semantics></math>
such that</span></p>
<table id="S1.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{\textrm{M}}(j)^{2}&lt;Z_{\textrm{M}}(j-1)Z_{\textrm{M}}(j+1)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mtext>M</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mtext>M</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mtext>M</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{\textrm{M}}(j)^{2}&lt;Z_{\textrm{M}}(j-1)Z_{\textrm{M}}(j+1),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmintrothm1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmintrothm1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">then for every sufficiently large integer <math id="Thmintrothm1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>, the polynomials</span></p>
<table id="S1.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(\textrm{M}^{\oplus n};t),\qquad h(\textrm{M}^{\oplus n};t),\qquad\underline{\textrm{H}}(\textrm{M}^{\oplus n};t),\qquad\textrm{H}(\textrm{M}^{\oplus n};t)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mtext>M</mtext><mrow><mo rspace="0em">⊕</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mtext>M</mtext><mrow><mo rspace="0em">⊕</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mtext>H</mtext><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mtext>M</mtext><mrow><mo rspace="0em">⊕</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mtext>H</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mtext>M</mtext><mrow><mo rspace="0em">⊕</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(\textrm{M}^{\oplus n};t),\qquad h(\textrm{M}^{\oplus n};t),\qquad\underline{\textrm{H}}(\textrm{M}^{\oplus n};t),\qquad\textrm{H}(\textrm{M}^{\oplus n};t)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmintrothm1.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmintrothm1.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">are not log-concave, and hence not real-rooted.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.p13" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p13.1" class="ltx_p">Our original mechanism for failure of log-concavity of the polynomials in question uses the failure of log-concavity of flag <math id="S1.p13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math>-vectors and flag <math id="S1.p13.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>-vectors similar to the statement above, instead of failures of multichain counts. The statement of the above theorem is then suggested by Ferroni, after we communicated the main discovery below, which simplifies our proofs.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p14" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p14.1" class="ltx_p">We construct four families of matroids from finite geometries whose
multichain counts satisfy the hypothesis of <a href="#Thmintrothm1" title="Theorem A ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Chow and chain polynomials of matroids are not log-concave" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a>. All four families use a geometric technique
which we call <em id="S1.p14.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">hyperplane coalescence</em>. In a nutshell, hyperplane coalescence merges selected collections of hyperplanes into larger hyperplanes while preserving every flat of lower rank, enabling us to alter chain counts without changing the lower-rank geometry of the matroid. Hyperplane coalescence generalizes the classical operation of tightening a free basis, which is the inverse of <em id="S1.p14.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">circuit-hyperplane relaxation</em>, and can be viewed as a matroid operation that naturally arises in the Crapo–Knuth theory of detruncations <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>, <a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>]</cite>; see also <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">38</a>, Section 3]</cite>.<span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
            
            
            
            
            
            
            
          Throughout this paper, we use <em id="footnote1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">detruncation</em> for what is classically called a nontrivial <em id="footnote1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">erection</em>: Given a matroid <span id="footnote1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">M</span>, a <em id="footnote1.5" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">detruncation</em> of <span id="footnote1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">M</span> is a rank-<math id="footnote1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> matroid <span id="footnote1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">N</span> whose rank-<math id="footnote1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(r-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(r-1)</annotation></semantics></math> truncation is <span id="footnote1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">M</span>. Our terminology emphasizes its relation to truncation.</span></span></span></p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p15" class="ltx_para">
<ol id="S1.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The <em id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">affine Klein family</em> <math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textrm{M}_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext>M</mtext><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textrm{M}_{q}</annotation></semantics></math> of rank <math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math>, for odd
prime powers <math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>, constructed from affine geometry using
spreads obtained from the Klein correspondence
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The <em id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">affine packing family</em> <math id="S1.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textrm{M}^{\mathrm{pk}}_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mtext>M</mtext><mi>q</mi><mi>pk</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textrm{M}^{\mathrm{pk}}_{q}</annotation></semantics></math> of rank <math id="S1.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math>,
for prime powers <math id="S1.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>, constructed from affine geometry using
Denniston’s packings of projective lines into spreads
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>, <a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(3)</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The <em id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Gabidulin family</em> <math id="S1.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textrm{G}_{k,q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext>G</mtext><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textrm{G}_{k,q}</annotation></semantics></math> of rank <math id="S1.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>,
for <math id="S1.I1.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\geqslant 4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>⩾</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\geqslant 4</annotation></semantics></math> and prime powers <math id="S1.I1.i3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>, constructed from
projective geometry using rank-metric codes
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>, <a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(4)</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The <em id="S1.I1.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">projective family</em> <math id="S1.I1.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textrm{C}(q,N)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>C</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textrm{C}(q,N)</annotation></semantics></math> of rank <math id="S1.I1.i4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>7</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">7</annotation></semantics></math>,
for prime powers <math id="S1.I1.i4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> and integers <math id="S1.I1.i4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4\leqslant N\leqslant q^{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>⩽</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>⩽</mo><msup><mi>q</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4\leqslant N\leqslant q^{3}</annotation></semantics></math>,
constructed from projective planes and auxiliary four-element
sets using rank-distance bounds
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>, <a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
<div id="Thmintrothm2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_introthm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmintrothm2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem B</span></span></h6>
<div id="Thmintrothm2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmintrothm2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmintrothm2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The following equivalences hold:</span></p>
<ol id="S1.I2" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S1.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S1.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I2.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For every odd prime power </span><math id="S1.I2.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I2.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">,</span></p>
<table id="S1.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="Z_{\textrm{M}_{q}}(3)^{2}&lt;Z_{\textrm{M}_{q}}(2)Z_{\textrm{M}_{q}}(4)\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad q\geqslant 97." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><msub><mtext>M</mtext><mi>q</mi></msub></msub><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><msub><mtext>M</mtext><mi>q</mi></msub></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><msub><mtext>M</mtext><mi>q</mi></msub></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟺</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>⩾</mo><mn>97</mn><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{\textrm{M}_{q}}(3)^{2}&lt;Z_{\textrm{M}_{q}}(2)Z_{\textrm{M}_{q}}(4)\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad q\geqslant 97.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S1.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I2.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For every prime power </span><math id="S1.I2.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I2.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">,</span></p>
<table id="S1.Ex8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="Z_{\textrm{M}^{\mathrm{pk}}_{q}}(3)^{2}&lt;Z_{\textrm{M}^{\mathrm{pk}}_{q}}(2)Z_{\textrm{M}^{\mathrm{pk}}_{q}}(4)\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad q\geqslant 81." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><msubsup><mtext>M</mtext><mi>q</mi><mi>pk</mi></msubsup></msub><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><msubsup><mtext>M</mtext><mi>q</mi><mi>pk</mi></msubsup></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><msubsup><mtext>M</mtext><mi>q</mi><mi>pk</mi></msubsup></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟺</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>⩾</mo><mn>81</mn><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{\textrm{M}^{\mathrm{pk}}_{q}}(3)^{2}&lt;Z_{\textrm{M}^{\mathrm{pk}}_{q}}(2)Z_{\textrm{M}^{\mathrm{pk}}_{q}}(4)\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad q\geqslant 81.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(3)</span> 
<div id="S1.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I2.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For every prime power </span><math id="S1.I2.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I2.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">,</span></p>
<table id="S1.Ex9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="Z_{\textrm{G}_{4,q}}(3)^{2}&lt;Z_{\textrm{G}_{4,q}}(2)Z_{\textrm{G}_{4,q}}(4)\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad q\geqslant 81." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><msub><mtext>G</mtext><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub></msub><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><msub><mtext>G</mtext><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><msub><mtext>G</mtext><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟺</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>⩾</mo><mn>81</mn><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{\textrm{G}_{4,q}}(3)^{2}&lt;Z_{\textrm{G}_{4,q}}(2)Z_{\textrm{G}_{4,q}}(4)\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad q\geqslant 81.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I2.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(4)</span> 
<div id="S1.I2.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I2.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I2.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For every prime power </span><math id="S1.I2.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I2.i4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">,</span></p>
<table id="S1.Ex10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="Z_{\textrm{C}(q,q^{3})}(3)^{2}&lt;Z_{\textrm{C}(q,q^{3})}(2)Z_{\textrm{C}(q,q^{3})}(4)\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad q\geqslant 149." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mtext>C</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>q</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mtext>C</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>q</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mtext>C</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>q</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟺</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>⩾</mo><mn>149</mn><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{\textrm{C}(q,q^{3})}(3)^{2}&lt;Z_{\textrm{C}(q,q^{3})}(2)Z_{\textrm{C}(q,q^{3})}(4)\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad q\geqslant 149.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div></li>
</ol>
<p id="Thmintrothm2.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmintrothm2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For each matroid <span id="Thmintrothm2.p1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_upright">M</span> satisfying one of these inequalities and every
sufficiently large integer <math id="Thmintrothm2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>, the polynomials</span></p>
<table id="S1.Ex11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(\textrm{M}^{\oplus n};t),\qquad h(\textrm{M}^{\oplus n};t),\qquad\underline{\textrm{H}}(\textrm{M}^{\oplus n};t),\qquad\textrm{H}(\textrm{M}^{\oplus n};t)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mtext>M</mtext><mrow><mo rspace="0em">⊕</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mtext>M</mtext><mrow><mo rspace="0em">⊕</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mtext>H</mtext><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mtext>M</mtext><mrow><mo rspace="0em">⊕</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mtext>H</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mtext>M</mtext><mrow><mo rspace="0em">⊕</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(\textrm{M}^{\oplus n};t),\qquad h(\textrm{M}^{\oplus n};t),\qquad\underline{\textrm{H}}(\textrm{M}^{\oplus n};t),\qquad\textrm{H}(\textrm{M}^{\oplus n};t)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmintrothm2.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmintrothm2.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">are neither log-concave nor real-rooted.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmthm4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_example">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmthm4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Example 1.4</span></span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmthm4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmthm4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The matroid <math id="S1.Thmthm4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textrm{M}_{97}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext>M</mtext><mn>97</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textrm{M}_{97}</annotation></semantics></math> is on <math id="S1.Thmthm4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="88\,533\,986" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>88 533 986</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">88\,533\,986</annotation></semantics></math> elements of rank <math id="S1.Thmthm4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math>, with</p>
<table id="S1.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S1.Ex12"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.Ex12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle Z_{\textrm{M}_{97}}(2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><msub><mtext>M</mtext><mn>97</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle Z_{\textrm{M}_{97}}(2)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.Ex12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=981\,796\,552\,261\,343," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mn>981 796 552 261 343</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=981\,796\,552\,261\,343,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S1.Ex13"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.Ex13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle Z_{\textrm{M}_{97}}(3)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><msub><mtext>M</mtext><mn>97</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle Z_{\textrm{M}_{97}}(3)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.Ex13.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=28\,747\,469\,795\,558\,806," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mn>28 747 469 795 558 806</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=28\,747\,469\,795\,558\,806,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S1.Ex14"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.Ex14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle Z_{\textrm{M}_{97}}(4)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><msub><mtext>M</mtext><mn>97</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle Z_{\textrm{M}_{97}}(4)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.Ex14.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=898\,967\,215\,589\,248\,050." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mn>898 967 215 589 248 050</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=898\,967\,215\,589\,248\,050.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.Thmthm4.p1.2" class="ltx_p">These numbers of multichains satisfy</p>
<table id="S1.Ex15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{Z_{\textrm{M}_{97}}(3)^{2}}{Z_{\textrm{M}_{97}}(2)Z_{\textrm{M}_{97}}(4)}\approx 0.93634&lt;1." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><msub><mtext>M</mtext><mn>97</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><msub><mtext>M</mtext><mn>97</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><msub><mtext>M</mtext><mn>97</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo>≈</mo><mn>0.93634</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{Z_{\textrm{M}_{97}}(3)^{2}}{Z_{\textrm{M}_{97}}(2)Z_{\textrm{M}_{97}}(4)}\approx 0.93634&lt;1.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.Thmthm4.p1.3" class="ltx_p">The chain polynomial of the full lattice <math id="S1.Thmthm4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}(\textrm{M}_{97})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mtext>M</mtext><mn>97</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}(\textrm{M}_{97})</annotation></semantics></math> is</p>
<table id="S1.EGx2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S1.Ex16"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.Ex16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle f(\mathcal{L}(\textrm{M}_{97});t)=\;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mtext>M</mtext><mn>97</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn> 1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle f(\mathcal{L}(\textrm{M}_{97});t)=\;1</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.Ex16.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle+981\,796\,552\,261\,343t+27\,765\,673\,243\,297\,463t^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>981 796 552 261 343</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>27 765 673 243 297 463</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle+981\,796\,552\,261\,343t+27\,765\,673\,243\,297\,463t^{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S1.Ex17"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.Ex17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle+842\,454\,072\,550\,391\,781t^{3}+1\,605\,538\,311\,579\,936\,540t^{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>842 454 072 550 391 781</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>1 605 538 311 579 936 540</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>4</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle+842\,454\,072\,550\,391\,781t^{3}+1\,605\,538\,311\,579\,936\,540t^{4}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S1.Ex18"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.Ex18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle+789\,868\,115\,720\,580\,880t^{5}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>789 868 115 720 580 880</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>5</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle+789\,868\,115\,720\,580\,880t^{5},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.Thmthm4.p1.4" class="ltx_p">with the coefficients <math id="S1.Thmthm4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{1},a_{2},a_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{1},a_{2},a_{3}</annotation></semantics></math> of the terms <math id="S1.Thmthm4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t,t^{2},t^{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t,t^{2},t^{3}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfying</p>
<table id="S1.Ex19" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{2}^{2}-a_{1}a_{3}=-56\,185\,893\,214\,937\,313\,548\,622\,648\,985\,514&lt;0." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>56 185 893 214 937 313 548 622 648 985 514</mn></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{2}^{2}-a_{1}a_{3}=-56\,185\,893\,214\,937\,313\,548\,622\,648\,985\,514&lt;0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.Thmthm4.p1.5" class="ltx_p">The matroid <math id="S1.Thmthm4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textrm{N}=\textrm{M}_{97}\oplus\textrm{M}_{97}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>N</mtext><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mtext>M</mtext><mn>97</mn></msub><mo>⊕</mo><msub><mtext>M</mtext><mn>97</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textrm{N}=\textrm{M}_{97}\oplus\textrm{M}_{97}</annotation></semantics></math> has rank <math id="S1.Thmthm4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>8</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">8</annotation></semantics></math>
on <math id="S1.Thmthm4.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="177\,067\,972" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>177 067 972</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">177\,067\,972</annotation></semantics></math> elements with Chow polynomial</p>
<table id="S1.EGx3" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S1.Ex20"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.Ex20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\underline{\textrm{H}}(\textrm{N};t)={}1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mtext>H</mtext><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext>N</mtext><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\underline{\textrm{H}}(\textrm{N};t)={}1</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.Ex20.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle+963\,924\,470\,032\,260\,016\,646\,795\,095\,676t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>963 924 470 032 260 016 646 795 095 676</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle+963\,924\,470\,032\,260\,016\,646\,795\,095\,676t</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S1.Ex21"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.Ex21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle+772\,700\,986\,375\,783\,620\,699\,737\,483\,420\,137t^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>772 700 986 375 783 620 699 737 483 420 137</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle+772\,700\,986\,375\,783\,620\,699\,737\,483\,420\,137t^{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S1.Ex22"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.Ex22.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle+668\,902\,642\,263\,664\,767\,416\,856\,175\,849\,115\,238t^{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>668 902 642 263 664 767 416 856 175 849 115 238</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle+668\,902\,642\,263\,664\,767\,416\,856\,175\,849\,115\,238t^{3}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S1.Ex23"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.Ex23.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle+668\,902\,642\,263\,664\,767\,416\,856\,175\,849\,115\,238t^{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>668 902 642 263 664 767 416 856 175 849 115 238</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>4</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle+668\,902\,642\,263\,664\,767\,416\,856\,175\,849\,115\,238t^{4}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S1.Ex24"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.Ex24.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle+772\,700\,986\,375\,783\,620\,699\,737\,483\,420\,137t^{5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>772 700 986 375 783 620 699 737 483 420 137</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>5</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle+772\,700\,986\,375\,783\,620\,699\,737\,483\,420\,137t^{5}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S1.Ex25"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.Ex25.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle+963\,924\,470\,032\,260\,016\,646\,795\,095\,676t^{6}+t^{7}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>963 924 470 032 260 016 646 795 095 676</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>6</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>7</mn></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle+963\,924\,470\,032\,260\,016\,646\,795\,095\,676t^{6}+t^{7}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.Thmthm4.p1.6" class="ltx_p">The coefficients <math id="S1.Thmthm4.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{1},a_{2},a_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{1},a_{2},a_{3}</annotation></semantics></math> of the terms <math id="S1.Thmthm4.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t,t^{2},t^{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t,t^{2},t^{3}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfy</p>
<table id="S1.Ex26" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex26.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{a_{2}^{2}}{a_{1}a_{3}}\approx 0.926013&lt;1." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mfrac><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>≈</mo><mn>0.926013</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{a_{2}^{2}}{a_{1}a_{3}}\approx 0.926013&lt;1.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.Thmthm4.p1.7" class="ltx_p">Hence, its Chow polynomial is not log-concave, and hence not real-rooted.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.p16" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p16.1" class="ltx_p">The projective family in rank <math id="S1.p16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>7</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">7</annotation></semantics></math> serves as counterexamples to the log-concavity of Chow polynomials. For every prime power <math id="S1.p16.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q\geqslant 512" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⩾</mo><mn>512</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q\geqslant 512</annotation></semantics></math>, the matroid
<math id="S1.p16.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textrm{C}(q,q^{3})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>C</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>q</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textrm{C}(q,q^{3})</annotation></semantics></math> is simple and <math id="S1.p16.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math>-connected, and its Chow polynomial
is not log-concave, and hence not real-rooted.
More generally, the same conclusions hold for
<math id="S1.p16.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textrm{C}(q,\lfloor\rho\,q^{3}\rfloor)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>C</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>q</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌋</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textrm{C}(q,\lfloor\rho\,q^{3}\rfloor)</annotation></semantics></math> for each fixed
<math id="S1.p16.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0&lt;\rho\leqslant 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⩽</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0&lt;\rho\leqslant 1</annotation></semantics></math> and all sufficiently large prime powers <math id="S1.p16.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmthm5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_example">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmthm5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Example 1.5</span></span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmthm5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmthm5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The matroid <math id="S1.Thmthm5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textrm{C}(512,512^{3})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>C</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mn>512,512</mn><mn>3</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textrm{C}(512,512^{3})</annotation></semantics></math> of rank <math id="S1.Thmthm5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>7</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">7</annotation></semantics></math> is simple and <math id="S1.Thmthm5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math>-connected,
on</p>
<table id="S1.Ex27" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex27.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1+512+512^{2}+2\cdot 512^{3}+512^{4}+512^{5}=35\,253\,360\,263\,681" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>512</mn><mo>+</mo><msup><mn>512</mn><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msup><mn>512</mn><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><msup><mn>512</mn><mn>4</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mn>512</mn><mn>5</mn></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>35 253 360 263 681</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1+512+512^{2}+2\cdot 512^{3}+512^{4}+512^{5}=35\,253\,360\,263\,681</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.Thmthm5.p1.2" class="ltx_p">elements. Its Chow polynomial is</p>
<table id="S1.EGx4" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S1.Ex28"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.Ex28.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\underline{\textrm{H}}(\textrm{C};t)={}1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mtext>H</mtext><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext>C</mtext><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\underline{\textrm{H}}(\textrm{C};t)={}1</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.Ex28.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle+12796287528694082360694375709107783381771733983561734t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>12796287528694082360694375709107783381771733983561734</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle+12796287528694082360694375709107783381771733983561734t</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S1.Ex29"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.Ex29.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle+2260867910885166349838189869115390690568965414423633935t^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2260867910885166349838189869115390690568965414423633935</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle+2260867910885166349838189869115390690568965414423633935t^{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S1.Ex30"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.Ex30.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle+402548512894232793390837832528792797279036205852996415508t^{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>402548512894232793390837832528792797279036205852996415508</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle+402548512894232793390837832528792797279036205852996415508t^{3}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S1.Ex31"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.Ex31.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle+2260867910885166349838189869115390690568965414423633935t^{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2260867910885166349838189869115390690568965414423633935</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>4</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle+2260867910885166349838189869115390690568965414423633935t^{4}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S1.Ex32"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.Ex32.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle+12796287528694082360694375709107783381771733983561734t^{5}+t^{6}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>12796287528694082360694375709107783381771733983561734</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>5</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>6</mn></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle+12796287528694082360694375709107783381771733983561734t^{5}+t^{6}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.Thmthm5.p1.3" class="ltx_p">The coefficients <math id="S1.Thmthm5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{1},a_{2},a_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{1},a_{2},a_{3}</annotation></semantics></math> of the terms <math id="S1.Thmthm5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t,t^{2},t^{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t,t^{2},t^{3}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfy</p>
<table id="S1.Ex33" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex33.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{a_{2}^{2}}{a_{1}a_{3}}\approx 0.992312&lt;1." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mfrac><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>≈</mo><mn>0.992312</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{a_{2}^{2}}{a_{1}a_{3}}\approx 0.992312&lt;1.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.Thmthm5.p1.4" class="ltx_p">Thus <math id="S1.Thmthm5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{\textrm{H}}(\textrm{C};t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mtext>H</mtext><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext>C</mtext><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{\textrm{H}}(\textrm{C};t)</annotation></semantics></math> is not log-concave, and hence not real-rooted.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmthm6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmthm6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 1.6</span></span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmthm6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmthm6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">These families achieve the smallest possible ranks for failures
of both log-concavity and real-rootedness of the chain polynomial
and the Chow polynomial. Brändén and Leite proved that the
chain polynomial and the chain polynomial of every matroid of rank at most <math id="S1.Thmthm6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math>
are real-rooted <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>, Theorem 6.1]</cite>. For Chow polynomials, Coron, Ferroni and Li proved real-rootedness for every matroid of rank at most <math id="S1.Thmthm6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>6</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">6</annotation></semantics></math>
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>, Corollary 8.5]</cite>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.p17" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p17.1" class="ltx_p">The nontrivial direct sums in <a href="#Thmintrothm1" title="Theorem A ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Chow and chain polynomials of matroids are not log-concave" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a> are
disconnected and have large ground sets.
Below, we obtain rank-<math id="S1.p17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>7</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">7</annotation></semantics></math> counterexamples to the log-concavity of Chow
polynomials directly from the rank-<math id="S1.p17.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math> counterexamples to the log-concavity
of the chain polynomials, by truncating the direct sum of two matroids in
the latter family or taking their parallel connections.
These constructions give counterexamples that are minimal in their ranks
and have much smaller ground sets. These examples also distinguish failure
of real-rootedness from failure of log-concavity. For example,
the Chow polynomial of <math id="S1.p17.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Tr}^{7}(\textrm{M}_{q}\oplus\textrm{M}_{q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>Tr</mi><mn>7</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mtext>M</mtext><mi>q</mi></msub><mo>⊕</mo><msub><mtext>M</mtext><mi>q</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Tr}^{7}(\textrm{M}_{q}\oplus\textrm{M}_{q})</annotation></semantics></math> is log-concave but not real-rooted for odd prime powers <math id="S1.p17.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="47\leqslant q\leqslant 89" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>47</mn><mo>⩽</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>⩽</mo><mn>89</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">47\leqslant q\leqslant 89</annotation></semantics></math>, and it becomes not log-concave for <math id="S1.p17.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q\geqslant 97" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⩾</mo><mn>97</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q\geqslant 97</annotation></semantics></math>.
Adding one coloop before truncation gives <math id="S1.p17.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math>-connected examples with the
same thresholds (<a href="#Thmintrothm3" title="Theorem C ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Chow and chain polynomials of matroids are not log-concave" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmintrothm3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_introthm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmintrothm3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem C</span></span></h6>
<div id="Thmintrothm3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmintrothm3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmintrothm3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The matroids</span></p>
<table id="S1.Ex34" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex34.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Tr}^{7}(\textrm{M}_{q}\oplus\textrm{M}_{q}\oplus\textrm{U}_{1,1}),\qquad\mathrm{Tr}^{7}(\textrm{G}_{4,q}\oplus\textrm{G}_{4,q}\oplus\textrm{U}_{1,1})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>Tr</mi><mn>7</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mtext>M</mtext><mi>q</mi></msub><mo>⊕</mo><msub><mtext>M</mtext><mi>q</mi></msub><mo>⊕</mo><msub><mtext>U</mtext><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>Tr</mi><mn>7</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mtext>G</mtext><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><mo>⊕</mo><msub><mtext>G</mtext><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><mo>⊕</mo><msub><mtext>U</mtext><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Tr}^{7}(\textrm{M}_{q}\oplus\textrm{M}_{q}\oplus\textrm{U}_{1,1}),\qquad\mathrm{Tr}^{7}(\textrm{G}_{4,q}\oplus\textrm{G}_{4,q}\oplus\textrm{U}_{1,1})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmintrothm3.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmintrothm3.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">are simple, <math id="Thmintrothm3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math>-connected and of rank <math id="Thmintrothm3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>7</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">7</annotation></semantics></math>.
The following hold:</span></p>
<ol id="S1.I3" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S1.I3.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S1.I3.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I3.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I3.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For every odd prime power </span><math id="S1.I3.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I3.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">,
</span><math id="S1.I3.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{\textrm{H}}(\mathrm{Tr}^{7}(\textrm{M}_{q}\oplus\textrm{M}_{q}\oplus\textrm{U}_{1,1});t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mtext>H</mtext><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>Tr</mi><mn>7</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mtext>M</mtext><mi>q</mi></msub><mo>⊕</mo><msub><mtext>M</mtext><mi>q</mi></msub><mo>⊕</mo><msub><mtext>U</mtext><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{\textrm{H}}(\mathrm{Tr}^{7}(\textrm{M}_{q}\oplus\textrm{M}_{q}\oplus\textrm{U}_{1,1});t)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I3.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">
is not real-rooted exactly when </span><math id="S1.I3.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q\geqslant 47" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⩾</mo><mn>47</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q\geqslant 47</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I3.i1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, and is not
log-concave exactly when </span><math id="S1.I3.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q\geqslant 97" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⩾</mo><mn>97</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q\geqslant 97</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I3.i1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I3.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S1.I3.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I3.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I3.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For every prime power </span><math id="S1.I3.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I3.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">,
</span><math id="S1.I3.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{\textrm{H}}(\mathrm{Tr}^{7}(\textrm{G}_{4,q}\oplus\textrm{G}_{4,q}\oplus\textrm{U}_{1,1});t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mtext>H</mtext><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>Tr</mi><mn>7</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mtext>G</mtext><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><mo>⊕</mo><msub><mtext>G</mtext><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><mo>⊕</mo><msub><mtext>U</mtext><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{\textrm{H}}(\mathrm{Tr}^{7}(\textrm{G}_{4,q}\oplus\textrm{G}_{4,q}\oplus\textrm{U}_{1,1});t)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I3.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">
is not real-rooted exactly when </span><math id="S1.I3.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q\geqslant 37" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⩾</mo><mn>37</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q\geqslant 37</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I3.i2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, and is not
log-concave exactly when </span><math id="S1.I3.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q\geqslant 81" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⩾</mo><mn>81</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q\geqslant 81</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I3.i2.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmintrothm4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_introcor">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmintrothm4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary D</span></span></h6>
<div id="Thmintrothm4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmintrothm4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><a href="#S1.Thmthm1" title="Conjecture 1.1 ([, Conjecture 4.1.3] [, Conjecture 8.18]) ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Chow and chain polynomials of matroids are not log-concave" class="ltx_ref ltx_font_italic"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Conjecture</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a><span id="Thmintrothm4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, <a href="#S1.Thmthm2" title="Conjecture 1.2 (Huh–Stevens) ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Chow and chain polynomials of matroids are not log-concave" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Conjecture</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.2</span></a> and <a href="#S1.Thmthm3" title="Conjecture 1.3 (Athanasiadis–Kalampogia-Evangelinou) ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Chow and chain polynomials of matroids are not log-concave" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Conjecture</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.3</span></a> are false.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S1.p18" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p18.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.p18.1.1" class="ltx_text">The inequalities for the numbers of multichains in <a href="#Thmintrothm2" title="Theorem B ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Chow and chain polynomials of matroids are not log-concave" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>, together
with <a href="#Thmintrothm1" title="Theorem A ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Chow and chain polynomials of matroids are not log-concave" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a>, imply non-log-concavity of the chain, Chow,
and augmented Chow polynomials. By Newton’s inequalities, the three
real-rootedness conjectures are false.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.p19" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p19.1" class="ltx_p">The key mechanism which allows us to transfer failures of log-concavity of multichain counts to other polynomial invariants, is given by <a href="#Thmintrothm1" title="Theorem A ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Chow and chain polynomials of matroids are not log-concave" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a>. We show that a similar mechanism also applies to the study of the so-called <em id="S1.p19.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">graph associahedra</em> and their <math id="S1.p19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math>-polynomials.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p20" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p20.1" class="ltx_p">Recall that for a connected graph <math id="S1.p20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>, its associated graph associahedron <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite> is the simple
polytope whose dual simplicial complex is the simplicial complex of tubings, which are compatible
families of nonempty proper connected vertex sets. Its <math id="S1.p20.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math>-polynomial
is denoted by <math id="S1.p20.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{G}(t)=\sum_{j}h_{j}(G)t^{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>j</mi></msub><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mi>j</mi></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{G}(t)=\sum_{j}h_{j}(G)t^{j}</annotation></semantics></math>. We refer to <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">41</a>]</cite> for more details of graph associahedra and its connections to Boolean matroids with building sets. For several prominent families of graphs, these polynomials and their positivity properties have been studied under different names. For instance, the <math id="S1.p20.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math>-polynomials of complete graphs coincide with the Eulerian polynomials, for path graphs we get Narayana polynomials, for cycle graphs we obtain type <math id="S1.p20.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> Narayana polynomials and finally, the <math id="S1.p20.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math>-polynomials of star graphs correspond to binomial-Eulerian polynomials. All these polynomials are known to be real-rooted using various techniques with wide range of connections to analysis, partial differential equations, topology and geometry (see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>, pp. 829–830]</cite> for Eulerian polynomials, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>, Corollary 7.2]</cite> for type A and type B Narayana polynomials, and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>, Theorem 3.1]</cite> for binomial-Eulerian polynomials). However, we show in Section <a href="#S5" title="5. ℎ-polynomials of graph associahedra are not log-concave ‣ Chow and chain polynomials of matroids are not log-concave" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> that <math id="S1.p20.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math>-polynomials of graph associahedra are not necessarily log-concave, and hence not real-rooted. <span id="footnote2" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup>
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">2</span>
            
            
            
            
            
            
            
          The question of whether <math id="footnote2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math>-polynomials of graph associahedra was studied and raised to the fourth author SL by Andrew Sack.</span></span></span></p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p21" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p21.1" class="ltx_p">For a connected rooted graph <math id="S1.p21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(G,v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(G,v)</annotation></semantics></math>, let
<math id="S1.p21.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}_{v}(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}_{v}(G)</annotation></semantics></math> be the poset of connected vertex sets containing <math id="S1.p21.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math>,
ordered by inclusion. Let
<math id="S1.p21.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{G}(j)=Z_{\mathcal{F}_{v}(G)}(j)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{G}(j)=Z_{\mathcal{F}_{v}(G)}(j)</annotation></semantics></math> count multichains
<math id="S1.p21.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{v\}=S_{0}\subseteq\cdots\subseteq S_{j}=V(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.1389em">⊆</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.1389em">⋯</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em">⊆</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{v\}=S_{0}\subseteq\cdots\subseteq S_{j}=V(G)</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S1.p21.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}_{v}(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}_{v}(G)</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S1.p21.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> steps, and we write <math id="S1.p21.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{s}(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{s}(G)</annotation></semantics></math> (for “wedging”) for the
graph obtained by identifying the roots of <math id="S1.p21.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> disjoint copies of <math id="S1.p21.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>.
This construction will play the same role as direct sums in the above matroid setting. Indeed, we have the similar identities</p>
<table id="S1.Ex35" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex35.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}(\textrm{M}^{\oplus s})\cong\mathcal{L}(\textrm{M})^{s},\qquad\mathcal{F}_{v}(W_{s}(G))\cong\mathcal{F}_{v}(G)^{s}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mtext>M</mtext><mrow><mo rspace="0em">⊕</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext>M</mtext><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>s</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>s</mi></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}(\textrm{M}^{\oplus s})\cong\mathcal{L}(\textrm{M})^{s},\qquad\mathcal{F}_{v}(W_{s}(G))\cong\mathcal{F}_{v}(G)^{s}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p21.2" class="ltx_p"><a href="#S2.Thmthm13" title="Theorem 2.13 (coefficient estimate) ‣ 2.5.4. An explicit amplification certificate ‣ 2.5. Face enumerations of graph associahedra ‣ 2. Polynomial invariants and multichain counts ‣ Chow and chain polynomials of matroids are not log-concave" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.13</span></a> and <a href="#S2.Thmthm14" title="Theorem 2.14 (graph zeta amplification) ‣ 2.5.4. An explicit amplification certificate ‣ 2.5. Face enumerations of graph associahedra ‣ 2. Polynomial invariants and multichain counts ‣ Chow and chain polynomials of matroids are not log-concave" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.14</span></a> imply an analogue of <a href="#Thmintrothm1" title="Theorem A ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Chow and chain polynomials of matroids are not log-concave" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a> for graphs: if a graph <math id="S1.p21.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> has a non log-concave sequence of multichain counts, then the polynomials <math id="S1.p21.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{W_{s}(G)}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{W_{s}(G)}(t)</annotation></semantics></math> are not log-concave for any sufficiently large integer <math id="S1.p21.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus, in both settings, a failure of log-concavity of the multichain counts produces a failure of log-concavity of the <math id="S1.p21.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math>-polynomial, after sufficiently many repetitions. We end by presenting a graph whose multichain counts are not log-concave.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmintrothm5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_introthm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmintrothm5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem E</span></span><span id="Thmintrothm5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(<a href="#S5.Thmthm3" title="Theorem 5.3 (Computer-assisted witness) ‣ 5.4. A counterexample ‣ 5. ℎ-polynomials of graph associahedra are not log-concave ‣ Chow and chain polynomials of matroids are not log-concave" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.3</span></a>)</h6>
<div id="Thmintrothm5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmintrothm5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmintrothm5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">There is a connected rooted graph <math id="Thmintrothm5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="Thmintrothm5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1540" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1540</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1540</annotation></semantics></math> vertices such that</span></p>
<table id="S1.Ex36" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex36.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{G}(37)^{2}&lt;Z_{G}(36)Z_{G}(38)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>37</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>36</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>38</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{G}(37)^{2}&lt;Z_{G}(36)Z_{G}(38).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmintrothm5.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmintrothm5.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For every integer <math id="Thmintrothm5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s\geq 23" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>23</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s\geq 23</annotation></semantics></math>, the graph associahedron of <math id="Thmintrothm5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{s}(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{s}(G)</annotation></semantics></math>
has an <math id="Thmintrothm5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math>-polynomial satisfying</span></p>
<table id="S1.Ex37" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex37.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{36}(W_{s}(G))^{2}&lt;h_{35}(W_{s}(G))h_{37}(W_{s}(G))." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mn>36</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mn>35</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mn>37</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{36}(W_{s}(G))^{2}&lt;h_{35}(W_{s}(G))h_{37}(W_{s}(G)).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmintrothm5.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmintrothm5.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">In particular, this polynomial is not log-concave, and hence not real-rooted.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.p22" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p22.1" class="ltx_p">The graph <math id="S1.p22.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> consists of a long path connected to a long periodic ladder graph via sparse bridges. Morally speaking, the competing contributions of the path graph and the ladder graphs in the multichain counts produces a failure of log-concavity of multichain counts. We present this construction, and conduct computer assisted computations and follow an analogous proof strategy of failure of log-concavity to the matroid case in Section <a href="#S5" title="5. ℎ-polynomials of graph associahedra are not log-concave ‣ Chow and chain polynomials of matroids are not log-concave" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S1.SSx1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">AI disclosure</h3>

<div id="S1.SSx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SSx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">LLMs were substantially used in the searches of these counterexamples. In an upcoming update of this paper, we will release a detailed analysis on the search history from an AI-methodology point of view. A brief summary of the searches is as follows.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SSx1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SSx1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The third author CE found a non-constructive disproof of the real-rootedness conjecture for Chow polynomials using ChatGPT Astra on September 23, and communicated to the fourth author SL about the nature of the disproof.
Upon suggestions by SL, Matt Larson, and Lorenzo Vecchi, disproofs of the real-rootedness of augmented Chow and chain polynomials were obtained similarly on September 29.
These disproofs involved inexplicit matroids of rank at least <math id="S1.SSx1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2^{65}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>2</mn><mn>65</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2^{65}</annotation></semantics></math>, whose existence relies on probabilistic methods, with no claim to the failure of log-concavity.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SSx1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SSx1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Prompted by the lack of concrete and explicit counterexamples of low ranks, and the question of whether log-concavity can fail, on September 30, the second author BC found using ChatGPT Astra a counterexample to the log-concavity of <math id="S1.SSx1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math>-polynomials of graph associahedra (a special case of built matroids) of very high vertex count. On October 4, the first author GB, BC, and SL then orchestrated and launched an LLM-search to target specifically the failure of log-concavity for both the chain polynomials and the Chow polynomials. About <math id="S1.SSx1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5</annotation></semantics></math> hours and <math id="S1.SSx1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="12" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>12</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">12</annotation></semantics></math> hours later, SL found using Claude Opus 5.5 three explicit families of matroids as counterexamples to both the log-concavity of chain polynomials and Chow polynomials of lowest ranks possible, which are presented here; soon after, GB also found using ChatGPT Astra a different family of counterexamples to the log-concavity of Chow polynomials.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SSx1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SSx1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Generative LLMs were further used for drafting the reports for the initial findings, literature search, identifying common patterns among the examples such as hyperplane coalescence, and producing figures. The mathematical content was digested and reshaped from the LLM search reports, checked, and written by the authors.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SSx2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Acknowledgements</h3>

<div id="S1.SSx2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SSx2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We thank the organizers of the BIRS-Oaxaca Workshop on “Algebra and Geometry of Matroids” from September 27 to October 2, 2026 for the wonderful conference, where CE communicated to SL about his LLM-generated disproof of real-rootedness and sparked the efforts on disproving log-concavity. We also thank Luis Ferroni for suggesting the consideration of multichain counts in Theorem <a href="#Thmintrothm1" title="Theorem A ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Chow and chain polynomials of matroids are not log-concave" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a>, which simplifies our proofs, as well as Matt Larson and Lorenzo Vecchi for helpful conversations. The question about <math id="S1.SSx2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math>-polynomials of graph associahedra was raised to the fourth author SL by Andrew Sack, and so we thank him for his communication and interest. SL is supported by a Donald J. Lewis postdoctoral fellowship at the University of Michigan. CE is supported by US NSF DMS-2246518 and Sloan Fellowship and GB is supported by DNRF 42296.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SSx3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Organization</h3>

<div id="S1.SSx3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SSx3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><a href="#S2" title="2. Polynomial invariants and multichain counts ‣ Chow and chain polynomials of matroids are not log-concave" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> reviews the polynomial invariants and proves
<a href="#Thmintrothm1" title="Theorem A ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Chow and chain polynomials of matroids are not log-concave" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a>. <a href="#S3" title="3. Matroid operations: hyperplane coalescence, absorption ‣ Chow and chain polynomials of matroids are not log-concave" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> develops hyperplane
coalescence and absorption, and studies the changes of the multichain counts under coalescence.
<a href="#S4" title="4. Four families from finite geometries ‣ Chow and chain polynomials of matroids are not log-concave" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> constructs the four geometric families, proves
<a href="#Thmintrothm2" title="Theorem B ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Chow and chain polynomials of matroids are not log-concave" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a> in <a href="#S4.SS4" title="4.4. Multichain counts are not log-concave ‣ 4. Four families from finite geometries ‣ Chow and chain polynomials of matroids are not log-concave" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.4</span></a>, and derives
the <math id="S1.SSx3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math>-connected rank-<math id="S1.SSx3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>7</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">7</annotation></semantics></math> counterexamples of <a href="#Thmintrothm3" title="Theorem C ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Chow and chain polynomials of matroids are not log-concave" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a> in
<a href="#S4.SS5" title="4.5. Three-connected rank-7 counterexamples ‣ 4. Four families from finite geometries ‣ Chow and chain polynomials of matroids are not log-concave" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.5</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2. </span>Polynomial invariants and multichain counts</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we review basic properties of the relevant polynomial invariants of matroids, and prove Theorem <a href="#Thmintrothm1" title="Theorem A ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Chow and chain polynomials of matroids are not log-concave" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a>. We then develop the parallel amplification argument for graph associahedra under wedges. Throughout, <span id="S2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">M</span> is a loopless matroid of positive rank <math id="S2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the lattice of flats of <span id="S2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">M</span>, and <math id="S2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{L}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{L}}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the poset of nonempty proper flats of <span id="S2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">M</span>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1. </span>Chain and face enumeration polynomials</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{k-1}(\mathcal{L})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{k-1}(\mathcal{L})</annotation></semantics></math> be the number of faces of dimension <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k-1</annotation></semantics></math>
in the order complex of <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math>, with <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{-1}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{-1}=1</annotation></semantics></math> for the empty
face. The face polynomials of <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{L}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{L}}</annotation></semantics></math> are defined by</p>
<table id="S2.Ex38" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex38.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(\mathcal{L};t)=\sum_{k\geqslant 0}f_{k-1}(\mathcal{L})t^{k},\qquad f(\overline{\mathcal{L}};t)=\sum_{k=0}^{r-1}f_{k-1}(\overline{\mathcal{L}})t^{k}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>⩾</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></munder><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(\mathcal{L};t)=\sum_{k\geqslant 0}f_{k-1}(\mathcal{L})t^{k},\qquad f(\overline{\mathcal{L}};t)=\sum_{k=0}^{r-1}f_{k-1}(\overline{\mathcal{L}})t^{k}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">For a matroid <span id="S2.SS1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">M</span>, write <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(\textrm{M};t)=f(\overline{\mathcal{L}};t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext>M</mtext><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(\textrm{M};t)=f(\overline{\mathcal{L}};t)</annotation></semantics></math>. For <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S\subseteq[r-1]=\{1,\dots,r-1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S\subseteq[r-1]=\{1,\dots,r-1\}</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{\textrm{M}}(S)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mtext>M</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{\textrm{M}}(S)</annotation></semantics></math>
count the chains in <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{L}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{L}}</annotation></semantics></math> with rank set <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>. These numbers
form the <em id="S2.SS1.p1.2.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">flag <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>-vector</em>. The <em id="S2.SS1.p1.2.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">flag <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math>-vector</em> is defined by</p>
<table id="S2.Ex39" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex39.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{\textrm{M}}(S)=\sum_{T\subseteq S}(-1)^{|S\setminus T|}\alpha_{\textrm{M}}(T)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mtext>M</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>∖</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mtext>M</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{\textrm{M}}(S)=\sum_{T\subseteq S}(-1)^{|S\setminus T|}\alpha_{\textrm{M}}(T).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">In particular, <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{\textrm{M}}(i)=\alpha_{\textrm{M}}(i)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mtext>M</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mtext>M</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{\textrm{M}}(i)=\alpha_{\textrm{M}}(i)</annotation></semantics></math> is the number of rank-<math id="S2.SS1.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> flats, and
<math id="S2.SS1.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{\textrm{M}}(\varnothing)=\beta_{\textrm{M}}(\varnothing)=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mtext>M</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mtext>M</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{\textrm{M}}(\varnothing)=\beta_{\textrm{M}}(\varnothing)=1</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">The order complex of <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{L}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{L}}</annotation></semantics></math> has dimension <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r-2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r-2</annotation></semantics></math> and face
numbers</p>
<table id="S2.Ex40" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex40.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{k-1}(\overline{\mathcal{L}})=\sum_{\begin{subarray}{c}S\subseteq[r-1]\\
|S|=k\end{subarray}}\alpha_{\textrm{M}}(S)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></munder><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mtext>M</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{k-1}(\overline{\mathcal{L}})=\sum_{\begin{subarray}{c}S\subseteq[r-1]\\
|S|=k\end{subarray}}\alpha_{\textrm{M}}(S).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.3" class="ltx_p">The <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math>-polynomial of <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{L}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{L}}</annotation></semantics></math> is defined by</p>
<table id="S2.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(1)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h(\textrm{M};t)\coloneqq\sum_{k=0}^{r-1}f_{k-1}(\overline{\mathcal{L}})t^{k}(1-t)^{r-1-k}=\sum_{S\subseteq[r-1]}\beta_{\textrm{M}}(S)t^{|S|}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext>M</mtext><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">≔</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></munder><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mtext>M</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h(\textrm{M};t)\coloneqq\sum_{k=0}^{r-1}f_{k-1}(\overline{\mathcal{L}})t^{k}(1-t)^{r-1-k}=\sum_{S\subseteq[r-1]}\beta_{\textrm{M}}(S)t^{|S|}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.4" class="ltx_p">The flag <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math>-numbers are nonnegative, and
<math id="S2.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{\mathcal{L}}([r-1])=|\mu_{\mathcal{L}}(\widehat{0},\widehat{1})|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mn>0</mn><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mn>1</mn><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{\mathcal{L}}([r-1])=|\mu_{\mathcal{L}}(\widehat{0},\widehat{1})|</annotation></semantics></math>
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>. The chain and <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math>-polynomials are related by</p>
<table id="S2.E2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S2.E2X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_left">(2)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E2X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle f(\mathcal{L};t)=(1+t)^{2}f(\overline{\mathcal{L}};t),\qquad f(\overline{\mathcal{L}};t)=(1+t)^{r-1}h\left(\textrm{M};\frac{t}{1+t}\right)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mi>h</mi><mo>(</mo><mtext>M</mtext><mo>;</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mi>t</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle f(\mathcal{L};t)=(1+t)^{2}f(\overline{\mathcal{L}};t),\qquad f(\overline{\mathcal{L}};t)=(1+t)^{r-1}h\left(\textrm{M};\frac{t}{1+t}\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2. </span>Multichain counts</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We recall the background of the multichain counting polynomial introduced in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">42</a>, Section 3.12]</cite> in the setting of matroids. For a loopless matroid <span id="S2.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">M</span> of positive rank <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>, the
function <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{\textrm{M}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Z</mi><mtext>M</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{\textrm{M}}</annotation></semantics></math> counts multichains in its
lattice of flats:</p>
<table id="S2.Ex41" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex41.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="Z_{\textrm{M}}(m)\coloneqq\#\{\widehat{0}=F_{0}\leqslant F_{1}\leqslant\cdots\leqslant F_{m}=\widehat{1}\}\qquad(m\geqslant 0)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mtext>M</mtext></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≔</mo><mi>#</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mover accent="true"><mn>0</mn><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>⩽</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.1389em">⩽</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.1389em">⋯</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em">⩽</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mover accent="true"><mn>1</mn><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>⩾</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{\textrm{M}}(m)\coloneqq\#\{\widehat{0}=F_{0}\leqslant F_{1}\leqslant\cdots\leqslant F_{m}=\widehat{1}\}\qquad(m\geqslant 0).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">In particular, <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{\textrm{M}}(0)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mtext>M</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{\textrm{M}}(0)=0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{\textrm{M}}(1)=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mtext>M</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{\textrm{M}}(1)=1</annotation></semantics></math>.
For <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\geqslant 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>⩾</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\geqslant 2</annotation></semantics></math>, a multichain counted by <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{\textrm{M}}(m)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mtext>M</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{\textrm{M}}(m)</annotation></semantics></math>
is determined by its <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m-1</annotation></semantics></math> intermediate flats in <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math>, which may
include <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mn>0</mn><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{0}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mn>1</mn><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{1}</annotation></semantics></math>. If these entries contain <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> distinct
flats, they form a strict chain
counted by <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{k-1}(\mathcal{L})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{k-1}(\mathcal{L})</annotation></semantics></math>. Their positive multiplicities sum
to <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m-1</annotation></semantics></math>, giving <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\binom{m-2}{k-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\binom{m-2}{k-1}</annotation></semantics></math> choices. Therefore,</p>
<table id="S2.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(3)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{\textrm{M}}(m)=\sum_{k=1}^{r+1}f_{k-1}(\mathcal{L})\binom{m-2}{k-1}=\sum_{k=0}^{r-1}f_{k-1}(\overline{\mathcal{L}})\binom{m}{k+1}\qquad(m\geqslant 2)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mtext>M</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>⩾</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{\textrm{M}}(m)=\sum_{k=1}^{r+1}f_{k-1}(\mathcal{L})\binom{m-2}{k-1}=\sum_{k=0}^{r-1}f_{k-1}(\overline{\mathcal{L}})\binom{m}{k+1}\qquad(m\geqslant 2),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.3" class="ltx_p">with <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{\textrm{M}}(0)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mtext>M</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{\textrm{M}}(0)=0</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{\textrm{M}}(1)=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mtext>M</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{\textrm{M}}(1)=1</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{\textrm{M}}(m)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mtext>M</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{\textrm{M}}(m)</annotation></semantics></math> is
strictly increasing for integers <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\geqslant 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>⩾</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\geqslant 1</annotation></semantics></math>. In particular,</p>
<table id="S2.Ex42" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.Ex42X"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex42X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle f_{0}(\mathcal{L})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle f_{0}(\mathcal{L})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex42X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=Z_{\textrm{M}}(2)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mtext>M</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=Z_{\textrm{M}}(2),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S2.Ex42Xa"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex42Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle f_{1}(\mathcal{L})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle f_{1}(\mathcal{L})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex42Xa.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=Z_{\textrm{M}}(3)-Z_{\textrm{M}}(2)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mtext>M</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mtext>M</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=Z_{\textrm{M}}(3)-Z_{\textrm{M}}(2),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S2.Ex42Xb"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex42Xb.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle f_{2}(\mathcal{L})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle f_{2}(\mathcal{L})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex42Xb.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=Z_{\textrm{M}}(4)-2Z_{\textrm{M}}(3)+Z_{\textrm{M}}(2)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mtext>M</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mtext>M</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mtext>M</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=Z_{\textrm{M}}(4)-2Z_{\textrm{M}}(3)+Z_{\textrm{M}}(2).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmthm1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 2.1</span></span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmthm1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For any matroid <span id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_upright">M</span>,</span></p>
<table id="S2.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(4)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{1}(\mathcal{L})^{2}-f_{0}(\mathcal{L})f_{2}(\mathcal{L})=Z_{\textrm{M}}(3)^{2}-Z_{\textrm{M}}(2)Z_{\textrm{M}}(4)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mtext>M</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mtext>M</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mtext>M</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{1}(\mathcal{L})^{2}-f_{0}(\mathcal{L})f_{2}(\mathcal{L})=Z_{\textrm{M}}(3)^{2}-Z_{\textrm{M}}(2)Z_{\textrm{M}}(4).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Thus <math id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(\mathcal{L};t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(\mathcal{L};t)</annotation></semantics></math> fails log-concavity at
coefficient index <math id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> exactly when the values of the zeta polynomial fail
log-concavity at <math id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m=3</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">One advantage of the zeta polynomial is its behavior over direct sums: a multichain in a product lattice is a tuple of multichains in its factors. By definition of multichain counts, we have</p>
<table id="S2.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(5)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{\textrm{M}^{\oplus n}}(m)=Z_{\textrm{M}}(m)^{n}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><msup><mtext>M</mtext><mrow><mo rspace="0em">⊕</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mtext>M</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{\textrm{M}^{\oplus n}}(m)=Z_{\textrm{M}}(m)^{n}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmthm2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 2.2</span></span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmthm2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmthm2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If for <math id="S2.Thmthm2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j\geqslant 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>⩾</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j\geqslant 2</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex43" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex43.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{\textrm{M}}(j)^{2}&lt;Z_{\textrm{M}}(j-1)Z_{\textrm{M}}(j+1)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mtext>M</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mtext>M</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mtext>M</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{\textrm{M}}(j)^{2}&lt;Z_{\textrm{M}}(j-1)Z_{\textrm{M}}(j+1)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.Thmthm2.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmthm2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">then the same inequality holds for <math id="S2.Thmthm2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textrm{M}^{\oplus n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mtext>M</mtext><mrow><mo rspace="0em">⊕</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textrm{M}^{\oplus n}</annotation></semantics></math> for every
<math id="S2.Thmthm2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geqslant 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>⩾</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geqslant 1</annotation></semantics></math>. Furthermore, if <math id="S2.Thmthm2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j=3</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S2.Thmthm2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(\mathcal{L}(\textrm{M}^{\oplus n});t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mtext>M</mtext><mrow><mo rspace="0em">⊕</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(\mathcal{L}(\textrm{M}^{\oplus n});t)</annotation></semantics></math>
fails to be log-concave at coefficient index <math id="S2.Thmthm2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S2.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">The first statement follows from (<a href="#S2.E5" title="Equation 5 ‣ 2.2. Multichain counts ‣ 2. Polynomial invariants and multichain counts ‣ Chow and chain polynomials of matroids are not log-concave" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>):</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex44" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex44.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{Z_{\textrm{M}^{\oplus n}}(j)^{2}}{Z_{\textrm{M}^{\oplus n}}(j-1)Z_{\textrm{M}^{\oplus n}}(j+1)}=\left(\frac{Z_{\textrm{M}}(j)^{2}}{Z_{\textrm{M}}(j-1)Z_{\textrm{M}}(j+1)}\right)^{n}&lt;1." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><msup><mtext>M</mtext><mrow><mo rspace="0em">⊕</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><msup><mtext>M</mtext><mrow><mo rspace="0em">⊕</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><msup><mtext>M</mtext><mrow><mo rspace="0em">⊕</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mtext>M</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mtext>M</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mtext>M</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{Z_{\textrm{M}^{\oplus n}}(j)^{2}}{Z_{\textrm{M}^{\oplus n}}(j-1)Z_{\textrm{M}^{\oplus n}}(j+1)}=\left(\frac{Z_{\textrm{M}}(j)^{2}}{Z_{\textrm{M}}(j-1)Z_{\textrm{M}}(j+1)}\right)^{n}&lt;1.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.p3.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.p3.2.1" class="ltx_text">The second statement follows from
(<a href="#S2.E4" title="Equation 4 ‣ Lemma 2.1 ‣ 2.2. Multichain counts ‣ 2. Polynomial invariants and multichain counts ‣ Chow and chain polynomials of matroids are not log-concave" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>).
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.3. </span>Chow polynomials and augmented Chow polynomials</h3>

<div id="S2.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">The Chow ring <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{\operatorname{CH}}(\textrm{M})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>CH</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext>M</mtext><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{\operatorname{CH}}(\textrm{M})</annotation></semantics></math> and the augmented Chow ring <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{CH}(\textrm{M})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>CH</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext>M</mtext><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{CH}(\textrm{M})</annotation></semantics></math>
have Hilbert–Poincaré polynomials</p>
<table id="S2.Ex45" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex45.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{\textrm{H}}(\textrm{M};t)=\sum_{i=0}^{r-1}\dim\underline{\operatorname{CH}}^{i}(\textrm{M})t^{i},\quad\textrm{H}(\textrm{M};t)=\sum_{i=0}^{r}\dim\operatorname{CH}^{i}(\textrm{M})t^{i}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mtext>H</mtext><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext>M</mtext><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mrow><msup><munder accentunder="true"><mi>CH</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext>M</mtext><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mtext>H</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext>M</mtext><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi>r</mi></munderover><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>CH</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext>M</mtext><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{\textrm{H}}(\textrm{M};t)=\sum_{i=0}^{r-1}\dim\underline{\operatorname{CH}}^{i}(\textrm{M})t^{i},\quad\textrm{H}(\textrm{M};t)=\sum_{i=0}^{r}\dim\operatorname{CH}^{i}(\textrm{M})t^{i}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS3.p1.3" class="ltx_p">We first describe the Chow polynomial using chains of flats, using <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>, Proposition 3.6]</cite> which is based on the Feichtner–Yuzvinsky Gröbner basis.
For a nonempty subset <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="S=\{s_{1}&lt;\cdots&lt;s_{k}\}\neq\varnothing" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.1389em">&lt;</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.1389em">⋯</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em">&lt;</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S=\{s_{1}&lt;\cdots&lt;s_{k}\}\neq\varnothing</annotation></semantics></math>, let</p>
<table id="S2.Ex46" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.Ex46X"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex46X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\underline{\tau}(S)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\underline{\tau}(S)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex46X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=t[s_{1}-1]_{t}\prod_{i=2}^{k}t[s_{i}-s_{i-1}-1]_{t}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∏</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=t[s_{1}-1]_{t}\prod_{i=2}^{k}t[s_{i}-s_{i-1}-1]_{t},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S2.Ex46Xa"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex46Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\tau(S)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\tau(S)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex46Xa.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=t[s_{1}]_{t}\prod_{i=2}^{k}t[s_{i}-s_{i-1}-1]_{t}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∏</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=t[s_{1}]_{t}\prod_{i=2}^{k}t[s_{i}-s_{i-1}-1]_{t},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS3.p1.4" class="ltx_p">with <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{\tau}(\varnothing)=\tau(\varnothing)=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{\tau}(\varnothing)=\tau(\varnothing)=1</annotation></semantics></math>.
Grouping chains by their proper-flat rank sets gives</p>
<table id="S2.E6" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S2.E6X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="2" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_left">(6)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E6X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\underline{\textrm{H}}(\textrm{M};t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mtext>H</mtext><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext>M</mtext><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\underline{\textrm{H}}(\textrm{M};t)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E6X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\sum_{S\subseteq[r-1]}\alpha_{\textrm{M}}(S)\bigl(\underline{\tau}(S)+\underline{\tau}(S\cup\{r\})\bigr)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></munder></mstyle><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mtext>M</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\sum_{S\subseteq[r-1]}\alpha_{\textrm{M}}(S)\bigl(\underline{\tau}(S)+\underline{\tau}(S\cup\{r\})\bigr),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr>
<tr id="S2.E6Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E6Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\textrm{H}(\textrm{M};t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>H</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext>M</mtext><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\textrm{H}(\textrm{M};t)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E6Xa.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\sum_{S\subseteq[r-1]}\alpha_{\textrm{M}}(S)\bigl(\tau(S)+\tau(S\cup\{r\})\bigr)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></munder></mstyle><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mtext>M</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\sum_{S\subseteq[r-1]}\alpha_{\textrm{M}}(S)\bigl(\tau(S)+\tau(S\cup\{r\})\bigr).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The Chow
and augmented Chow polynomials can be expressed using the flag <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math>-vectors
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">44</a>, Theorems 1.1 and 1.2]</cite>. A set <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S\subseteq[r-1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S\subseteq[r-1]</annotation></semantics></math> is <em id="S2.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">stable</em> if it contains
no consecutive integers. Then</p>
<table id="S2.Ex47" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.Ex47X"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex47X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\underline{\textrm{H}}(\textrm{M};t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mtext>H</mtext><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext>M</mtext><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\underline{\textrm{H}}(\textrm{M};t)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex47X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\sum_{\begin{subarray}{c}S\subseteq[r-1]\text{ stable}\\
1\notin S\end{subarray}}\beta_{\textrm{M}}(S)t^{|S|}(1+t)^{r-1-2|S|}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> stable</mtext></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>∉</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></munder></mstyle><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mtext>M</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\sum_{\begin{subarray}{c}S\subseteq[r-1]\text{ stable}\\
1\notin S\end{subarray}}\beta_{\textrm{M}}(S)t^{|S|}(1+t)^{r-1-2|S|},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S2.Ex47Xa"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex47Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\textrm{H}(\textrm{M};t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>H</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext>M</mtext><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\textrm{H}(\textrm{M};t)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex47Xa.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\sum_{\begin{subarray}{c}S\subseteq[r-1]\text{ stable}\end{subarray}}\beta_{\textrm{M}}(S)t^{|S|}(1+t)^{r-2|S|}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> stable</mtext></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></munder></mstyle><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mtext>M</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\sum_{\begin{subarray}{c}S\subseteq[r-1]\text{ stable}\end{subarray}}\beta_{\textrm{M}}(S)t^{|S|}(1+t)^{r-2|S|}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.4. </span>Asymptotic coefficients</h3>

<div id="S2.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we give asymptotic formulas for the coefficients of the aforementioned polynomial invariants of the direct sum <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textrm{M}^{\oplus n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mtext>M</mtext><mrow><mo rspace="0em">⊕</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textrm{M}^{\oplus n}</annotation></semantics></math> in terms of the multichain counts of <span id="S2.SS4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">M</span>. First, we have the following explicit formula for every coefficient of the face enumeration polynomials of <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textrm{M}^{\oplus n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mtext>M</mtext><mrow><mo rspace="0em">⊕</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textrm{M}^{\oplus n}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmthm3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 2.3</span></span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmthm3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmthm3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For <math id="S2.Thmthm3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geqslant 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>⩾</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geqslant 1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.Thmthm3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j\geqslant 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>⩾</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j\geqslant 0</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="S2.EGx5" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.Ex48"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex48.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle f_{j-1}(\textrm{M}^{\oplus n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mtext>M</mtext><mrow><mo rspace="0em">⊕</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle f_{j-1}(\textrm{M}^{\oplus n})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex48.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\sum_{m=1}^{j+1}(-1)^{j+1-m}\binom{j+1}{m}Z_{\textrm{M}}(m)^{n}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover></mstyle><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>m</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mtext>M</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\sum_{m=1}^{j+1}(-1)^{j+1-m}\binom{j+1}{m}Z_{\textrm{M}}(m)^{n},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S2.Ex49"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex49.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle h_{j}(\textrm{M}^{\oplus n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mtext>M</mtext><mrow><mo rspace="0em">⊕</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle h_{j}(\textrm{M}^{\oplus n})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex49.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\sum_{m=1}^{j+1}(-1)^{j+1-m}\binom{nr+1}{j+1-m}Z_{\textrm{M}}(m)^{n}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover></mstyle><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mtext>M</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\sum_{m=1}^{j+1}(-1)^{j+1-m}\binom{nr+1}{j+1-m}Z_{\textrm{M}}(m)^{n}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS4.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S2.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">The first identity follows by binomial inversion from Equation
 <a href="#S2.E3" title="Equation 3 ‣ 2.2. Multichain counts ‣ 2. Polynomial invariants and multichain counts ‣ Chow and chain polynomials of matroids are not log-concave" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> and Equation <a href="#S2.E5" title="Equation 5 ‣ 2.2. Multichain counts ‣ 2. Polynomial invariants and multichain counts ‣ Chow and chain polynomials of matroids are not log-concave" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>. For the second identity, the identity</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex50" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex50.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{m\geqslant 0}\binom{m}{k+1}t^{m}=\frac{t^{k+1}}{(1-t)^{k+2}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>⩾</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></munder><mrow><mrow><mo lspace="0em">(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mi>m</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><msup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{m\geqslant 0}\binom{m}{k+1}t^{m}=\frac{t^{k+1}}{(1-t)^{k+2}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS4.p2.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS4.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text">implies that</span></p>
<table id="S2.EGx6" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.Ex51"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex51.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\sum_{m\geqslant 1}Z_{\textrm{M}^{\oplus n}}(m)t^{m-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>⩾</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munder></mstyle><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><msup><mtext>M</mtext><mrow><mo rspace="0em">⊕</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\sum_{m\geqslant 1}Z_{\textrm{M}^{\oplus n}}(m)t^{m-1}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex51.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\sum_{k=0}^{nr-1}f_{k-1}(\textrm{M}^{\oplus n})\frac{t^{k}}{(1-t)^{k+2}}=\frac{h(\textrm{M}^{\oplus n};t)}{(1-t)^{nr+1}}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover></mstyle><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mtext>M</mtext><mrow><mo rspace="0em">⊕</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msup><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mtext>M</mtext><mrow><mo rspace="0em">⊕</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\sum_{k=0}^{nr-1}f_{k-1}(\textrm{M}^{\oplus n})\frac{t^{k}}{(1-t)^{k+2}}=\frac{h(\textrm{M}^{\oplus n};t)}{(1-t)^{nr+1}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS4.p2.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS4.p2.3.1" class="ltx_text">Multiply by <math id="S2.SS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1-t)^{nr+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1-t)^{nr+1}</annotation></semantics></math>, substitute
<math id="S2.SS4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{\textrm{M}^{\oplus n}}(m)=Z_{\textrm{M}}(m)^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><msup><mtext>M</mtext><mrow><mo rspace="0em">⊕</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mtext>M</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{\textrm{M}^{\oplus n}}(m)=Z_{\textrm{M}}(m)^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, and compare coefficients.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS4.p3.2" class="ltx_p">For the asymptotic coefficients of the Chow and augmented Chow polynomials of <math id="S2.SS4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textrm{M}^{\oplus n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mtext>M</mtext><mrow><mo rspace="0em">⊕</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textrm{M}^{\oplus n}</annotation></semantics></math>, we express the polynomials using incidence-algebraic techniques from the work of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite>.
The incidence algebra <math id="S2.SS4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{I}(\mathcal{L})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{I}(\mathcal{L})</annotation></semantics></math> is an associative algebra, consisting of polynomials on closed intervals of <math id="S2.SS4.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math>. For a more general description of incidence algebra for posets, see
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">42</a>, Section 3.7]</cite>.
In <math id="S2.SS4.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{I}(\mathcal{L})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{I}(\mathcal{L})</annotation></semantics></math>, multiplication is convolution</p>
<table id="S2.Ex52" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex52.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(a\cdot b)(x,y;t)\coloneqq\sum_{x\leqslant u\leqslant y}a(x,u;t)\,b(u,y;t),\text{ for every }x\leqslant y\text{ in }\mathcal{L}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">≔</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>⩽</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>⩽</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mtext> for every </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>⩽</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> in </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(a\cdot b)(x,y;t)\coloneqq\sum_{x\leqslant u\leqslant y}a(x,u;t)\,b(u,y;t),\text{ for every }x\leqslant y\text{ in }\mathcal{L},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS4.p3.3" class="ltx_p">and the multiplicative identity of <math id="S2.SS4.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{I}(\mathcal{L})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{I}(\mathcal{L})</annotation></semantics></math> is the delta function</p>
<table id="S2.Ex53" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex53.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta(x,y)\coloneqq\begin{cases}1&amp;\text{ if }x=y\\
0&amp;\text{ otherwise}\end{cases}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mn>1</mn></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mtext> if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mtext> otherwise</mtext></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta(x,y)\coloneqq\begin{cases}1&amp;\text{ if }x=y\\
0&amp;\text{ otherwise}\end{cases}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS4.p3.4" class="ltx_p">We also have the zeta function, together with the Möbius function as its inverse:</p>
<table id="S2.Ex54" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex54.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\zeta(x,y)\coloneqq\begin{cases}1&amp;\text{ if }x\leqslant y\\
0&amp;\text{ otherwise }\end{cases},\qquad\mu(x,y)\coloneqq\begin{cases}1&amp;\text{ if }x=y\\
-\sum_{x\leqslant u&lt;y}\mu(x,u)&amp;\text{ if }x&lt;y\end{cases}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>ζ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mn>1</mn></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mtext> if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>⩽</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mtext> otherwise </mtext></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mn>1</mn></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mtext> if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mo>−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mo>∑</mo></mstyle><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>⩽</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mtext> if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\zeta(x,y)\coloneqq\begin{cases}1&amp;\text{ if }x\leqslant y\\
0&amp;\text{ otherwise }\end{cases},\qquad\mu(x,y)\coloneqq\begin{cases}1&amp;\text{ if }x=y\\
-\sum_{x\leqslant u&lt;y}\mu(x,u)&amp;\text{ if }x&lt;y\end{cases}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS4.p3.5" class="ltx_p">For <math id="S2.SS4.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS4.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{I}(\mathcal{L})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{I}(\mathcal{L})</annotation></semantics></math> admits an involution <math id="S2.SS4.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\mapsto a^{\mathrm{rev}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><msup><mi>a</mi><mi>rev</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\mapsto a^{\mathrm{rev}}</annotation></semantics></math> defined by</p>
<table id="S2.Ex55" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex55.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a^{\mathrm{rev}}(x,y;t)\coloneqq t^{\operatorname{rk}_{\textrm{M}}(y)-\operatorname{rk}_{\textrm{M}}(x)}a(x,y;t^{-1})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>a</mi><mi>rev</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≔</mo><mrow><msup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>rk</mi><mtext>M</mtext></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>rk</mi><mtext>M</mtext></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a^{\mathrm{rev}}(x,y;t)\coloneqq t^{\operatorname{rk}_{\textrm{M}}(y)-\operatorname{rk}_{\textrm{M}}(x)}a(x,y;t^{-1}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS4.p3.6" class="ltx_p">which reverses the coefficients of the polynomials <math id="S2.SS4.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a(x,y;t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a(x,y;t)</annotation></semantics></math> on an interval <math id="S2.SS4.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[x,y]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[x,y]</annotation></semantics></math>.
The <em id="S2.SS4.p3.6.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">characteristic function</em> is defined as</p>
<table id="S2.Ex56" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex56.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi\coloneqq\mu\cdot\zeta^{\mathrm{rev}},\text{ with }\zeta^{\mathrm{rev}}(x,y;t)=t^{\operatorname{rk}(y)-\operatorname{rk}(x)}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msup><mi>ζ</mi><mi>rev</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mtext> with </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ζ</mi><mi>rev</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>rk</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>rk</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi\coloneqq\mu\cdot\zeta^{\mathrm{rev}},\text{ with }\zeta^{\mathrm{rev}}(x,y;t)=t^{\operatorname{rk}(y)-\operatorname{rk}(x)},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS4.p3.7" class="ltx_p">and for <math id="S2.SS4.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS4.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi(\widehat{0},\widehat{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mn>0</mn><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mn>1</mn><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi(\widehat{0},\widehat{1})</annotation></semantics></math> coincides with the characteristic polynomial of the matroid <span id="S2.SS4.p3.7.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">M</span>.
Following the construction and result in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>, Definitions 3.2, 3.3, 3.13 and Theorems 4.9, 4.11]</cite>,
the Chow and left augmented Chow functions are defined as</p>
<table id="S2.E7" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S2.E7X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="2" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_left">(7)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E7X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\underline{\textrm{H}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><munder accentunder="true"><mtext>H</mtext><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\underline{\textrm{H}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E7X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\coloneqq(1-t)(\chi-t\delta)^{-1}=(1-t)\sum_{m\geqslant 1}t^{m-1}\chi^{-m}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>⩾</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munder></mstyle><mrow><msup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>χ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\coloneqq(1-t)(\chi-t\delta)^{-1}=(1-t)\sum_{m\geqslant 1}t^{m-1}\chi^{-m},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr>
<tr id="S2.E7Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E7Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\textrm{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext>H</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\textrm{H}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E7Xa.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\coloneqq\zeta^{\mathrm{rev}}\cdot\underline{\textrm{H}}=(1-t)\sum_{m\geqslant 1}t^{m-1}\bigl(\zeta^{\mathrm{rev}}\cdot\chi^{-m}\bigr)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≔</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ζ</mi><mi>rev</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mtext>H</mtext><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>⩾</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munder></mstyle><mrow><msup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ζ</mi><mi>rev</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msup><mi>χ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\coloneqq\zeta^{\mathrm{rev}}\cdot\underline{\textrm{H}}=(1-t)\sum_{m\geqslant 1}t^{m-1}\bigl(\zeta^{\mathrm{rev}}\cdot\chi^{-m}\bigr),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS4.p3.8" class="ltx_p">with <math id="S2.SS4.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{\textrm{H}}(\widehat{0},\widehat{1};t)=\underline{\textrm{H}}(\textrm{M};t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mtext>H</mtext><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mn>0</mn><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mn>1</mn><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mtext>H</mtext><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext>M</mtext><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{\textrm{H}}(\widehat{0},\widehat{1};t)=\underline{\textrm{H}}(\textrm{M};t)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS4.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textrm{H}(\widehat{0},\widehat{1};t)=\textrm{H}(\textrm{M};t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext>H</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mn>0</mn><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mn>1</mn><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>H</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext>M</mtext><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textrm{H}(\widehat{0},\widehat{1};t)=\textrm{H}(\textrm{M};t)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmthm4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 2.4</span></span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmthm4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm4.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmthm4.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For every <math id="S2.Thmthm4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geqslant 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>⩾</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geqslant 1</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="S2.E8" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S2.E8X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="2" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_left">(8)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E8X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\underline{\textrm{H}}(\textrm{M}^{\oplus n};t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mtext>H</mtext><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mtext>M</mtext><mrow><mo rspace="0em">⊕</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\underline{\textrm{H}}(\textrm{M}^{\oplus n};t)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E8X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=(1-t)\sum_{m\geqslant 1}t^{m-1}\bigl((\chi^{-m})(\widehat{0},\widehat{1};t)\bigr)^{n}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>⩾</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munder></mstyle><mrow><msup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>χ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mn>0</mn><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mn>1</mn><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=(1-t)\sum_{m\geqslant 1}t^{m-1}\bigl((\chi^{-m})(\widehat{0},\widehat{1};t)\bigr)^{n},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr>
<tr id="S2.E8Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E8Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\textrm{H}(\textrm{M}^{\oplus n};t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>H</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mtext>M</mtext><mrow><mo rspace="0em">⊕</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\textrm{H}(\textrm{M}^{\oplus n};t)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E8Xa.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=(1-t)\sum_{m\geqslant 1}t^{m-1}\bigl((\zeta^{\mathrm{rev}}\cdot\chi^{-m})(\widehat{0},\widehat{1};t)\bigr)^{n}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>⩾</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munder></mstyle><mrow><msup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ζ</mi><mi>rev</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msup><mi>χ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mn>0</mn><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mn>1</mn><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=(1-t)\sum_{m\geqslant 1}t^{m-1}\bigl((\zeta^{\mathrm{rev}}\cdot\chi^{-m})(\widehat{0},\widehat{1};t)\bigr)^{n}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS4.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S2.SS4.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS4.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS4.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">Since <math id="S2.SS4.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}(\textrm{M}^{\oplus n})\cong\mathcal{L}(\textrm{M})^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mtext>M</mtext><mrow><mo rspace="0em">⊕</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext>M</mtext><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}(\textrm{M}^{\oplus n})\cong\mathcal{L}(\textrm{M})^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, the functions <math id="S2.SS4.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\zeta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ζ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\zeta</annotation></semantics></math>,
<math id="S2.SS4.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\zeta^{\mathrm{rev}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ζ</mi><mi>rev</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\zeta^{\mathrm{rev}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S2.SS4.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>χ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi</annotation></semantics></math> in each summand of Equation <a href="#S2.E7" title="Equation 7 ‣ 2.4. Asymptotic coefficients ‣ 2. Polynomial invariants and multichain counts ‣ Chow and chain polynomials of matroids are not log-concave" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a> factor over the direct sum. Convolution and taking inverses respect this factorization, and the formulas then follow.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS4.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS4.p5.1" class="ltx_p">We have the following computational lemma relating the expressions above to multichain counts.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmthm5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 2.5</span></span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmthm5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmthm5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For any fixed <math id="S2.Thmthm5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\geqslant 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>⩾</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\geqslant 1</annotation></semantics></math>, and a polynomial <math id="S2.Thmthm5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{m}(\textrm{M};t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext>M</mtext><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{m}(\textrm{M};t)</annotation></semantics></math> equal to</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex57" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex57.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\chi_{\textrm{M}}^{-m})(\widehat{0},\widehat{1};t)\quad\text{ or }\quad(\zeta_{\textrm{M}}^{\mathrm{rev}}\cdot\chi_{\textrm{M}}^{-m})(\widehat{0},\widehat{1};t)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>χ</mi><mtext>M</mtext><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mn>0</mn><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mn>1</mn><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"> or </mtext><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>ζ</mi><mtext>M</mtext><mi>rev</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msubsup><mi>χ</mi><mtext>M</mtext><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mn>0</mn><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mn>1</mn><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\chi_{\textrm{M}}^{-m})(\widehat{0},\widehat{1};t)\quad\text{ or }\quad(\zeta_{\textrm{M}}^{\mathrm{rev}}\cdot\chi_{\textrm{M}}^{-m})(\widehat{0},\widehat{1};t),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.Thmthm5.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmthm5.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">we have the constant terms</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex58" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex58.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{m}(\textrm{M};0)=\zeta_{\textrm{M}}^{m}(\widehat{0},\widehat{1})=Z_{\textrm{M}}(m),\qquad Q_{m}(\textrm{M}^{\oplus n};0)=Z_{\textrm{M}}(m)^{n}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext>M</mtext><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>ζ</mi><mtext>M</mtext><mi>m</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mn>0</mn><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mn>1</mn><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mtext>M</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mtext>M</mtext><mrow><mo rspace="0em">⊕</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mtext>M</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{m}(\textrm{M};0)=\zeta_{\textrm{M}}^{m}(\widehat{0},\widehat{1})=Z_{\textrm{M}}(m),\qquad Q_{m}(\textrm{M}^{\oplus n};0)=Z_{\textrm{M}}(m)^{n}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS4.4" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S2.SS4.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS4.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS4.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">We first consider the case of a single matroid <span id="S2.SS4.p6.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">M</span>. For <math id="S2.SS4.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\chi_{\textrm{M}}^{-m})(\widehat{0},\widehat{1};t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>χ</mi><mtext>M</mtext><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mn>0</mn><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mn>1</mn><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\chi_{\textrm{M}}^{-m})(\widehat{0},\widehat{1};t)</annotation></semantics></math>, evaluation at <math id="S2.SS4.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=0</annotation></semantics></math> commutes with convolution and therefore also with
inversion. Since <math id="S2.SS4.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\chi_{\textrm{M}}(0)=\mu_{\textrm{M}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>χ</mi><mtext>M</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mtext>M</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\chi_{\textrm{M}}(0)=\mu_{\textrm{M}}}</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="S2.SS4.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{\textrm{M}}^{-1}=\zeta_{\textrm{M}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>μ</mi><mtext>M</mtext><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>ζ</mi><mtext>M</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{\textrm{M}}^{-1}=\zeta_{\textrm{M}}</annotation></semantics></math>, we obtain</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex59" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex59.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi_{\textrm{M}}^{-m}(0)=\mu_{\textrm{M}}^{-m}=\zeta_{\textrm{M}}^{m}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>χ</mi><mtext>M</mtext><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>μ</mi><mtext>M</mtext><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>ζ</mi><mtext>M</mtext><mi>m</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi_{\textrm{M}}^{-m}(0)=\mu_{\textrm{M}}^{-m}=\zeta_{\textrm{M}}^{m}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS4.p6.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS4.p6.2.1" class="ltx_text">For <math id="S2.SS4.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\zeta_{\textrm{M}}^{\mathrm{rev}}\cdot\chi_{\textrm{M}}^{-m})(\widehat{0},\widehat{1};t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>ζ</mi><mtext>M</mtext><mi>rev</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msubsup><mi>χ</mi><mtext>M</mtext><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mn>0</mn><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mn>1</mn><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\zeta_{\textrm{M}}^{\mathrm{rev}}\cdot\chi_{\textrm{M}}^{-m})(\widehat{0},\widehat{1};t)</annotation></semantics></math>, since
<math id="S2.SS4.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\zeta_{\textrm{M}}^{\mathrm{rev}}(0)=\delta_{\textrm{M}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>ζ</mi><mtext>M</mtext><mi>rev</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mtext>M</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\zeta_{\textrm{M}}^{\mathrm{rev}}(0)=\delta_{\textrm{M}}</annotation></semantics></math>, which is the multiplicative identity, the constant term is unchanged:</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex60" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex60.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\zeta_{\textrm{M}}^{\mathrm{rev}}\cdot\chi_{\textrm{M}}^{-m})(0)=\chi_{\textrm{M}}^{-m}(0)=\zeta^{m}_{\textrm{M}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>ζ</mi><mtext>M</mtext><mi>rev</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msubsup><mi>χ</mi><mtext>M</mtext><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>χ</mi><mtext>M</mtext><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>ζ</mi><mtext>M</mtext><mi>m</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\zeta_{\textrm{M}}^{\mathrm{rev}}\cdot\chi_{\textrm{M}}^{-m})(0)=\chi_{\textrm{M}}^{-m}(0)=\zeta^{m}_{\textrm{M}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS4.p6.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS4.p6.3.1" class="ltx_text">Therefore, <math id="S2.SS4.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{m}(\textrm{M};0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext>M</mtext><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{m}(\textrm{M};0)</annotation></semantics></math> is equal to</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex61" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex61.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\zeta_{\textrm{M}}^{m}(\widehat{0},\widehat{1})=\sum_{\widehat{0}=F_{0}\leqslant F_{1}\leqslant\cdots\leqslant F_{m}=\widehat{1}}\,\Big(\prod_{i=1}^{m}\zeta_{\textrm{M}}(F_{i-1},F_{i})\Big)=\sum_{\widehat{0}=F_{0}\leqslant F_{1}\leqslant\cdots\leqslant F_{m}=\widehat{1}}1=Z_{\textrm{M}}(m)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>ζ</mi><mtext>M</mtext><mi>m</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mn>0</mn><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mn>1</mn><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mn>0</mn><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>⩽</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.1389em">⩽</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.1389em">⋯</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em">⩽</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mover accent="true"><mn>1</mn><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow></munder><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><munderover><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false">∏</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>m</mi></munderover><mrow><msub><mi>ζ</mi><mtext>M</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mn>0</mn><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>⩽</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.1389em">⩽</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.1389em">⋯</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em">⩽</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mover accent="true"><mn>1</mn><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow></munder><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mtext>M</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\zeta_{\textrm{M}}^{m}(\widehat{0},\widehat{1})=\sum_{\widehat{0}=F_{0}\leqslant F_{1}\leqslant\cdots\leqslant F_{m}=\widehat{1}}\,\Big(\prod_{i=1}^{m}\zeta_{\textrm{M}}(F_{i-1},F_{i})\Big)=\sum_{\widehat{0}=F_{0}\leqslant F_{1}\leqslant\cdots\leqslant F_{m}=\widehat{1}}1=Z_{\textrm{M}}(m).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS4.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS4.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS4.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text">We next apply the same argument to <math id="S2.SS4.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textrm{M}^{\oplus n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mtext>M</mtext><mrow><mo rspace="0em">⊕</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textrm{M}^{\oplus n}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Since <math id="S2.SS4.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}(\textrm{M}^{\oplus n})\cong\mathcal{L}(\textrm{M})^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mtext>M</mtext><mrow><mo rspace="0em">⊕</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext>M</mtext><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}(\textrm{M}^{\oplus n})\cong\mathcal{L}(\textrm{M})^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, a multichain in this
product is determined by an independent choice of a multichain
in each coordinate. Therefore,</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex62" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex62.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{m}(\textrm{M}^{\oplus n};0)=Z_{\textrm{M}^{\oplus n}}(m)=Z_{\textrm{M}}(m)^{n}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mtext>M</mtext><mrow><mo rspace="0em">⊕</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><msup><mtext>M</mtext><mrow><mo rspace="0em">⊕</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mtext>M</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{m}(\textrm{M}^{\oplus n};0)=Z_{\textrm{M}^{\oplus n}}(m)=Z_{\textrm{M}}(m)^{n}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS4.p7.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS4.p7.2.1" class="ltx_text">∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmthm6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 2.6</span></span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmthm6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmthm6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S2.Thmthm6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(\textrm{M};t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext>M</mtext><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(\textrm{M};t)</annotation></semantics></math> denote any one of</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex63" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex63.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(\mathcal{L};t),\qquad f(\overline{\mathcal{L}};t),\qquad h(\textrm{M};t),\qquad\underline{\textrm{H}}(\textrm{M};t),\qquad\textrm{H}(\textrm{M};t)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext>M</mtext><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mtext>H</mtext><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext>M</mtext><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mtext>H</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext>M</mtext><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(\mathcal{L};t),\qquad f(\overline{\mathcal{L}};t),\qquad h(\textrm{M};t),\qquad\underline{\textrm{H}}(\textrm{M};t),\qquad\textrm{H}(\textrm{M};t),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.Thmthm6.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmthm6.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">and write <math id="S2.Thmthm6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(\textrm{M};t)=\sum_{j}P_{j}(\textrm{M})t^{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext>M</mtext><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>j</mi></msub><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext>M</mtext><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mi>j</mi></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(\textrm{M};t)=\sum_{j}P_{j}(\textrm{M})t^{j}</annotation></semantics></math>.
For a fixed <math id="S2.Thmthm6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j\geqslant 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>⩾</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j\geqslant 1</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="S2.E9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(9)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lim_{n\to\infty}P_{j}(\textrm{M}^{\oplus n})=Z_{\textrm{M}}(j+1)^{n}+O\bigl(n^{j}Z_{\textrm{M}}(j)^{n}\bigr)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">lim</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mtext>M</mtext><mrow><mo rspace="0em">⊕</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mtext>M</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mi>j</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mtext>M</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lim_{n\to\infty}P_{j}(\textrm{M}^{\oplus n})=Z_{\textrm{M}}(j+1)^{n}+O\bigl(n^{j}Z_{\textrm{M}}(j)^{n}\bigr).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.Thmthm6.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmthm6.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Furthermore,</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex64" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex64.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lim_{n\to\infty}\frac{P_{j}(\textrm{M}^{\oplus n})}{Z_{\textrm{M}}(j+1)^{n}}=1." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">lim</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></munder><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mtext>M</mtext><mrow><mo rspace="0em">⊕</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mtext>M</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lim_{n\to\infty}\frac{P_{j}(\textrm{M}^{\oplus n})}{Z_{\textrm{M}}(j+1)^{n}}=1.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS4.5" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S2.SS4.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS4.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS4.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text">For <math id="S2.SS4.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(\textrm{M};t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext>M</mtext><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(\textrm{M};t)</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S2.SS4.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h(\textrm{M};t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext>M</mtext><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h(\textrm{M};t)</annotation></semantics></math>, since <math id="S2.SS4.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{\textrm{M}}(m)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mtext>M</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{\textrm{M}}(m)</annotation></semantics></math> is strictly increasing, <a href="#S2.Thmthm3" title="Proposition 2.3 ‣ 2.4. Asymptotic coefficients ‣ 2. Polynomial invariants and multichain counts ‣ Chow and chain polynomials of matroids are not log-concave" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Proposition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.3</span></a> implies that as <math id="S2.SS4.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> goes to <math id="S2.SS4.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\infty</annotation></semantics></math>, the <math id="S2.SS4.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>th coefficient of <math id="S2.SS4.p8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(\textrm{M}^{\oplus n};t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mtext>M</mtext><mrow><mo rspace="0em">⊕</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(\textrm{M}^{\oplus n};t)</annotation></semantics></math>, which is <math id="S2.SS4.p8.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{j-1}(\textrm{M}^{\oplus n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mtext>M</mtext><mrow><mo rspace="0em">⊕</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{j-1}(\textrm{M}^{\oplus n})</annotation></semantics></math>, or that of <math id="S2.SS4.p8.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h(\textrm{M}^{\oplus n};t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mtext>M</mtext><mrow><mo rspace="0em">⊕</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h(\textrm{M}^{\oplus n};t)</annotation></semantics></math>, which is <math id="S2.SS4.p8.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{j}(\textrm{M}^{\oplus n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mtext>M</mtext><mrow><mo rspace="0em">⊕</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{j}(\textrm{M}^{\oplus n})</annotation></semantics></math>, is dominated by the term <math id="S2.SS4.p8.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{\textrm{M}}(j+1)^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mtext>M</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{\textrm{M}}(j+1)^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>. Therefore, we have the asymptotic ratios</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex65" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex65.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lim_{n\to\infty}\frac{f_{j-1}(\textrm{M}^{\oplus n})}{Z_{\textrm{M}}(j+1)^{n}}=1,\qquad\lim_{n\to\infty}\frac{h_{j}(\textrm{M}^{\oplus n})}{Z_{\textrm{M}}(j+1)^{n}}=1." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">lim</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></munder><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mtext>M</mtext><mrow><mo rspace="0em">⊕</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mtext>M</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="1.889em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">lim</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></munder><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mtext>M</mtext><mrow><mo rspace="0em">⊕</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mtext>M</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lim_{n\to\infty}\frac{f_{j-1}(\textrm{M}^{\oplus n})}{Z_{\textrm{M}}(j+1)^{n}}=1,\qquad\lim_{n\to\infty}\frac{h_{j}(\textrm{M}^{\oplus n})}{Z_{\textrm{M}}(j+1)^{n}}=1.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS4.p8.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS4.p8.2.1" class="ltx_text">By (<a href="#S2.E2" title="Equation 2 ‣ 2.1. Chain and face enumeration polynomials ‣ 2. Polynomial invariants and multichain counts ‣ Chow and chain polynomials of matroids are not log-concave" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>), the same limit is true for the <math id="S2.SS4.p8.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>th coefficient of <math id="S2.SS4.p8.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(\mathcal{L};t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(\mathcal{L};t)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS4.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS4.p9.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS4.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text">For <math id="S2.SS4.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{\textrm{H}}(\textrm{M};t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mtext>H</mtext><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext>M</mtext><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{\textrm{H}}(\textrm{M};t)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS4.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textrm{H}(\textrm{M};t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>H</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext>M</mtext><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textrm{H}(\textrm{M};t)</annotation></semantics></math>, if we write</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex66" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex66.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{m}(\textrm{M}^{\oplus n};t)=\begin{cases}(\chi^{-m})(\widehat{0},\widehat{1};t)&amp;\text{ for }\underline{\textrm{H}}\\
(\zeta^{\mathrm{rev}}\cdot\chi^{-m})(\widehat{0},\widehat{1};t)&amp;\text{ for }\textrm{H}\end{cases}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mtext>M</mtext><mrow><mo rspace="0em">⊕</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>χ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mn>0</mn><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mn>1</mn><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mtext> for </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mtext>H</mtext><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ζ</mi><mi>rev</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msup><mi>χ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mn>0</mn><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mn>1</mn><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mtext> for </mtext><mtext>H</mtext></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{m}(\textrm{M}^{\oplus n};t)=\begin{cases}(\chi^{-m})(\widehat{0},\widehat{1};t)&amp;\text{ for }\underline{\textrm{H}}\\
(\zeta^{\mathrm{rev}}\cdot\chi^{-m})(\widehat{0},\widehat{1};t)&amp;\text{ for }\textrm{H}\end{cases},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS4.p9.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS4.p9.2.1" class="ltx_text">then <a href="#S2.Thmthm4" title="Proposition 2.4 ‣ 2.4. Asymptotic coefficients ‣ 2. Polynomial invariants and multichain counts ‣ Chow and chain polynomials of matroids are not log-concave" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Proposition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.4</span></a> implies that</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex67" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex67.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(\textrm{M}^{\oplus n};t)=\sum_{m\geqslant 1}(t^{m-1}-t^{m})Q_{m}(\textrm{M}^{\oplus n};t)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mtext>M</mtext><mrow><mo rspace="0em">⊕</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>⩾</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munder><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mi>m</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mtext>M</mtext><mrow><mo rspace="0em">⊕</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(\textrm{M}^{\oplus n};t)=\sum_{m\geqslant 1}(t^{m-1}-t^{m})Q_{m}(\textrm{M}^{\oplus n};t).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS4.p9.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS4.p9.3.1" class="ltx_text">Therefore, for <math id="S2.SS4.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j\geqslant 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>⩾</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j\geqslant 1</annotation></semantics></math>, the <math id="S2.SS4.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>th coefficient of <math id="S2.SS4.p9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(\textrm{M}^{\oplus n};t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mtext>M</mtext><mrow><mo rspace="0em">⊕</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(\textrm{M}^{\oplus n};t)</annotation></semantics></math> is</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex68" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex68.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{j}(\textrm{M}^{\oplus n})=Q_{j+1}(\textrm{M};0)^{n}+\sum_{m=1}^{j}\big(Q_{m,j+1-m}(\textrm{M}^{\oplus n})-Q_{m,j-m}(\textrm{M}^{\oplus n})\big)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mtext>M</mtext><mrow><mo rspace="0em">⊕</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext>M</mtext><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">+</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>j</mi></munderover><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mtext>M</mtext><mrow><mo rspace="0em">⊕</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mtext>M</mtext><mrow><mo rspace="0em">⊕</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{j}(\textrm{M}^{\oplus n})=Q_{j+1}(\textrm{M};0)^{n}+\sum_{m=1}^{j}\big(Q_{m,j+1-m}(\textrm{M}^{\oplus n})-Q_{m,j-m}(\textrm{M}^{\oplus n})\big).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS4.p9.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS4.p9.4.1" class="ltx_text">Then Lemma <a href="#S2.Thmthm5" title="Lemma 2.5 ‣ 2.4. Asymptotic coefficients ‣ 2. Polynomial invariants and multichain counts ‣ Chow and chain polynomials of matroids are not log-concave" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.5</span></a> and Lemma <a href="#S2.Thmthm7" title="Lemma 2.7 ‣ 2.4. Asymptotic coefficients ‣ 2. Polynomial invariants and multichain counts ‣ Chow and chain polynomials of matroids are not log-concave" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.7</span></a> imply that, as <math id="S2.SS4.p9.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> goes to infinity, the first term <math id="S2.SS4.p9.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{j+1}(\textrm{M};0)^{n}=Z_{\textrm{M}}(j+1)^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext>M</mtext><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mtext>M</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{j+1}(\textrm{M};0)^{n}=Z_{\textrm{M}}(j+1)^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> dominates, and</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex69" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex69.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lim_{n\to\infty}\frac{P_{j}(\textrm{M}^{\oplus n})}{Z_{\textrm{M}}(j+1)^{n}}=1." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">lim</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></munder><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mtext>M</mtext><mrow><mo rspace="0em">⊕</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mtext>M</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lim_{n\to\infty}\frac{P_{j}(\textrm{M}^{\oplus n})}{Z_{\textrm{M}}(j+1)^{n}}=1.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS4.p9.5" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS4.p9.5.1" class="ltx_text">∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm7" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmthm7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 2.7</span></span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmthm7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm7.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmthm7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For either choice of <math id="S2.Thmthm7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{m}</annotation></semantics></math>, and for every fixed <math id="S2.Thmthm7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j\geqslant 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>⩾</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j\geqslant 1</annotation></semantics></math>, there exists a constant <math id="S2.Thmthm7.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C=C_{\textrm{M},j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mtext>M</mtext><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C=C_{\textrm{M},j}</annotation></semantics></math> such that, as <math id="S2.Thmthm7.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\to\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\to\infty</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex70" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex70.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{m=1}^{j}\bigl(Q_{m,j+1-m}(\textrm{M}^{\oplus n})-Q_{m,j-m}(\textrm{M}^{\oplus n})\bigr)\leqslant C_{\textrm{M},j}n^{j}Z_{\textrm{M}}(j)^{n}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>j</mi></munderover><mrow><mo lspace="0em" maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mtext>M</mtext><mrow><mo rspace="0em">⊕</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mtext>M</mtext><mrow><mo rspace="0em">⊕</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⩽</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mtext>M</mtext><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mi>j</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mtext>M</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{m=1}^{j}\bigl(Q_{m,j+1-m}(\textrm{M}^{\oplus n})-Q_{m,j-m}(\textrm{M}^{\oplus n})\bigr)\leqslant C_{\textrm{M},j}n^{j}Z_{\textrm{M}}(j)^{n}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS4.6" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S2.SS4.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS4.p10.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS4.p10.1.1" class="ltx_text">By multiplicativity and Lemma <a href="#S2.Thmthm5" title="Lemma 2.5 ‣ 2.4. Asymptotic coefficients ‣ 2. Polynomial invariants and multichain counts ‣ Chow and chain polynomials of matroids are not log-concave" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.5</span></a>,</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex71" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex71.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{m}(\textrm{M}^{\oplus n};t)=Q_{m}(\textrm{M};t)^{n},\qquad c_{m}:=Q_{m}(\textrm{M};0)=Z_{\textrm{M}}(m)&gt;0." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mtext>M</mtext><mrow><mo rspace="0em">⊕</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext>M</mtext><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext>M</mtext><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mtext>M</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{m}(\textrm{M}^{\oplus n};t)=Q_{m}(\textrm{M};t)^{n},\qquad c_{m}:=Q_{m}(\textrm{M};0)=Z_{\textrm{M}}(m)&gt;0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS4.p10.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS4.p10.2.1" class="ltx_text">Write <math id="S2.SS4.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{m}(\textrm{M};t)=c_{m}(1+tB_{m}(t))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext>M</mtext><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{m}(\textrm{M};t)=c_{m}(1+tB_{m}(t))</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S2.SS4.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{m}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{m}(t)</annotation></semantics></math>
is independent of <math id="S2.SS4.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>. The binomial theorem implies</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex72" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex72.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{m,d}(\textrm{M}^{\oplus n})=c_{m}^{n}\sum_{\ell=0}^{\min(n,d)}\binom{n}{\ell}B_{m,d-\ell}(t)^{\ell}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mtext>M</mtext><mrow><mo rspace="0em">⊕</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>c</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></munderover><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mi>n</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{m,d}(\textrm{M}^{\oplus n})=c_{m}^{n}\sum_{\ell=0}^{\min(n,d)}\binom{n}{\ell}B_{m,d-\ell}(t)^{\ell},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS4.p10.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS4.p10.3.1" class="ltx_text">where the coefficients
<math id="S2.SS4.p10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{m,d-\ell}(t)^{\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{m,d-\ell}(t)^{\ell}</annotation></semantics></math> are independent of <math id="S2.SS4.p10.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>.
Since <math id="S2.SS4.p10.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\leqslant\ell\leqslant\min(n,d)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>⩽</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⩽</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\leqslant\ell\leqslant\min(n,d)</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex73" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex73.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\binom{n}{\ell}\leqslant\frac{n^{\ell}}{\ell!}\leqslant\frac{n^{d}}{\ell!}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mi>n</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>⩽</mo><mfrac><msup><mi>n</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msup><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>!</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>⩽</mo><mfrac><msup><mi>n</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>!</mo></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\binom{n}{\ell}\leqslant\frac{n^{\ell}}{\ell!}\leqslant\frac{n^{d}}{\ell!},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS4.p10.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS4.p10.4.1" class="ltx_text">and there exists a constant <math id="S2.SS4.p10.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{\textrm{M},m,d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mtext>M</mtext><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{\textrm{M},m,d}</annotation></semantics></math> such that</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex74" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex74.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|Q_{m,d}(\textrm{M}^{\oplus n})|\leqslant C_{\textrm{M},m,d}\,n^{d}Z_{\textrm{M}}(m)^{n}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mtext>M</mtext><mrow><mo rspace="0em">⊕</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>⩽</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mtext>M</mtext><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mtext>M</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|Q_{m,d}(\textrm{M}^{\oplus n})|\leqslant C_{\textrm{M},m,d}\,n^{d}Z_{\textrm{M}}(m)^{n}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS4.p10.5" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS4.p10.5.1" class="ltx_text">There are only finitely many pairs
<math id="S2.SS4.p10.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leqslant m\leqslant j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>⩽</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>⩽</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leqslant m\leqslant j</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS4.p10.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\leqslant d\leqslant j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>⩽</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>⩽</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\leqslant d\leqslant j</annotation></semantics></math>,
so these bounds hold with a common constant <math id="S2.SS4.p10.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C=C(\textrm{M},j)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext>M</mtext><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C=C(\textrm{M},j)</annotation></semantics></math>.
Therefore,</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex75" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.Ex75X"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex75X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\left|\sum_{m=1}^{j}\bigl(Q_{m,j+1-m}(\textrm{M}^{\oplus n})-Q_{m,j-m}(\textrm{M}^{\oplus n})\bigr)\right|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>|</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>j</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mtext>M</mtext><mrow><mo rspace="0em">⊕</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mtext>M</mtext><mrow><mo rspace="0em">⊕</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\left|\sum_{m=1}^{j}\bigl(Q_{m,j+1-m}(\textrm{M}^{\oplus n})-Q_{m,j-m}(\textrm{M}^{\oplus n})\bigr)\right|</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex75X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\leqslant C\sum_{m=1}^{j}\bigl(n^{j+1-m}+n^{j-m}\bigr)Z_{\textrm{M}}(m)^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>⩽</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>j</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mtext>M</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\leqslant C\sum_{m=1}^{j}\bigl(n^{j+1-m}+n^{j-m}\bigr)Z_{\textrm{M}}(m)^{n}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S2.Ex75Xa"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex75Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\leqslant 2jC\,n^{j}Z_{\textrm{M}}(j)^{n}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>⩽</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mi>j</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mtext>M</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\leqslant 2jC\,n^{j}Z_{\textrm{M}}(j)^{n}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS4.p10.6" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS4.p10.6.1" class="ltx_text">Updating the bounding constant <math id="S2.SS4.p10.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> gives the desired statement.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm8" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmthm8.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 2.8</span></span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmthm8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm8.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The work of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>]</cite> gives a decomposition of Chow rings of direct sums of possibly different matroids using the Chow rings of the matroid summands. It would be interesting to obtain our asymptotic formula above using the decomposition.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS4.7" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof of <a href="#Thmintrothm1" title="Theorem A ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Chow and chain polynomials of matroids are not log-concave" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a>.</h6>
<div id="S2.SS4.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS4.p11.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS4.p11.1.1" class="ltx_text">Suppose that for any <math id="S2.SS4.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j\geqslant 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>⩾</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j\geqslant 2</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS4.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{\textrm{M}}(j)^{2}&lt;Z_{\textrm{M}}(j-1)Z_{\textrm{M}}(j+1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mtext>M</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mtext>M</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mtext>M</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{\textrm{M}}(j)^{2}&lt;Z_{\textrm{M}}(j-1)Z_{\textrm{M}}(j+1)</annotation></semantics></math>. By <a href="#S2.Thmthm6" title="Proposition 2.6 ‣ 2.4. Asymptotic coefficients ‣ 2. Polynomial invariants and multichain counts ‣ Chow and chain polynomials of matroids are not log-concave" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Proposition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.6</span></a>, for a fixed <math id="S2.SS4.p11.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j\geqslant 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>⩾</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j\geqslant 1</annotation></semantics></math>, as <math id="S2.SS4.p11.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> goes to infinity, the <math id="S2.SS4.p11.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>th coefficient
<math id="S2.SS4.p11.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{j}(\textrm{M}^{\oplus n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mtext>M</mtext><mrow><mo rspace="0em">⊕</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{j}(\textrm{M}^{\oplus n})</annotation></semantics></math> is asymptotically equal to <math id="S2.SS4.p11.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{\textrm{M}}(j+1)^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mtext>M</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{\textrm{M}}(j+1)^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Furthermore,</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex76" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex76.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{0}(\textrm{M}^{\oplus n})=1=Z_{\textrm{M}}(1)^{n}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mtext>M</mtext><mrow><mo rspace="0em">⊕</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mtext>M</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{0}(\textrm{M}^{\oplus n})=1=Z_{\textrm{M}}(1)^{n}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS4.p11.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS4.p11.2.1" class="ltx_text">Therefore, for any <math id="S2.SS4.p11.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j\geqslant 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>⩾</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j\geqslant 2</annotation></semantics></math>, the asymptotic ratio</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex77" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex77.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lim_{n\to\infty}\frac{P_{j-1}(\textrm{M}^{\oplus n})^{2}}{P_{j-2}(\textrm{M}^{\oplus n})P_{j}(\textrm{M}^{\oplus n})}=\lim_{n\to\infty}\left(\frac{Z_{\textrm{M}}(j)^{2}}{Z_{\textrm{M}}(j-1)Z_{\textrm{M}}(j+1)}\right)^{n}=0&lt;1," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">lim</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></munder><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mtext>M</mtext><mrow><mo rspace="0em">⊕</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mtext>M</mtext><mrow><mo rspace="0em">⊕</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mtext>M</mtext><mrow><mo rspace="0em">⊕</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0.1389em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">lim</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></munder><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mtext>M</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mtext>M</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mtext>M</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lim_{n\to\infty}\frac{P_{j-1}(\textrm{M}^{\oplus n})^{2}}{P_{j-2}(\textrm{M}^{\oplus n})P_{j}(\textrm{M}^{\oplus n})}=\lim_{n\to\infty}\left(\frac{Z_{\textrm{M}}(j)^{2}}{Z_{\textrm{M}}(j-1)Z_{\textrm{M}}(j+1)}\right)^{n}=0&lt;1,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS4.p11.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS4.p11.3.1" class="ltx_text">which implies that log-concavity of coefficients of <math id="S2.SS4.p11.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(\textrm{M}^{\oplus n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mtext>M</mtext><mrow><mo rspace="0em">⊕</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(\textrm{M}^{\oplus n})</annotation></semantics></math> fails for sufficiently large <math id="S2.SS4.p11.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>. Newton’s inequalities in turn imply failures of
real-rootedness <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a>, <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.5. </span>Face enumerations of graph associahedra</h3>

<div id="S2.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We now establish the graph analogue of Proposition <a href="#S2.Thmthm6" title="Proposition 2.6 ‣ 2.4. Asymptotic coefficients ‣ 2. Polynomial invariants and multichain counts ‣ Chow and chain polynomials of matroids are not log-concave" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.6</span></a>.
For matroids, direct sums give products of lattices of flats. For rooted
graphs, wedge of graphs gives products of posets of connected
vertex sets containing the root, which will play an analogous role.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The construction of a graph with non log-concave <math id="S2.SS5.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math>-polynomial has two distinct steps. First, we find a rooted connected graph whose numbers of multichains of rooted connected sets fail
log-concavity. Then identify the roots of sufficiently many copies of
that graph. We prove that this operation transfers the failure of log-concavity of the multichain counts to
the <math id="S2.SS5.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math>-polynomial of the resulting graph associahedron, and we will construct the explicit rooted graph in
Section <a href="#S5" title="5. ℎ-polynomials of graph associahedra are not log-concave ‣ Chow and chain polynomials of matroids are not log-concave" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS5.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">2.5.1. </span>Tubings and height functions</h4>

<div id="S2.SS5.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS5.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Throughout, we consider only finite simple graphs. For a finite simple graph <math id="S2.SS5.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S2.SS5.SSS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{B}(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{B}(G)</annotation></semantics></math> be the
set of nonempty vertex sets inducing connected subgraphs. A family
<math id="S2.SS5.SSS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}\subseteq\mathcal{B}(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}\subseteq\mathcal{B}(G)</annotation></semantics></math> is <em id="S2.SS5.SSS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">nested</em> if every two members are
comparable by inclusion, or disjoint with no edge between them.
If <math id="S2.SS5.SSS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is a connected graph, a <em id="S2.SS5.SSS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">tube</em> is a member of
<math id="S2.SS5.SSS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{B}(G)\setminus\{V(G)\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∖</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{B}(G)\setminus\{V(G)\}</annotation></semantics></math>, and a <em id="S2.SS5.SSS1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">tubing</em> is a nested family of
tubes. The complex of tubings is the simplicial complex dual to the
graph associahedron; it is pure of dimension <math id="S2.SS5.SSS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n-2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n-2</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S2.SS5.SSS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> is the number of vertices of <math id="S2.SS5.SSS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G.</annotation></semantics></math>
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">41</a>]</cite>. Its <math id="S2.SS5.SSS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math>-polynomial is</p>
<table id="S2.E10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(10)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{G}(t)=\sum_{\mathcal{T}}t^{|\mathcal{T}|}(1-t)^{n-1-|\mathcal{T}|}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi></munder><mrow><msup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{G}(t)=\sum_{\mathcal{T}}t^{|\mathcal{T}|}(1-t)^{n-1-|\mathcal{T}|},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS5.SSS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where the sum runs over all tubings, including the empty tubing.
We write <math id="S2.SS5.SSS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{j}(G)=[t^{j}]h_{G}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mi>j</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{j}(G)=[t^{j}]h_{G}(t)</annotation></semantics></math>. These coefficients are nonnegative.
We use <math id="S2.SS5.SSS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{K_{1}}(t)=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{K_{1}}(t)=1</annotation></semantics></math> when a component consists of one vertex.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS5.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS5.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">As recalled in Section <a href="#S2.SS6" title="2.6. Log-concavity and real-rootedness ‣ 2. Polynomial invariants and multichain counts ‣ Chow and chain polynomials of matroids are not log-concave" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.6</span></a>, Newton’s
inequalities imply that a real-rooted polynomial with nonnegative
coefficients is log-concave. Thus a strict reverse inequality for three
consecutive <math id="S2.SS5.SSS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math>-coefficients also disproves real-rootedness.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS5.SSS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS5.SSS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">For a height function <math id="S2.SS5.SSS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d:V(G)\to\mathbb{Z}_{\geq 0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d:V(G)\to\mathbb{Z}_{\geq 0}</annotation></semantics></math>, define</p>
<table id="S2.Ex78" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex78.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{G}(d)=\sum_{r\geq 1}\#\{\text{components of }G[\{x:d(x)\geqslant r\}]\},\qquad\Phi_{G}(t)=\sum_{d}t^{w_{G}(d)}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munder><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mtext>components of </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⩾</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mi>d</mi></munder><msup><mi>t</mi><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{G}(d)=\sum_{r\geq 1}\#\{\text{components of }G[\{x:d(x)\geqslant r\}]\},\qquad\Phi_{G}(t)=\sum_{d}t^{w_{G}(d)},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS5.SSS1.p3.2" class="ltx_p">which is a well-defined power series, since <math id="S2.SS5.SSS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{G}(d)\geqslant\max d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⩾</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{G}(d)\geqslant\max d</annotation></semantics></math>, and only
finitely many height functions contribute to any coefficient.
For the empty graph, set <math id="S2.SS5.SSS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi_{\varnothing}(t)=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi_{\varnothing}(t)=1</annotation></semantics></math>. The following classical result shows that the <math id="S2.SS5.SSS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math>-polynomial of a connected graph <math id="S2.SS5.SSS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is the numerator of the generating series <math id="S2.SS5.SSS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi_{G}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi_{G}.</annotation></semantics></math> For completeness, we provide a proof.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm9" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmthm9.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 2.9</span></span><span id="S2.Thmthm9.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">45</a>, Theorem 5.1]</cite>)</h6>
<div id="S2.Thmthm9.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm9.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmthm9.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">There is a weight-preserving bijection between height functions on
<math id="S2.Thmthm9.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> and nested families <math id="S2.Thmthm9.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}\subseteq\mathcal{B}(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}\subseteq\mathcal{B}(G)</annotation></semantics></math> equipped with
positive integer multiplicities <math id="S2.Thmthm9.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu(T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu(T)</annotation></semantics></math>, under which</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex79" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex79.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d(x)=\sum_{T\ni x}\mu(T),\qquad w_{G}(d)=\sum_{T\in\mathcal{S}}\mu(T)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>∋</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d(x)=\sum_{T\ni x}\mu(T),\qquad w_{G}(d)=\sum_{T\in\mathcal{S}}\mu(T).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.Thmthm9.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmthm9.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Consequently</span></p>
<table id="S2.E11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(11)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi_{G}(t)=\sum_{\mathcal{S}}\left(\frac{t}{1-t}\right)^{|\mathcal{S}|}=\prod_{C}\frac{h_{C}(t)}{(1-t)^{|C|}}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi></munder><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mi>t</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∏</mo><mi>C</mi></munder><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></msup></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi_{G}(t)=\sum_{\mathcal{S}}\left(\frac{t}{1-t}\right)^{|\mathcal{S}|}=\prod_{C}\frac{h_{C}(t)}{(1-t)^{|C|}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.Thmthm9.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmthm9.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="S2.Thmthm9.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> runs over the connected components of <math id="S2.Thmthm9.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>. In particular,
for connected <math id="S2.Thmthm9.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex80" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex80.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{G}(t)=(1-t)^{|V(G)|}\Phi_{G}(t)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{G}(t)=(1-t)^{|V(G)|}\Phi_{G}(t).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS5.SSS1.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S2.SS5.SSS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS5.SSS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS5.SSS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">Given <math id="S2.SS5.SSS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>, collect all components of all positive superlevel sets,
and let <math id="S2.SS5.SSS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu(T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu(T)</annotation></semantics></math> count the levels at which <math id="S2.SS5.SSS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> occurs. Components at
different levels are comparable or disjoint. Disjoint components
cannot be adjacent: an edge would connect them at the lower level.
Thus they form a nested family. Counting, for each vertex, the levels
at which it occurs gives the formula for <math id="S2.SS5.SSS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>, and counting components
gives the formula for <math id="S2.SS5.SSS1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{H}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS5.SSS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS5.SSS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS5.SSS1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">Conversely, start with <math id="S2.SS5.SSS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathcal{S},\mu)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{S},\mu)</annotation></semantics></math> and define <math id="S2.SS5.SSS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> by the displayed
formula. Put <math id="S2.SS5.SSS1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D(T)=\sum_{U\supseteq T}\mu(U)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>⊇</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D(T)=\sum_{U\supseteq T}\mu(U)</annotation></semantics></math>, with the sum over
<math id="S2.SS5.SSS1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U\in\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U\in\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math>. For
<math id="S2.SS5.SSS1.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D(T)-\mu(T)&lt;r\leqslant D(T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>⩽</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D(T)-\mu(T)&lt;r\leqslant D(T)</annotation></semantics></math>, every vertex of <math id="S2.SS5.SSS1.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> has height at least <math id="S2.SS5.SSS1.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>.
If a vertex outside <math id="S2.SS5.SSS1.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> is adjacent to <math id="S2.SS5.SSS1.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>, every member of
<math id="S2.SS5.SSS1.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math> containing that vertex must properly contain <math id="S2.SS5.SSS1.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>;
its height is therefore at most <math id="S2.SS5.SSS1.p5.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D(T)-\mu(T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D(T)-\mu(T)</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence <math id="S2.SS5.SSS1.p5.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> is a component
of <math id="S2.SS5.SSS1.p5.m14" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\{d\geqslant r\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>⩾</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{d\geqslant r\}</annotation></semantics></math>. For each vertex <math id="S2.SS5.SSS1.p5.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S2.SS5.SSS1.p5.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d(x)\geqslant r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⩾</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d(x)\geqslant r</annotation></semantics></math>, the members
of <math id="S2.SS5.SSS1.p5.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math> containing <math id="S2.SS5.SSS1.p5.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> form a chain, and exactly one of their
successive multiplicity intervals contains <math id="S2.SS5.SSS1.p5.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>. These components
therefore cover the superlevel set, proving the bijection.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS5.SSS1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS5.SSS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS5.SSS1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">Summing the positive multiplicities gives the first equality in
(<a href="#S2.E11" title="Equation 11 ‣ Proposition 2.9 ([, Theorem 5.1]) ‣ 2.5.1. Tubings and height functions ‣ 2.5. Face enumerations of graph associahedra ‣ 2. Polynomial invariants and multichain counts ‣ Chow and chain polynomials of matroids are not log-concave" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>). Nested families split independently over
components of <math id="S2.SS5.SSS1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math>. For connected <math id="S2.SS5.SSS1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>, such a family is a tubing,
possibly with <math id="S2.SS5.SSS1.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V(G)</annotation></semantics></math> adjoined. Setting <math id="S2.SS5.SSS1.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y=t/(1-t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y=t/(1-t)</annotation></semantics></math> therefore gives
<math id="S2.SS5.SSS1.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi_{G}(t)=(1+y)\sum_{\mathcal{T}}y^{|\mathcal{T}|}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi></msub><msup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi_{G}(t)=(1+y)\sum_{\mathcal{T}}y^{|\mathcal{T}|}</annotation></semantics></math>; now apply
(<a href="#S2.E10" title="Equation 10 ‣ 2.5.1. Tubings and height functions ‣ 2.5. Face enumerations of graph associahedra ‣ 2. Polynomial invariants and multichain counts ‣ Chow and chain polynomials of matroids are not log-concave" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>).
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS5.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">2.5.2. </span>Rooted chains and products under gluing</h4>

<div id="S2.SS5.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS5.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Fix a connected rooted graph <math id="S2.SS5.SSS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(G,v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(G,v)</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S2.SS5.SSS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math> vertices. Let
<math id="S2.SS5.SSS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}_{v}(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}_{v}(G)</annotation></semantics></math> be the poset, ordered by inclusion, of connected vertex
sets containing <math id="S2.SS5.SSS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math>. For <math id="S2.SS5.SSS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>, put</p>
<table id="S2.Ex81" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex81.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{G}(j)=\#\{\{v\}=S_{0}\subseteq S_{1}\subseteq\cdots\subseteq S_{j}=V(G):S_{i}\in\mathcal{F}_{v}(G)\}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>⊆</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.1389em">⊆</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.1389em">⋯</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em">⊆</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{G}(j)=\#\{\{v\}=S_{0}\subseteq S_{1}\subseteq\cdots\subseteq S_{j}=V(G):S_{i}\in\mathcal{F}_{v}(G)\},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS5.SSS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">which is the zeta polynomial <math id="S2.SS5.SSS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{\mathcal{F}_{v}(G)}(j)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{\mathcal{F}_{v}(G)}(j)</annotation></semantics></math>. In particular, we have</p>
<table id="S2.Ex82" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex82.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{G}(0)=0,\qquad Z_{G}(1)=1,\qquad Z_{G}(2)=|\mathcal{F}_{v}(G)|." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{G}(0)=0,\qquad Z_{G}(1)=1,\qquad Z_{G}(2)=|\mathcal{F}_{v}(G)|.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS5.SSS2.p1.3" class="ltx_p">For <math id="S2.SS5.SSS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>, a multichain counted by <math id="S2.SS5.SSS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{G}(j)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{G}(j)</annotation></semantics></math> has <math id="S2.SS5.SSS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> steps and
<math id="S2.SS5.SSS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j-1</annotation></semantics></math> unrestricted intermediate sets.
Throughout, a <em id="S2.SS5.SSS2.p1.3.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">rooted zeta witness at <math id="S2.SS5.SSS2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math></em> means</p>
<table id="S2.E12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(12)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{G}(k)^{2}&lt;Z_{G}(k-1)Z_{G}(k+1)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{G}(k)^{2}&lt;Z_{G}(k-1)Z_{G}(k+1).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS5.SSS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS5.SSS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For <math id="S2.SS5.SSS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>, appending <math id="S2.SS5.SSS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V(G)</annotation></semantics></math> to the list of intermediate sets
injects the multichains counted by <math id="S2.SS5.SSS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{G}(j)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{G}(j)</annotation></semantics></math> into those counted by
<math id="S2.SS5.SSS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{G}(j+1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{G}(j+1)</annotation></semantics></math>. Its image misses the chain whose intermediate sets are
all <math id="S2.SS5.SSS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{v\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{v\}</annotation></semantics></math>. Together with <math id="S2.SS5.SSS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{G}(0)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{G}(0)=0</annotation></semantics></math>, this proves strict increase.
Moreover,</p>
<table id="S2.Ex83" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex83.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{G}(3)\leq|\mathcal{F}_{v}(G)|^{2}=Z_{G}(2)^{2}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{G}(3)\leq|\mathcal{F}_{v}(G)|^{2}=Z_{G}(2)^{2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS5.SSS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">Thus the log-concavity inequalities at indices <math id="S2.SS5.SSS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS5.SSS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> hold,
and a rooted zeta witness necessarily has <math id="S2.SS5.SSS2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\geq 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\geq 3</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS5.SSS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS5.SSS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">If <math id="S2.SS5.SSS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{1},\ldots,G_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{1},\ldots,G_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> are rooted graphs, the <em id="S2.SS5.SSS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">wedge</em> <math id="S2.SS5.SSS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{1}\vee\cdots\vee G_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∨</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>∨</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{1}\vee\cdots\vee G_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> of rooted graphs is obtained
by gluing the graphs <math id="S2.SS5.SSS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{1},\ldots,G_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{1},\ldots,G_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> along their respective roots. Write <math id="S2.SS5.SSS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{q}(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{q}(G)</annotation></semantics></math> for the wedge
of <math id="S2.SS5.SSS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> copies of <math id="S2.SS5.SSS2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>; it has <math id="S2.SS5.SSS2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1+q(n-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1+q(n-1)</annotation></semantics></math> vertices. We have the following simple lemma.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm10" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmthm10.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 2.10</span></span><span id="S2.Thmthm10.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(product under wedges)</h6>
<div id="S2.Thmthm10.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm10.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmthm10.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Restriction to the copies induces a poset isomorphism</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex84" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex84.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}_{v}(G_{1}\vee\cdots\vee G_{s})\cong\prod_{i=1}^{s}\mathcal{F}_{v_{i}}(G_{i})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∨</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>∨</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">≅</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∏</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>s</mi></munderover><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}_{v}(G_{1}\vee\cdots\vee G_{s})\cong\prod_{i=1}^{s}\mathcal{F}_{v_{i}}(G_{i}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.Thmthm10.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmthm10.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">In particular, <math id="S2.Thmthm10.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{W_{q}(G)}(j)=Z_{G}(j)^{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>q</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{W_{q}(G)}(j)=Z_{G}(j)^{q}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS5.SSS2.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S2.SS5.SSS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS5.SSS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS5.SSS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">A vertex set containing the common root is connected exactly when
its intersection with every copy is connected. The intersections
recover the set by taking their union, and both operations preserve
inclusion. A multichain in a product is a tuple of multichains.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS5.SSS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS5.SSS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Our next task is to relate the coefficients of the <math id="S2.SS5.SSS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math>-polynomial <math id="S2.SS5.SSS2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{G}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{G}(t)</annotation></semantics></math> to the multichain counts <math id="S2.SS5.SSS2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{G}(i)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{G}(i)</annotation></semantics></math>. This will be achieved in the next section.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS5.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">2.5.3. </span>The wedge formula</h4>

<div id="S2.SS5.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS5.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S2.SS5.SSS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{G}=|\mathcal{B}(G-v)|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{G}=|\mathcal{B}(G-v)|</annotation></semantics></math>, and for <math id="S2.SS5.SSS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math> define</p>
<table id="S2.Ex85" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex85.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\Psi_{a}(t)=\sum_{d:\,d(v)=a}t^{w_{G}(d)-a},\qquad A_{a}(t)=(1-t)^{n-1}\Psi_{a}(t)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.448em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></mrow></munder><msup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></msup><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi_{a}(t)=\sum_{d:\,d(v)=a}t^{w_{G}(d)-a},\qquad A_{a}(t)=(1-t)^{n-1}\Psi_{a}(t).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS5.SSS3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">At levels <math id="S2.SS5.SSS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1,\dots,a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1,\dots,a</annotation></semantics></math> there is a component containing <math id="S2.SS5.SSS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math>, so every
exponent in <math id="S2.SS5.SSS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Psi_{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi_{a}</annotation></semantics></math> is nonnegative. For a vertex set <math id="S2.SS5.SSS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>, write
<math id="S2.SS5.SSS3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N[S]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N[S]</annotation></semantics></math> for its closed neighbourhood in <math id="S2.SS5.SSS3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>. Inequalities between
series below mean coefficientwise inequalities.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm11" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmthm11.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 2.11</span></span><span id="S2.Thmthm11.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(rooted layers)</h6>
<div id="S2.Thmthm11.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm11.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmthm11.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For each chain <math id="S2.Thmthm11.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}:V(G)=S_{0}\supseteq S_{1}\supseteq\cdots\supseteq S_{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>⊇</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.1389em">⊇</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.1389em">⋯</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em">⊇</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>a</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}:V(G)=S_{0}\supseteq S_{1}\supseteq\cdots\supseteq S_{a}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S2.Thmthm11.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}_{v}(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}_{v}(G)</annotation></semantics></math>, put</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex86" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex86.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{\ell}=S_{\ell}\setminus N[S_{\ell+1}]\quad(0\leq\ell&lt;a),\qquad U_{a}=S_{a}\setminus\{v\},\qquad U(\mathcal{C})=\bigcup_{\ell=0}^{a}U_{\ell}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo>∖</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo>∖</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">⋃</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi>a</mi></munderover><msub><mi>U</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{\ell}=S_{\ell}\setminus N[S_{\ell+1}]\quad(0\leq\ell&lt;a),\qquad U_{a}=S_{a}\setminus\{v\},\qquad U(\mathcal{C})=\bigcup_{\ell=0}^{a}U_{\ell}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.Thmthm11.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmthm11.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Then</span></p>
<table id="S2.E13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(13)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Psi_{a}(t)=\sum_{\mathcal{C}}\Phi_{G[U(\mathcal{C})]}(t),\qquad\Psi_{a}(0)=Z_{G}(a+1),\qquad\Psi_{a}(t)\leqslant Z_{G}(a+1)(1-t)^{-t_{G}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi></munder><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⩽</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>G</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi_{a}(t)=\sum_{\mathcal{C}}\Phi_{G[U(\mathcal{C})]}(t),\qquad\Psi_{a}(0)=Z_{G}(a+1),\qquad\Psi_{a}(t)\leqslant Z_{G}(a+1)(1-t)^{-t_{G}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.Thmthm11.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmthm11.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Moreover <math id="S2.Thmthm11.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{a}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{a}(t)</annotation></semantics></math> is a polynomial of degree at most <math id="S2.Thmthm11.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n-1</annotation></semantics></math>, with
constant term <math id="S2.Thmthm11.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{G}(a+1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{G}(a+1)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS5.SSS3.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S2.SS5.SSS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS5.SSS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS5.SSS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">Use the nested family with multiplicities associated to a height
function by Proposition <a href="#S2.Thmthm9" title="Proposition 2.9 ([, Theorem 5.1]) ‣ 2.5.1. Tubings and height functions ‣ 2.5. Face enumerations of graph associahedra ‣ 2. Polynomial invariants and multichain counts ‣ Chow and chain polynomials of matroids are not log-concave" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.9</span></a>. Its members containing <math id="S2.SS5.SSS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math>
form a chain, and their multiplicities sum to <math id="S2.SS5.SSS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d(v)=a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d(v)=a</annotation></semantics></math>. Listing these
members in decreasing order, each repeated with its multiplicity,
gives exactly <math id="S2.SS5.SSS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{1},\dots,S_{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>a</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{1},\dots,S_{a}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS5.SSS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS5.SSS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS5.SSS3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">A connected set <math id="S2.SS5.SSS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> avoiding <math id="S2.SS5.SSS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> is compatible with this chain
exactly when it lies in one of the sets <math id="S2.SS5.SSS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>U</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{\ell}</annotation></semantics></math>. Indeed, take the
largest <math id="S2.SS5.SSS3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math> for which <math id="S2.SS5.SSS3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\subseteq S_{\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⊆</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\subseteq S_{\ell}</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="S2.SS5.SSS3.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell&lt;a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell&lt;a</annotation></semantics></math>,
compatibility forces <math id="S2.SS5.SSS3.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> to be disjoint from and nonadjacent to
<math id="S2.SS5.SSS3.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{\ell+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{\ell+1}</annotation></semantics></math>, since <math id="S2.SS5.SSS3.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> cannot contain the root-containing set
<math id="S2.SS5.SSS3.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{\ell+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{\ell+1}</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="S2.SS5.SSS3.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell=a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell=a</annotation></semantics></math>, it lies in <math id="S2.SS5.SSS3.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{a}\setminus\{v\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo>∖</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{a}\setminus\{v\}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The converse follows immediately from these same conditions.
The <math id="S2.SS5.SSS3.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>U</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{\ell}</annotation></semantics></math> are pairwise disjoint and nonadjacent: for
<math id="S2.SS5.SSS3.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell&lt;\ell^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell&lt;\ell^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, the set <math id="S2.SS5.SSS3.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{\ell^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>U</mi><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{\ell^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math> lies in <math id="S2.SS5.SSS3.p3.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{\ell+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{\ell+1}</annotation></semantics></math>, which has no
vertex or neighbour in <math id="S2.SS5.SSS3.p3.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>U</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{\ell}</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus all the remaining weighted
nested families are precisely those of <math id="S2.SS5.SSS3.p3.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G[U(\mathcal{C})]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G[U(\mathcal{C})]</annotation></semantics></math>.
Their weights contribute <math id="S2.SS5.SSS3.p3.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{G}(d)-a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{G}(d)-a</annotation></semantics></math>, proving the series identity.
Reversing <math id="S2.SS5.SSS3.p3.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{1},\ldots,S_{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>a</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{1},\ldots,S_{a}</annotation></semantics></math> and adjoining the bottom <math id="S2.SS5.SSS3.p3.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{v\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{v\}</annotation></semantics></math> and
top <math id="S2.SS5.SSS3.p3.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V(G)</annotation></semantics></math> gives a multichain with <math id="S2.SS5.SSS3.p3.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a+1</annotation></semantics></math> steps. Thus there are
<math id="S2.SS5.SSS3.p3.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{G}(a+1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{G}(a+1)</annotation></semantics></math> chains, and each summand has constant term <math id="S2.SS5.SSS3.p3.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS5.SSS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS5.SSS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS5.SSS3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">Every connected set of <math id="S2.SS5.SSS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G[U(\mathcal{C})]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G[U(\mathcal{C})]</annotation></semantics></math> belongs to <math id="S2.SS5.SSS3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{B}(G-v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{B}(G-v)</annotation></semantics></math>.
Forgetting the nestedness restriction bounds its weighted nested
families by all multisets from this set of <math id="S2.SS5.SSS3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{G}</annotation></semantics></math> possibilities.
Their generating series is <math id="S2.SS5.SSS3.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1-t)^{-t_{G}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>G</mi></msub></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1-t)^{-t_{G}}</annotation></semantics></math>, proving the bound.
Finally, for <math id="S2.SS5.SSS3.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U=U(\mathcal{C})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U=U(\mathcal{C})</annotation></semantics></math>, Proposition <a href="#S2.Thmthm9" title="Proposition 2.9 ([, Theorem 5.1]) ‣ 2.5.1. Tubings and height functions ‣ 2.5. Face enumerations of graph associahedra ‣ 2. Polynomial invariants and multichain counts ‣ Chow and chain polynomials of matroids are not log-concave" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.9</span></a> gives</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex87" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex87.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1-t)^{n-1}\Phi_{G[U]}(t)=(1-t)^{n-1-|U|}\prod_{C}h_{C}(t)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∏</mo><mi>C</mi></munder><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1-t)^{n-1}\Phi_{G[U]}(t)=(1-t)^{n-1-|U|}\prod_{C}h_{C}(t).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS5.SSS3.p4.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS5.SSS3.p4.2.1" class="ltx_text">Since <math id="S2.SS5.SSS3.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U\subseteq V(G)\setminus\{v\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∖</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U\subseteq V(G)\setminus\{v\}</annotation></semantics></math>, this is a polynomial, of degree
at most</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex88" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex88.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n-1-|U|+\sum_{C}(|C|-1)\leqslant n-1." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">+</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mi>C</mi></munder><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>⩽</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n-1-|U|+\sum_{C}(|C|-1)\leqslant n-1.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS5.SSS3.p4.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS5.SSS3.p4.3.1" class="ltx_text">∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm12" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmthm12.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 2.12</span></span><span id="S2.Thmthm12.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(wedge formula)</h6>
<div id="S2.Thmthm12.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm12.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmthm12.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For every integer <math id="S2.Thmthm12.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="S2.E14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(14)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{W_{q}(G)}(t)=(1-t)^{1+q(n-1)}\sum_{a\geq 0}t^{a}\Psi_{a}(t)^{q}=(1-t)\sum_{a\geq 0}t^{a}A_{a}(t)^{q}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></munder><mrow><msup><mi>t</mi><mi>a</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>q</mi></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></munder><mrow><msup><mi>t</mi><mi>a</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>q</mi></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{W_{q}(G)}(t)=(1-t)^{1+q(n-1)}\sum_{a\geq 0}t^{a}\Psi_{a}(t)^{q}=(1-t)\sum_{a\geq 0}t^{a}A_{a}(t)^{q}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.Thmthm12.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmthm12.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">These are identities of formal power series, whose left side is a
polynomial.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS5.SSS3.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S2.SS5.SSS3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS5.SSS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS5.SSS3.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">Fix a height function on the wedge with common root height <math id="S2.SS5.SSS3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math>, and
let <math id="S2.SS5.SSS3.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> be its restriction to copy <math id="S2.SS5.SSS3.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>. At each of the first <math id="S2.SS5.SSS3.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math>
levels, the <math id="S2.SS5.SSS3.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> components containing the root merge into one.
All other components remain separate. Hence</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex89" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex89.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{W_{q}(G)}(d)=\sum_{i=1}^{q}w_{G}(d_{i})-(q-1)a." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>q</mi></munderover><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{W_{q}(G)}(d)=\sum_{i=1}^{q}w_{G}(d_{i})-(q-1)a.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS5.SSS3.p5.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS5.SSS3.p5.2.1" class="ltx_text">The <math id="S2.SS5.SSS3.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> are independent once their root heights are fixed, so their
total generating series is <math id="S2.SS5.SSS3.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t^{a}\Psi_{a}(t)^{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>t</mi><mi>a</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>q</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t^{a}\Psi_{a}(t)^{q}</annotation></semantics></math>. Sum over <math id="S2.SS5.SSS3.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> and apply
the height-function formula to the wedge, which has <math id="S2.SS5.SSS3.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1+q(n-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1+q(n-1)</annotation></semantics></math>
vertices. The definition of <math id="S2.SS5.SSS3.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{a}</annotation></semantics></math> gives the second equality.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS5.SSS3.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS5.SSS3.p6.1" class="ltx_p">In particular, the contribution with <math id="S2.SS5.SSS3.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a=j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a=j</annotation></semantics></math> to the coefficient of
<math id="S2.SS5.SSS3.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t^{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>t</mi><mi>j</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t^{j}</annotation></semantics></math> is exactly <math id="S2.SS5.SSS3.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{G}(j+1)^{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>q</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{G}(j+1)^{q}</annotation></semantics></math>. Contributions with <math id="S2.SS5.SSS3.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a&lt;j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a&lt;j</annotation></semantics></math> involve smaller
numbers of multichains, and no term with <math id="S2.SS5.SSS3.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a&gt;j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a&gt;j</annotation></semantics></math> contributes. The next estimate
makes this separation effective without computing the polynomials
<math id="S2.SS5.SSS3.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{a}</annotation></semantics></math> themselves.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS5.SSS4" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">2.5.4. </span>An explicit amplification certificate</h4>

<div id="S2.SS5.SSS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS5.SSS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For <math id="S2.SS5.SSS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j,q\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j,q\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>, set</p>
<table id="S2.E15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(15)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{j}(q)=\sum_{a=0}^{j-1}\left(\frac{Z_{G}(a+1)}{Z_{G}(j+1)}\right)^{q}\binom{q(n-1+t_{G})+j-a}{j-a},\qquad E_{0}(q)=0." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mrow><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><mi>q</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>G</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{j}(q)=\sum_{a=0}^{j-1}\left(\frac{Z_{G}(a+1)}{Z_{G}(j+1)}\right)^{q}\binom{q(n-1+t_{G})+j-a}{j-a},\qquad E_{0}(q)=0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm13" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmthm13.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 2.13</span></span><span id="S2.Thmthm13.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(coefficient estimate)</h6>
<div id="S2.Thmthm13.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm13.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmthm13.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For every <math id="S2.Thmthm13.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j,q\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j,q\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="S2.E16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(16)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left|\frac{h_{j}(W_{q}(G))}{Z_{G}(j+1)^{q}}-1\right|\leqslant E_{j}(q)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>|</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>q</mi></msup></mrow></mfrac><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>|</mo></mrow><mo>⩽</mo><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left|\frac{h_{j}(W_{q}(G))}{Z_{G}(j+1)^{q}}-1\right|\leqslant E_{j}(q).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.Thmthm13.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmthm13.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For fixed <math id="S2.Thmthm13.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.Thmthm13.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>, this gives</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex90" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex90.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{j}(W_{q}(G))=Z_{G}(j+1)^{q}+O\bigl(q^{j}Z_{G}(j)^{q}\bigr)\quad(q\longrightarrow\infty)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>q</mi></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>q</mi><mi>j</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>q</mi></msup></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟶</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{j}(W_{q}(G))=Z_{G}(j+1)^{q}+O\bigl(q^{j}Z_{G}(j)^{q}\bigr)\quad(q\longrightarrow\infty).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS5.SSS4.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S2.SS5.SSS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS5.SSS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS5.SSS4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">Put <math id="S2.SS5.SSS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D=1+q(n-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D=1+q(n-1)</annotation></semantics></math>. Extracting the coefficient in
(<a href="#S2.E14" title="Equation 14 ‣ Theorem 2.12 (wedge formula) ‣ 2.5.3. The wedge formula ‣ 2.5. Face enumerations of graph associahedra ‣ 2. Polynomial invariants and multichain counts ‣ Chow and chain polynomials of matroids are not log-concave" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14</span></a>) yields</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex91" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex91.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{j}(W_{q}(G))=\sum_{a=0}^{j}[t^{j-a}](1-t)^{D}\Psi_{a}(t)^{q}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi>j</mi></munderover><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>D</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>q</mi></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{j}(W_{q}(G))=\sum_{a=0}^{j}[t^{j-a}](1-t)^{D}\Psi_{a}(t)^{q}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS5.SSS4.p2.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS5.SSS4.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text">The term <math id="S2.SS5.SSS4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a=j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a=j</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S2.SS5.SSS4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{G}(j+1)^{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>q</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{G}(j+1)^{q}</annotation></semantics></math>. For <math id="S2.SS5.SSS4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m=j-a\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m=j-a\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>, the absolute
coefficients of <math id="S2.SS5.SSS4.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1-t)^{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>D</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1-t)^{D}</annotation></semantics></math> are those of <math id="S2.SS5.SSS4.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1+t)^{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>D</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1+t)^{D}</annotation></semantics></math>, and
<math id="S2.SS5.SSS4.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1+t)^{D}\leq(1-t)^{-D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>D</mi></msup><mo>≤</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1+t)^{D}\leq(1-t)^{-D}</annotation></semantics></math> coefficientwise. Since <math id="S2.SS5.SSS4.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Psi_{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi_{a}</annotation></semantics></math> has
nonnegative coefficients, Lemma <a href="#S2.Thmthm11" title="Lemma 2.11 (rooted layers) ‣ 2.5.3. The wedge formula ‣ 2.5. Face enumerations of graph associahedra ‣ 2. Polynomial invariants and multichain counts ‣ Chow and chain polynomials of matroids are not log-concave" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.11</span></a> gives</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex92" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.Ex92X"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex92X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\left|[t^{m}](1-t)^{D}\Psi_{a}(t)^{q}\right|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>|</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mi>m</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>D</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>q</mi></msup></mrow><mo>|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\left|[t^{m}](1-t)^{D}\Psi_{a}(t)^{q}\right|</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex92X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\leqslant Z_{G}(a+1)^{q}[t^{m}](1-t)^{-D-qt_{G}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>⩽</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>q</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mi>m</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>G</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\leqslant Z_{G}(a+1)^{q}[t^{m}](1-t)^{-D-qt_{G}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S2.Ex92Xa"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex92Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=Z_{G}(a+1)^{q}\binom{q(n-1+t_{G})+m}{m}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>q</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>G</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mi>m</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=Z_{G}(a+1)^{q}\binom{q(n-1+t_{G})+m}{m}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS5.SSS4.p2.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS5.SSS4.p2.3.1" class="ltx_text">Summing and dividing by <math id="S2.SS5.SSS4.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{G}(j+1)^{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>q</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{G}(j+1)^{q}</annotation></semantics></math> proves the bound. For fixed <math id="S2.SS5.SSS4.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>,
each binomial factor is a polynomial in <math id="S2.SS5.SSS4.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> of degree at most <math id="S2.SS5.SSS4.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>,
and <math id="S2.SS5.SSS4.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{G}(a+1)\leqslant Z_{G}(j)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⩽</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{G}(a+1)\leqslant Z_{G}(j)</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S2.SS5.SSS4.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a&lt;j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a&lt;j</annotation></semantics></math>, proving the asymptotic statement.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm14" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmthm14.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 2.14</span></span><span id="S2.Thmthm14.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(graph zeta amplification)</h6>
<div id="S2.Thmthm14.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm14.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmthm14.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Suppose <math id="S2.Thmthm14.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(G,v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(G,v)</annotation></semantics></math> is a rooted zeta witness at <math id="S2.Thmthm14.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>, and put</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex93" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex93.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho=\frac{Z_{G}(k)^{2}}{Z_{G}(k-1)Z_{G}(k+1)}&lt;1." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho=\frac{Z_{G}(k)^{2}}{Z_{G}(k-1)Z_{G}(k+1)}&lt;1.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.Thmthm14.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmthm14.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If an integer <math id="S2.Thmthm14.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math> satisfies <math id="S2.Thmthm14.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{k-2}(q)&lt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{k-2}(q)&lt;1</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.Thmthm14.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{k}(q)&lt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{k}(q)&lt;1</annotation></semantics></math>, and</span></p>
<table id="S2.E17" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(17)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho^{q}\frac{(1+E_{k-1}(q))^{2}}{(1-E_{k-2}(q))(1-E_{k}(q))}&lt;1," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>ρ</mi><mi>q</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho^{q}\frac{(1+E_{k-1}(q))^{2}}{(1-E_{k-2}(q))(1-E_{k}(q))}&lt;1,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.Thmthm14.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmthm14.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">then</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex94" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex94.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{k-1}(W_{q}(G))^{2}&lt;h_{k-2}(W_{q}(G))h_{k}(W_{q}(G))." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{k-1}(W_{q}(G))^{2}&lt;h_{k-2}(W_{q}(G))h_{k}(W_{q}(G)).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.Thmthm14.p1.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmthm14.p1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">All these conditions hold for every sufficiently large <math id="S2.Thmthm14.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>.
As a consequence, the <math id="S2.Thmthm14.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math>-polynomials of these wedges are not
log-concave, and hence not real-rooted.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS5.SSS4.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S2.SS5.SSS4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS5.SSS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS5.SSS4.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">The coefficient estimate places <math id="S2.SS5.SSS4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{j}(W_{q}(G))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{j}(W_{q}(G))</annotation></semantics></math> between
<math id="S2.SS5.SSS4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{G}(j+1)^{q}(1-E_{j}(q))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>q</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{G}(j+1)^{q}(1-E_{j}(q))</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS5.SSS4.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{G}(j+1)^{q}(1+E_{j}(q))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>q</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{G}(j+1)^{q}(1+E_{j}(q))</annotation></semantics></math>. The assumed positive
lower bounds at <math id="S2.SS5.SSS4.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k-2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k-2</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS5.SSS4.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>, together with nonnegativity at <math id="S2.SS5.SSS4.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k-1</annotation></semantics></math>,
therefore give</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex95" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex95.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{h_{k-1}(W_{q}(G))^{2}}{h_{k-2}(W_{q}(G))h_{k}(W_{q}(G))}\leq\rho^{q}\frac{(1+E_{k-1}(q))^{2}}{(1-E_{k-2}(q))(1-E_{k}(q))}&lt;1." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ρ</mi><mi>q</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{h_{k-1}(W_{q}(G))^{2}}{h_{k-2}(W_{q}(G))h_{k}(W_{q}(G))}\leq\rho^{q}\frac{(1+E_{k-1}(q))^{2}}{(1-E_{k-2}(q))(1-E_{k}(q))}&lt;1.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS5.SSS4.p3.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS5.SSS4.p3.2.1" class="ltx_text">Strict increase of <math id="S2.SS5.SSS4.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{G}</annotation></semantics></math> implies <math id="S2.SS5.SSS4.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{G}(a+1)/Z_{G}(j+1)&lt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>G</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{G}(a+1)/Z_{G}(j+1)&lt;1</annotation></semantics></math> whenever <math id="S2.SS5.SSS4.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a&lt;j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a&lt;j</annotation></semantics></math>.
Each summand of <math id="S2.SS5.SSS4.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{j}(q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{j}(q)</annotation></semantics></math> is thus a fixed polynomial in <math id="S2.SS5.SSS4.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> times a
decaying exponential, so <math id="S2.SS5.SSS4.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{j}(q)\to 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{j}(q)\to 0</annotation></semantics></math>. Also <math id="S2.SS5.SSS4.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho^{q}\to 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ρ</mi><mi>q</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho^{q}\to 0</annotation></semantics></math>, proving
eventual validity of the certificate. Newton’s inequalities give the
failure of real-rootedness.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS5.SSS4.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS5.SSS4.p4.1" class="ltx_p">In Section <a href="#S5" title="5. ℎ-polynomials of graph associahedra are not log-concave ‣ Chow and chain polynomials of matroids are not log-concave" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> we will construct a rooted graph <math id="S2.SS5.SSS4.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> which satisfies the hypothesis above. The above theorems will allow us to produce an example of a wedge of <math id="S2.SS5.SSS4.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> having a non log-concave <math id="S2.SS5.SSS4.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math>-polynomial. For this, it will be enough to know the multichain counts <math id="S2.SS5.SSS4.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{G}(1),\dots,Z_{G}(k+1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{G}(1),\dots,Z_{G}(k+1)</annotation></semantics></math>, the number
<math id="S2.SS5.SSS4.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{G}</annotation></semantics></math> of connected sets avoiding the root, and an integer <math id="S2.SS5.SSS4.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> passing
(<a href="#S2.E17" title="Equation 17 ‣ Theorem 2.14 (graph zeta amplification) ‣ 2.5.4. An explicit amplification certificate ‣ 2.5. Face enumerations of graph associahedra ‣ 2. Polynomial invariants and multichain counts ‣ Chow and chain polynomials of matroids are not log-concave" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">17</span></a>). No enumeration of all tubings of the much
larger graph <math id="S2.SS5.SSS4.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{q}(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{q}(G)</annotation></semantics></math> is needed.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS6" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.6. </span>Log-concavity and real-rootedness</h3>

<div id="S2.SS6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We briefly recall relevant properties of polynomials, and refer the reader to Brändén’s survey <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite>. A polynomial <math id="S2.SS6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{p(t)=\sum_{i=0}^{d}a_{i}t^{i}\in\mathbb{R}_{\geqslant 0}[t]}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi>d</mi></msubsup><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>⩾</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{p(t)=\sum_{i=0}^{d}a_{i}t^{i}\in\mathbb{R}_{\geqslant 0}[t]}</annotation></semantics></math> is <em id="S2.SS6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">log-concave</em>, if
<math id="S2.SS6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{i}^{2}\geqslant a_{i-1}a_{i+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>⩾</mo><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{i}^{2}\geqslant a_{i-1}a_{i+1}</annotation></semantics></math> for every <math id="S2.SS6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>.
For every <math id="S2.SS6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>, the <em id="S2.SS6.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">defect at index <math id="S2.SS6.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math></em> is defined to be <math id="S2.SS6.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{i}^{2}-a_{i-1}a_{i+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{i}^{2}-a_{i-1}a_{i+1}</annotation></semantics></math>.
If <math id="S2.SS6.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(t)</annotation></semantics></math> only has real zeros, the well-known result of Newton
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a>, pp. 242–245]</cite> implies <em id="S2.SS6.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">ultra log-concavity</em> of the coefficients of <math id="S2.SS6.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(t)</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S2.Ex96" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex96.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{a_{i}^{2}}{{\binom{d}{i}}^{2}}\geqslant\frac{a_{i-1}}{\binom{d}{i-1}}\frac{a_{i+1}}{\binom{d}{i+1}},\quad\text{ for every }0&lt;i&lt;d," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mfrac><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac><mo>⩾</mo><mrow><mfrac><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mtext> for every </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{a_{i}^{2}}{{\binom{d}{i}}^{2}}\geqslant\frac{a_{i-1}}{\binom{d}{i-1}}\frac{a_{i+1}}{\binom{d}{i+1}},\quad\text{ for every }0&lt;i&lt;d,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS6.p1.2" class="ltx_p">which implies log-concavity with no internal zeros.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS6.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS6.p2.2" class="ltx_p">If a polynomial <math id="S2.SS6.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{p(t)=\sum_{i=0}^{d}a_{i}t^{i}\in\mathbb{R}_{\geqslant 0}[t]}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi>d</mi></msubsup><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>⩾</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{p(t)=\sum_{i=0}^{d}a_{i}t^{i}\in\mathbb{R}_{\geqslant 0}[t]}</annotation></semantics></math> is palindromic, it admits a unique expansion into the basis <math id="S2.SS6.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{t^{i}(1+t)^{d-2i}\}_{i\geqslant 0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msup><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>⩾</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{t^{i}(1+t)^{d-2i}\}_{i\geqslant 0}</annotation></semantics></math>, and has <math id="S2.SS6.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math>-polynomial</p>
<table id="S2.Ex97" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex97.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(t)=\sum_{i}\gamma_{i}\&gt;t^{i}(1+t)^{d-{2i}},\quad\gamma(p(t);x)\coloneqq\sum_{i=0}^{\lfloor d/2\rfloor}\gamma_{i}x^{i}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mi>i</mi></munder><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">≔</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌋</mo></mrow></munderover><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(t)=\sum_{i}\gamma_{i}\&gt;t^{i}(1+t)^{d-{2i}},\quad\gamma(p(t);x)\coloneqq\sum_{i=0}^{\lfloor d/2\rfloor}\gamma_{i}x^{i}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS6.p2.3" class="ltx_p">Since the Chow ring of a matroid is a Poincaré duality algebra, the Chow polynomial is palindromic. By <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">44</a>, <a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite>, its <math id="S2.SS6.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math>-coefficients can be written using the flag <math id="S2.SS6.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math>-vector as follows:</p>
<table id="S2.E18" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(18)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{\textrm{H}}(\textrm{M};t)=\sum_{i}\gamma_{i}t^{i}(1+t)^{r-1-2i},\quad\gamma_{i}=\sum_{\begin{subarray}{c}S\subseteq[r-1]\text{ stable}\\
1\notin S,\ |S|=i\end{subarray}}\beta_{\textrm{M}}(S)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mtext>H</mtext><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext>M</mtext><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mi>i</mi></munder><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> stable</mtext></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>∉</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.577em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></munder><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mtext>M</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{\textrm{H}}(\textrm{M};t)=\sum_{i}\gamma_{i}t^{i}(1+t)^{r-1-2i},\quad\gamma_{i}=\sum_{\begin{subarray}{c}S\subseteq[r-1]\text{ stable}\\
1\notin S,\ |S|=i\end{subarray}}\beta_{\textrm{M}}(S).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS6.p2.4" class="ltx_p">We will be particularly interested in Chow polynomials of degree <math id="S2.SS6.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>6</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">6</annotation></semantics></math>.
For any matroid of rank <math id="S2.SS6.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>7</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">7</annotation></semantics></math>, the only stable subset of <math id="S2.SS6.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{2,\ldots,6\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>6</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{2,\ldots,6\}</annotation></semantics></math> with size <math id="S2.SS6.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S2.SS6.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{2,4,6\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>6</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{2,4,6\}</annotation></semantics></math>. Therefore,</p>
<table id="S2.Ex98" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex98.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{0}=1,\qquad\gamma_{3}=\beta_{\textrm{M}}(246)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mtext>M</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>246</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{0}=1,\qquad\gamma_{3}=\beta_{\textrm{M}}(246),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS6.p2.5" class="ltx_p">and the coefficients of the Chow polynomial satisfy</p>
<table id="S2.E19" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S2.E19X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="4" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_left">(19)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E19X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\underline{\textrm{H}}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><munder accentunder="true"><mtext>H</mtext><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\underline{\textrm{H}}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E19X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\underline{\textrm{H}}_{6}=1," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><msub><munder accentunder="true"><mtext>H</mtext><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mn>6</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\underline{\textrm{H}}_{6}=1,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr>
<tr id="S2.E19Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E19Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\underline{\textrm{H}}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><munder accentunder="true"><mtext>H</mtext><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\underline{\textrm{H}}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E19Xa.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\underline{\textrm{H}}_{5}=\gamma_{1}+\binom{6}{1}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><msub><munder accentunder="true"><mtext>H</mtext><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mn>5</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mn>6</mn><mn>1</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\underline{\textrm{H}}_{5}=\gamma_{1}+\binom{6}{1},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr>
<tr id="S2.E19Xb" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E19Xb.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\underline{\textrm{H}}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><munder accentunder="true"><mtext>H</mtext><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\underline{\textrm{H}}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E19Xb.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\underline{\textrm{H}}_{4}=\gamma_{2}+\binom{4}{1}\gamma_{1}+\binom{6}{2}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><msub><munder accentunder="true"><mtext>H</mtext><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mn>4</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mn>4</mn><mn>1</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mn>6</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\underline{\textrm{H}}_{4}=\gamma_{2}+\binom{4}{1}\gamma_{1}+\binom{6}{2},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr>
<tr id="S2.E19Xc" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E19Xc.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\underline{\textrm{H}}_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><munder accentunder="true"><mtext>H</mtext><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mn>3</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\underline{\textrm{H}}_{3}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E19Xc.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\gamma_{3}+\binom{2}{1}\gamma_{2}+\binom{4}{2}\gamma_{1}+\binom{6}{3}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mn>2</mn><mn>1</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mn>4</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mn>6</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\gamma_{3}+\binom{2}{1}\gamma_{2}+\binom{4}{2}\gamma_{1}+\binom{6}{3}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm15" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmthm15.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 2.15</span></span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmthm15.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm15.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmthm15.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <span id="S2.Thmthm15.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_upright">M</span> be a loopless matroid of rank <math id="S2.Thmthm15.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>7</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">7</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
<ol id="S2.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S2.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S2.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{\textrm{H}}(\textrm{M};t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mtext>H</mtext><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext>M</mtext><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{\textrm{H}}(\textrm{M};t)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is log-concave if and only if</span></p>
<table id="S2.E20" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(20)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{\underline{\textrm{H}}}:=\underline{\textrm{H}}_{2}^{2}-\underline{\textrm{H}}_{1}\underline{\textrm{H}}_{3}\geqslant 0." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><munder accentunder="true"><mtext>H</mtext><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msubsup><munder accentunder="true"><mtext>H</mtext><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><munder accentunder="true"><mtext>H</mtext><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><munder accentunder="true"><mtext>H</mtext><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>⩾</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{\underline{\textrm{H}}}:=\underline{\textrm{H}}_{2}^{2}-\underline{\textrm{H}}_{1}\underline{\textrm{H}}_{3}\geqslant 0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{\textrm{H}}(\textrm{M};t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mtext>H</mtext><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext>M</mtext><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{\textrm{H}}(\textrm{M};t)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is real-rooted
if and only if the discriminant for the cubic polynomial </span><math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma(\underline{\textrm{H}}(t);x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mtext>H</mtext><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma(\underline{\textrm{H}}(t);x)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is nonnegative:</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex99" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex99.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{disc}(\gamma(\underline{\textrm{H}}(t);x))=\gamma_{1}^{2}\gamma_{2}^{2}-4\gamma_{1}^{3}\gamma_{3}-4\gamma_{2}^{3}+18\gamma_{1}\gamma_{2}\gamma_{3}-27\gamma_{3}^{2}\geqslant 0." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>disc</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mtext>H</mtext><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>γ</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>γ</mi><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>γ</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>3</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>γ</mi><mn>2</mn><mn>3</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>18</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>27</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>γ</mi><mn>3</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo>⩾</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{disc}(\gamma(\underline{\textrm{H}}(t);x))=\gamma_{1}^{2}\gamma_{2}^{2}-4\gamma_{1}^{3}\gamma_{3}-4\gamma_{2}^{3}+18\gamma_{1}\gamma_{2}\gamma_{3}-27\gamma_{3}^{2}\geqslant 0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS6.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S2.SS6.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS6.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS6.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">The first statement follows from the definition and palindromicity of <math id="S2.SS6.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{\textrm{H}}(\textrm{M};t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mtext>H</mtext><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext>M</mtext><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{\textrm{H}}(\textrm{M};t)</annotation></semantics></math>.
The second statement follows from the equivalence of real-rootedness of a polynomial and its <math id="S2.SS6.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math>-polynomial. See, for instance, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">39</a>, Observation 4.2]</cite>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3. </span>Matroid operations: hyperplane coalescence, absorption</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We study the properties of two crucial matroid operations, which are essential in our construction: hyperplane coalescence, and a special class of extensions by modular cuts, called <em id="S3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">absorption</em>.
For background on matroids, we refer to <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">37</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We start with the classical theory of detruncation, introduced by Crapo <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite> to construct matroids
with prescribed hyperplanes and a fixed truncation. See also
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">38</a>, Section 3 and Theorem 12]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmthm1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmthm1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 3.1</span></span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmthm1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmthm1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <span id="S3.Thmthm1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">M</span> be a matroid of positive rank, and <math id="S3.Thmthm1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\geqslant 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>⩾</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\geqslant 0</annotation></semantics></math>.
The <math id="S3.Thmthm1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>th truncation <math id="S3.Thmthm1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{T}^{k}(\textrm{M})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext>M</mtext><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{T}^{k}(\textrm{M})</annotation></semantics></math> of <span id="S3.Thmthm1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">M</span> is the matroid with rank <math id="S3.Thmthm1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r-k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r-k</annotation></semantics></math> with lattice of flats</p>
<table id="S3.Ex100" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex100.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}(\mathrm{T}^{k}(\textrm{M}))=\{F\in\mathcal{L}(\textrm{M})\colon\operatorname{rk}_{\textrm{M}}(F)&lt;k\}\cup\{E\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext>M</mtext><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext>M</mtext><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>rk</mi><mtext>M</mtext></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}(\mathrm{T}^{k}(\textrm{M}))=\{F\in\mathcal{L}(\textrm{M})\colon\operatorname{rk}_{\textrm{M}}(F)&lt;k\}\cup\{E\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmthm1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">If <math id="S3.Thmthm1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=1</annotation></semantics></math>, the truncation <math id="S3.Thmthm1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{T}^{k}(\textrm{M})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext>M</mtext><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{T}^{k}(\textrm{M})</annotation></semantics></math> is called the <em id="S3.Thmthm1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">principal truncation</em>, or simply <em id="S3.Thmthm1.p1.2.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">truncation</em>.
A matroid <span id="S3.Thmthm1.p1.2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">M</span> of rank <math id="S3.Thmthm1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> is a <em id="S3.Thmthm1.p1.2.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">detruncation</em> of a
matroid <span id="S3.Thmthm1.p1.2.5" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">T</span> of rank <math id="S3.Thmthm1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r-1</annotation></semantics></math>, if
the principal truncation <math id="S3.Thmthm1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Tr}(\textrm{M})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Tr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext>M</mtext><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Tr}(\textrm{M})</annotation></semantics></math> is <span id="S3.Thmthm1.p1.2.6" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">T</span>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The following properties are standard.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmthm2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmthm2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 3.2</span></span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmthm2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmthm2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmthm2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For a matroid <span id="S3.Thmthm2.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_upright">M</span> of rank <math id="S3.Thmthm2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S3.Thmthm2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.Thmthm2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leqslant k\leqslant r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>⩽</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>⩽</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leqslant k\leqslant r</annotation></semantics></math>, the following hold.</span></p>
<ol id="S3.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S3.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The flats of </span><math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Tr}^{k}(\textrm{M})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>Tr</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext>M</mtext><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Tr}^{k}(\textrm{M})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> are the flats of </span><span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">M</span><span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> of rank at most </span><math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k-1</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, with their rank in </span><span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">M</span><span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, and </span><math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">. In
particular </span><math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{\mathcal{L}(\mathrm{Tr}^{k}(\textrm{M}))}(S)=\alpha_{\mathcal{L}(\textrm{M})}(S)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>Tr</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext>M</mtext><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext>M</mtext><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{\mathcal{L}(\mathrm{Tr}^{k}(\textrm{M}))}(S)=\alpha_{\mathcal{L}(\textrm{M})}(S)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> for every </span><math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S\subseteq[k-1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S\subseteq[k-1]</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> (</span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.11" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">[</span><a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">37</a><span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.12" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, Proposition 7.3.10]</span></cite><span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.13" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">).</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If </span><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">M</span><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is loopless and </span><math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k&lt;r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k&lt;r</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, then </span><math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Tr}^{k}(\textrm{M})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>Tr</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext>M</mtext><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Tr}^{k}(\textrm{M})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is connected (</span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">[</span><a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">37</a><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, Section 7.3, Exercise 8]</span></cite><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">).</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(3)</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If </span><span id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">M</span><span id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is simple and </span><math id="S3.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\geqslant 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>⩾</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\geqslant 2</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, then </span><math id="S3.I1.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Tr}^{k}(\textrm{M})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>Tr</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext>M</mtext><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Tr}^{k}(\textrm{M})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is simple (</span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><span id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">[</span><a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">37</a><span id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, Proposition 7.3.10]</span></cite><span id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">).</span></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1. </span>Hyperplane coalescence</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Hyperplane coalescence is a natural geometric operation on matroids that merges groups of hyperplanes into larger
hyperplanes while preserving the geometry of lower ranks. It is an
application of Crapo’s criterion in the Crapo–Knuth theory of
detruncations <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>, <a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>, <a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">38</a>]</cite>.
The spanning subsets that become new hyperplanes are called
<em id="S3.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">blocks</em>; the lemma below specifies which blocks may be selected.
For a single block with restriction <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textrm{U}_{r,|H|}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext>U</mtext><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textrm{U}_{r,|H|}</annotation></semantics></math>, coalescence
replaces this restriction by <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textrm{U}_{r-1,|H|}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext>U</mtext><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textrm{U}_{r-1,|H|}</annotation></semantics></math>, reversing
stressed-hyperplane relaxation
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>, Theorem 3.4 and Proposition 3.10]</cite>.
When <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|H|=r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|H|=r</annotation></semantics></math>, this is tightening a free basis.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmthm3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmthm3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 3.3</span></span><span id="S3.Thmthm3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Hyperplane coalescence)</h6>
<div id="S3.Thmthm3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmthm3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <span id="S3.Thmthm3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">M</span> be a matroid of rank <math id="S3.Thmthm3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\geqslant 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⩾</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\geqslant 2</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S3.Thmthm3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math>, and let
<math id="S3.Thmthm3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}</annotation></semantics></math> be a family of proper spanning subsets of <math id="S3.Thmthm3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math>.
A matroid <math id="S3.Thmthm3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textrm{M}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mtext>M</mtext><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textrm{M}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> of rank <math id="S3.Thmthm3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S3.Thmthm3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math> is obtained from <span id="S3.Thmthm3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">M</span> via
<em id="S3.Thmthm3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">hyperplane coalescence along <math id="S3.Thmthm3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}</annotation></semantics></math></em> if:</p>
<ol id="S3.I2" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S3.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S3.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I2.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">M</span> and <math id="S3.I2.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textrm{M}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mtext>M</mtext><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textrm{M}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> have the same flats of rank at most
<math id="S3.I2.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r-2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r-2</annotation></semantics></math>, with the same ranks;</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S3.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">the hyperplanes of <math id="S3.I2.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textrm{M}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mtext>M</mtext><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textrm{M}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> are precisely the elements
in <math id="S3.I2.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}</annotation></semantics></math> together with the hyperplanes of <span id="S3.I2.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">M</span> not contained in
any element in <math id="S3.I2.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
<p id="S3.Thmthm3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">In other words, the collection of hyperplanes of <math id="S3.Thmthm3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textrm{M}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mtext>M</mtext><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textrm{M}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is</p>
<table id="S3.Ex101" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex101.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}^{r-1}(\textrm{M}^{\prime})=\mathcal{H}\;\cup\;\bigl\{F\in\mathcal{L}^{r-1}(\textrm{M}):F\nsubseteq H\text{ for every }H\in\mathcal{H}\bigr\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mtext>M</mtext><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo lspace="0.502em" rspace="0.502em">∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext>M</mtext><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⊈</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> for every </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}^{r-1}(\textrm{M}^{\prime})=\mathcal{H}\;\cup\;\bigl\{F\in\mathcal{L}^{r-1}(\textrm{M}):F\nsubseteq H\text{ for every }H\in\mathcal{H}\bigr\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmthm3.p1.3" class="ltx_p">The members of <math id="S3.Thmthm3.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}</annotation></semantics></math> are called <em id="S3.Thmthm3.p1.3.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">blocks</em>. We say that <math id="S3.Thmthm3.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textrm{M}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mtext>M</mtext><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textrm{M}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is obtained by
<em id="S3.Thmthm3.p1.3.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">coalescing the blocks <math id="S3.Thmthm3.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}</annotation></semantics></math> in <span id="S3.Thmthm3.p1.3.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_upright">M</span></em>.
Thus, to <em id="S3.Thmthm3.p1.3.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">coalesce</em> a block <math id="S3.Thmthm3.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> is to replace the
hyperplanes contained in <math id="S3.Thmthm3.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> by the single hyperplane <math id="S3.Thmthm3.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math>.
For each block <math id="S3.Thmthm3.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math>, the hyperplanes in <span id="S3.Thmthm3.p1.3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">M</span> contained in
<math id="S3.Thmthm3.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> are replaced by the single hyperplane <math id="S3.Thmthm3.p1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S3.Thmthm3.p1.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textrm{M}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mtext>M</mtext><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textrm{M}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmthm4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_example">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmthm4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Example 3.4</span></span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmthm4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmthm4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For <math id="S3.Thmthm4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textrm{U}_{3,4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext>U</mtext><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textrm{U}_{3,4}</annotation></semantics></math>, the coalescence along <math id="S3.Thmthm4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}=\{123\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>123</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}=\{123\}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S3.Thmthm4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textrm{U}_{3,4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext>U</mtext><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textrm{U}_{3,4}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S3.Thmthm4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textrm{U}_{2,3}\oplus\textrm{U}_{1,1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mtext>U</mtext><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msub><mo>⊕</mo><msub><mtext>U</mtext><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textrm{U}_{2,3}\oplus\textrm{U}_{1,1}</annotation></semantics></math>. See Figure <a href="#S3.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ Example 3.4 ‣ 3.1. Hyperplane coalescence ‣ 3. Matroid operations: hyperplane coalescence, absorption ‣ Chow and chain polynomials of matroids are not log-concave" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F1" class="ltx_figure"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S3.F1.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="160.11" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 288.67 160.11" width="288.67"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,160.11) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(44.81,0) translate(0,117.52)"><g stroke-width="0.65pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M 66.14 -93.7 L 140.55 -93.7"></path><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 68.47 -93.7 C 68.47 -92.41 67.43 -91.37 66.14 -91.37 C 64.86 -91.37 63.81 -92.41 63.81 -93.7 C 63.81 -94.99 64.86 -96.03 66.14 -96.03 C 67.43 -96.03 68.47 -94.99 68.47 -93.7 Z M 66.14 -93.7"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(0.7 0.0 0.0 0.7 63.9 -87.51)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.5em;--ltx-fo-height:0.63em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:9.25pt;" height="8.03" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 8.03)" width="6.4"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S3.F1.pic1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 93.27 -93.7 C 93.27 -92.41 92.23 -91.37 90.94 -91.37 C 89.66 -91.37 88.62 -92.41 88.62 -93.7 C 88.62 -94.99 89.66 -96.03 90.94 -96.03 C 92.23 -96.03 93.27 -94.99 93.27 -93.7 Z M 90.94 -93.7"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(0.7 0.0 0.0 0.7 88.7 -87.51)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.5em;--ltx-fo-height:0.63em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-s