le="--ltx-fg-color:#808080;" mathcolor="#808080" mathsize="0.900em">t</mi></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#808080;" mathcolor="#808080" mathsize="0.900em">+</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#808080;" mathcolor="#808080" mathsize="0.900em">4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4t+4</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -33.47 80.74)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.89em;--ltx-fo-height:0.42em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.12em;font-size:9.25pt;" height="6.85" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 5.36)" width="11.36"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S5.F5.pic2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">a</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{i}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -32.23 25.62)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.79em;--ltx-fo-height:0.42em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.12em;font-size:9.25pt;" height="6.85" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 5.36)" width="10.12"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S5.F5.pic2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">c</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{i}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -32.23 -4.32)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.79em;--ltx-fo-height:0.79em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.12em;font-size:9.25pt;" height="11.62" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 10.13)" width="10.12"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S5.F5.pic2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{i}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.900em">c</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">i</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{i}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -57.46 96.65)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:2.47em;--ltx-fo-height:0.68em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.19em;font-size:9.25pt;" height="11.07" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 8.65)" width="31.68"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><span id="S5.F5.pic2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">layer <math id="S5.F5.pic2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></span></foreignObject></g><path style="fill:none" d="M 3.25 82.68 L 51.86 82.68 M 58.37 82.68 L 106.98 82.68 M 113.49 82.68 L 162.1 82.68"></path><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#808080;--ltx-fill-color:#808080;--ltx-fg-color:#808080;" color="#808080" fill="#808080" stroke="#808080"><path style="fill:none" d="M 168.61 82.68 L 217.22 82.68"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#808080;--ltx-fill-color:#808080;--ltx-fg-color:#808080;" color="#808080" fill="#808080" stroke="#808080" stroke-dasharray="3.0pt,3.0pt" stroke-dashoffset="0.0pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M -8.82 82.68 L -3.25 82.68"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#808080;--ltx-fill-color:#808080;--ltx-fg-color:#808080;" color="#808080" fill="#808080" stroke="#808080" stroke-dasharray="3.0pt,3.0pt" stroke-dashoffset="0.0pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M 223.72 82.68 L 232.87 82.68"></path></g><path style="fill:none" d="M 3.25 27.56 L 51.86 27.56 M 2.91 1.45 L 52.21 26.1"></path><path style="fill:none" d="M 58.03 1.45 L 107.33 26.1 M 58.37 0 L 106.98 0"></path><path style="fill:none" d="M 113.49 27.56 L 162.1 27.56 M 113.14 1.45 L 162.44 26.1"></path><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#808080;--ltx-fill-color:#808080;--ltx-fg-color:#808080;" color="#808080" fill="#808080" stroke="#808080"><path style="fill:none" d="M 168.61 27.56 L 217.22 27.56 M 168.26 26.1 L 217.56 1.45 M 168.26 1.45 L 217.56 26.1 M 168.61 0 L 217.22 0"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 184.18 69.45 C 184.18 71.02 182.91 72.29 181.34 72.29 C 179.77 72.29 178.5 71.02 178.5 69.45 C 178.5 67.88 179.77 66.61 181.34 66.61 C 182.91 66.61 184.18 67.88 184.18 69.45 Z M 181.34 69.45"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 184.18 55.67 C 184.18 57.24 182.91 58.51 181.34 58.51 C 179.77 58.51 178.5 57.24 178.5 55.67 C 178.5 54.1 179.77 52.83 181.34 52.83 C 182.91 52.83 184.18 54.1 184.18 55.67 Z M 181.34 55.67"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 184.18 41.89 C 184.18 43.46 182.91 44.73 181.34 44.73 C 179.77 44.73 178.5 43.46 178.5 41.89 C 178.5 40.32 179.77 39.05 181.34 39.05 C 182.91 39.05 184.18 40.32 184.18 41.89 Z M 181.34 41.89"></path></g><path style="fill:none" d="M 167.86 80.6 L 178.83 71.52 M 181.34 66.2 L 181.34 58.92 M 181.34 52.42 L 181.34 45.14 M 178.92 39.72 L 167.77 29.73"></path><path style="fill:none" d="M 180.18 38.85 L 166.51 3.04"></path><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 133.72 52.56)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:2.72em;--ltx-fo-height:0.68em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.19em;font-size:9.25pt;" height="11.07" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 8.65)" width="34.88"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><span id="S5.F5.pic2.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">bridge</span></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 25.25 -20.79)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.36em;--ltx-fo-height:0.66em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:9.25pt;" height="8.51" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 8.51)" width="4.62"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S5.F5.pic2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathsize="0.900em">I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{I}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 77.7 -20.79)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.78em;--ltx-fo-height:0.66em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:9.25pt;" height="8.51" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 8.51)" width="9.96"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S5.F5.pic2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{O}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathsize="0.900em">O</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{O}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 135.48 -20.79)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.36em;--ltx-fo-height:0.66em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:9.25pt;" height="8.51" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 8.51)" width="4.62"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S5.F5.pic2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathsize="0.900em">I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{I}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 188.11 -20.79)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.75em;--ltx-fo-height:0.66em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:9.25pt;" height="8.51" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 8.51)" width="9.6"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S5.F5.pic2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathsize="0.900em">N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{N}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 32.01 -31.22)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:3.61em;--ltx-fo-height:0.68em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.19em;font-size:9.25pt;" height="11.07" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 8.65)" width="46.22"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><span id="S5.F5.pic2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">toll gate</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
</span></div></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 5. </span>An example of a path and ladder.
Above: the full strip with root <math id="S5.F5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> and optional terminal vertex <math id="S5.F5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>
and arm; each box hides one period’s internal edges.
Below: the black vertices
form one period; the grey layer starts the next. Dashed vertical lines
mark period boundaries. The shaded layer is a toll gate: the incoming
<math id="S5.F5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{I}</annotation></semantics></math> edges reach only <math id="S5.F5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{4t+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{4t+1}</annotation></semantics></math>, whereas the outgoing
<math id="S5.F5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{O}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>O</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{O}</annotation></semantics></math> edges leave only from <math id="S5.F5.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{4t+1}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>c</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{4t+1}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>. The bridge has three
additional vertices.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S5.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The design which we will use for our explicit counter-example is <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{IOIN}/(-1,-1,-1,3)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>IOIN</mi><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{IOIN}/(-1,-1,-1,3)</annotation></semantics></math>
(Figure <a href="#S5.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ 5.1. The graphs ‣ 5. ℎ-polynomials of graph associahedra are not log-concave ‣ Chow and chain polynomials of matroids are not log-concave" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>). It has four layers and <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="15" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>15</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">15</annotation></semantics></math> vertices per
period. At a layer entered through
<math id="S5.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{I}</annotation></semantics></math> and left through <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{O}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>O</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{O}</annotation></semantics></math>, a crossing confined to the
ladder must use both vertices. We call such a layer a <em id="S5.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">toll gate</em>.
There is one toll gate per period in this design. The heuristics given in Section <a href="#S5.SS3" title="5.3. Why the two parts compete ‣ 5. ℎ-polynomials of graph associahedra are not log-concave ‣ Chow and chain polynomials of matroids are not log-concave" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.3</span></a> explain why toll gates tend to produce failure of log-concavity of rooted multichain counts.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.2. </span>Computing multichain counts by a transfer matrix</h3>

<div id="S5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Fix a design of Path and ladder graph, and a choice of terminal attachment, and write <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{R}</annotation></semantics></math> for
the resulting graph with <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> periods. We now explain how to compute
<math id="S5.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{X_{R}}(j)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>R</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{X_{R}}(j)</annotation></semantics></math> exactly, providing the counts used in Section <a href="#S5.SS4" title="5.4. A counterexample ‣ 5. ℎ-polynomials of graph associahedra are not log-concave ‣ Chow and chain polynomials of matroids are not log-concave" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.4</span></a>.
The method processes one layer at a time and retains only the information
needed to check connectivity. We call the long path and ladder, together with the bridges, the <em id="S5.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">strip</em>; the root and optional terminal attachment are
handled at its two ends.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math>, encode a multichain
<math id="S5.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{p\}=S_{0}\subseteq S_{1}\subseteq\cdots\subseteq S_{j}=V(X_{R})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>⊆</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.1389em">⊆</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.1389em">⋯</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em">⊆</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{p\}=S_{0}\subseteq S_{1}\subseteq\cdots\subseteq S_{j}=V(X_{R})</annotation></semantics></math>
by its <em id="S5.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">entry labels</em></p>
<table id="S5.Ex188" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex188.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h(v)=\min\{t\in\{1,\ldots,j\}:v\in S_{t}\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h(v)=\min\{t\in\{1,\ldots,j\}:v\in S_{t}\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">Thus <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h(p)=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h(p)=1</annotation></semantics></math> by convention, and <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{t}=\{v:h(v)\leqslant t\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⩽</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{t}=\{v:h(v)\leqslant t\}</annotation></semantics></math> for
<math id="S5.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leqslant t\leqslant j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>⩽</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>⩽</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leqslant t\leqslant j</annotation></semantics></math>. Counting multichains amounts to counting labelings
<math id="S5.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h:V(X_{R})\to\{1,\ldots,j\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h:V(X_{R})\to\{1,\ldots,j\}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h(p)=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h(p)=1</annotation></semantics></math> for which these sets are connected.
Since the endpoints are prescribed
and connected, only the thresholds <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leqslant t\leqslant j-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>⩽</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>⩽</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leqslant t\leqslant j-1</annotation></semantics></math> need to be checked.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We construct all possible entry labelings <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math> layer by layer. After processing layer <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>, only <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{i},c_{i},c_{i}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{i},c_{i},c_{i}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> can have neighbours
in the unprocessed part. These three vertices form the <em id="S5.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">frontier</em>.
A <em id="S5.SS2.p3.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">state</em> is a record consisting of their labels and, for each
threshold <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, a partition of</p>
<table id="S5.Ex189" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex189.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{p\}\cup\{v\text{ in the frontier}:h(v)\leqslant t\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> in the frontier</mtext></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⩽</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{p\}\cup\{v\text{ in the frontier}:h(v)\leqslant t\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS2.p3.2" class="ltx_p">Two vertices lie in the same block exactly when they are connected
through processed vertices with labels at most <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>. This includes paths
through vertices no longer in the frontier. We keep <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> as a permanent
marker; its block records the component containing the root.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">A <em id="S5.SS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">transfer</em> updates the counts of partial labelings in each state
when a layer is added. Choose labels for the new vertices and, at each
threshold <math id="S5.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, merge blocks along new edges whose endpoints both have
labels at most <math id="S5.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <em id="S5.SS2.p4.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">forget</em> vertices outside the new frontier:
remove them from the record, retaining the root marker. These vertices
have no unprocessed neighbours. Reject an extension whenever a component
not containing <math id="S5.SS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> loses its last frontier vertex: it can never connect
to the root later, and so the labeling <math id="S5.SS2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math> will not correspond to a chain of connected subsets.
The root component may lose all its frontier vertices,
since <math id="S5.SS2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> can be complete before the strip ends. Apply these rules at
every threshold using the same vertex labels, and sum the counts of
extensions giving the same new state. Bridge vertices are introduced and
forgotten within their layer.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">After processing the terminal attachment, if present, forget the last frontier,
applying the same rejection rule. The accepted labelings are exactly the
desired multichains: disconnected pieces are allowed temporarily, but
each must eventually join the root component. For fixed <math id="S5.SS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>, there are
only finitely many states, independently of <math id="S5.SS2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmthm2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmthm2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 5.2</span></span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmthm2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmthm2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmthm2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Fix a design <math id="S5.Thmthm2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(w,\beta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(w,\beta)</annotation></semantics></math> and a choice of terminal attachment, including
its arm length when present. For each <math id="S5.Thmthm2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math> there are a finite
nonnegative integer matrix <math id="S5.Thmthm2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> and nonnegative integer vectors <math id="S5.Thmthm2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{j},w_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{j},w_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>,
independent of <math id="S5.Thmthm2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math>, such that</span></p>
<table id="S5.E29" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(29)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E29.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{X_{R}}(j)=u_{j}^{\mathsf{T}}T_{j}^{R}w_{j}\qquad(R\geq 1)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>R</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>u</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>𝖳</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>T</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>R</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{X_{R}}(j)=u_{j}^{\mathsf{T}}T_{j}^{R}w_{j}\qquad(R\geq 1).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S5.SS2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS2.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">For <math id="S5.SS2.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j=1</annotation></semantics></math>, there is only the chain <math id="S5.SS2.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{p\}\subseteq V(X_{R})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{p\}\subseteq V(X_{R})</annotation></semantics></math>,
so <math id="S5.SS2.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{X_{R}}(1)=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>R</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{X_{R}}(1)=1</annotation></semantics></math>; take <math id="S5.SS2.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{1}=(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{1}=(1)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS2.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{1}=w_{1}=(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{1}=w_{1}=(1)</annotation></semantics></math>.
For <math id="S5.SS2.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math>, group <math id="S5.SS2.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> successive layer transfers into one period.
At every period boundary, the frontier has the same three positions
and the next period has the same edge pattern. Let <math id="S5.SS2.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{j}(\sigma,\tau)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{j}(\sigma,\tau)</annotation></semantics></math>
count the allowed labelings of one further period taking state <math id="S5.SS2.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math>
to state <math id="S5.SS2.p6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S5.SS2.p6.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{j}(\sigma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{j}(\sigma)</annotation></semantics></math> count initializations through the
first period, including the root edges, and let <math id="S5.SS2.p6.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{j}(\sigma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{j}(\sigma)</annotation></semantics></math> count
accepted terminal extensions, including <math id="S5.SS2.p6.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> and its arm when present.
These are nonnegative integers, and summing over successive states gives
<math id="S5.SS2.p6.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{X_{R}}(j)=v_{j}^{\mathsf{T}}A_{j}^{R-1}\omega_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>R</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>v</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>𝖳</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{X_{R}}(j)=v_{j}^{\mathsf{T}}A_{j}^{R-1}\omega_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS2.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text">To obtain the exponent <math id="S5.SS2.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> in (<a href="#S5.E29" title="Equation 29 ‣ Proposition 5.2 ‣ 5.2. Computing multichain counts by a transfer matrix ‣ 5. ℎ-polynomials of graph associahedra are not log-concave ‣ Chow and chain polynomials of matroids are not log-concave" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">29</span></a>), add one start state
<math id="S5.SS2.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="*" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∗</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">*</annotation></semantics></math>, put <math id="S5.SS2.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{j}(*,\sigma)=v_{j}(\sigma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∗</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{j}(*,\sigma)=v_{j}(\sigma)</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="S5.SS2.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{j}(\sigma,\tau)=A_{j}(\sigma,\tau)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{j}(\sigma,\tau)=A_{j}(\sigma,\tau)</annotation></semantics></math>, and allow no transition into <math id="S5.SS2.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="*" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∗</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">*</annotation></semantics></math>.
Take <math id="S5.SS2.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> supported at <math id="S5.SS2.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="*" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∗</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">*</annotation></semantics></math> with value <math id="S5.SS2.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>, and set <math id="S5.SS2.p7.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{j}(*)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∗</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{j}(*)=0</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="S5.SS2.p7.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{j}(\sigma)=\omega_{j}(\sigma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{j}(\sigma)=\omega_{j}(\sigma)</annotation></semantics></math>. Then
<math id="S5.SS2.p7.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{j}^{\mathsf{T}}T_{j}^{R}w_{j}=v_{j}^{\mathsf{T}}A_{j}^{R-1}\omega_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>u</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>𝖳</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>T</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>R</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>v</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>𝖳</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{j}^{\mathsf{T}}T_{j}^{R}w_{j}=v_{j}^{\mathsf{T}}A_{j}^{R-1}\omega_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>, as required.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.3. </span>Why the two parts compete</h3>

<div id="S5.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This subsection is heuristic. Fix <math id="S5.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math> and consider chains whose
innermost intermediate set <math id="S5.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> crosses many periods. A small crossing along the path uses
one vertex in each layer. A ladder crossing has more route choices,
but must pay for the toll gate with one extra vertex per period.
For the design in Figure <a href="#S5.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ 5.1. The graphs ‣ 5. ℎ-polynomials of graph associahedra are not log-concave ‣ Chow and chain polynomials of matroids are not log-concave" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>, the two crossings therefore
use respectively <math id="S5.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5</annotation></semantics></math> of the <math id="S5.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="15" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>15</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">15</annotation></semantics></math> vertices in a period.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Once <math id="S5.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is fixed, its vertices have label <math id="S5.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>. Each vertex outside
<math id="S5.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> has <math id="S5.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j-1</annotation></semantics></math> possible entry labels, namely <math id="S5.SS3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2,\ldots,j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2,\ldots,j</annotation></semantics></math>.
Consequently, a minimal path crossing leaves <math id="S5.SS3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="11" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>11</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">11</annotation></semantics></math> labels free, whereas
a minimal ladder crossing leaves <math id="S5.SS3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>10</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10</annotation></semantics></math>. This suggests per-period
contributions of the forms</p>
<table id="S5.E30" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(30)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E30.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{\mathrm{P}}(j-1)^{11}\qquad\text{and}\qquad m_{\mathrm{L}}(j-1)^{10}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>11</mn></msup></mrow><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace><mtext>and</mtext><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>10</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{\mathrm{P}}(j-1)^{11}\qquad\text{and}\qquad m_{\mathrm{L}}(j-1)^{10}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS3.p2.2" class="ltx_p">The coefficients absorb route choices and connectivity constraints;
the free labels cannot in general be chosen independently.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">For example, suppose <math id="S5.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> new vertices form a path attached to a vertex in
<math id="S5.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, with all <math id="S5.SS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> vertices outside <math id="S5.SS3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and no other connection to the
graph. Its labels, ordered away from the
attachment, must satisfy</p>
<table id="S5.Ex190" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex190.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2\leqslant h_{1}\leq\cdots\leqslant h_{s}\leqslant j." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>⩽</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.1389em">≤</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.1389em">⋯</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em">⩽</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>⩽</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\leqslant h_{1}\leq\cdots\leqslant h_{s}\leqslant j.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS3.p3.2" class="ltx_p">There are <math id="S5.SS3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\binom{j+s-2}{s}\sim(j-1)^{s}/s!" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mi>s</mi></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>∼</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>s</mi></msup><mo>/</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>!</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\binom{j+s-2}{s}\sim(j-1)^{s}/s!</annotation></semantics></math> choices. Thus the exponent still
counts free vertices, but the leading coefficient can be fractional.
The quantities <math id="S5.SS3.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{\mathrm{P}},m_{\mathrm{L}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>L</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{\mathrm{P}},m_{\mathrm{L}}</annotation></semantics></math> in (<a href="#S5.E30" title="Equation 30 ‣ 5.3. Why the two parts compete ‣ 5. ℎ-polynomials of graph associahedra are not log-concave ‣ Chow and chain polynomials of matroids are not log-concave" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">30</span></a>)
should be understood in this sense, rather than as integer numbers of
routes. In the ladder regime, multiple routes help compensate for the
lost power of <math id="S5.SS3.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j-1</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">More generally, if a path crossing leaves <math id="S5.SS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>e</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e</annotation></semantics></math> free vertices and the
ladder pays a toll of <math id="S5.SS3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math> extra vertices per period, the comparison
is between <math id="S5.SS3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{\mathrm{P}}(j-1)^{e}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>e</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{\mathrm{P}}(j-1)^{e}</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="S5.SS3.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{\mathrm{L}}(j-1)^{e-\Delta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{\mathrm{L}}(j-1)^{e-\Delta}</annotation></semantics></math>. When <math id="S5.SS3.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{\mathrm{L}}&gt;m_{\mathrm{P}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>P</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{\mathrm{L}}&gt;m_{\mathrm{P}}</annotation></semantics></math>, their
balance occurs at</p>
<table id="S5.Ex191" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex191.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j_{*}=1+(m_{\mathrm{L}}/m_{\mathrm{P}})^{1/\Delta}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>j</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>P</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j_{*}=1+(m_{\mathrm{L}}/m_{\mathrm{P}})^{1/\Delta}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS3.p4.2" class="ltx_p">Below this scale the ladder’s route choices can dominate; above it, the
path’s extra free labels can dominate. Here <math id="S5.SS3.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta=1</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p">This change can favour log-convexity even though each separate power
of <math id="S5.SS3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j-1</annotation></semantics></math> is log-concave. Indeed, the logarithmic slopes of the two
expressions are respectively <math id="S5.SS3.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(e-\Delta)/(j-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(e-\Delta)/(j-1)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS3.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e/(j-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e/(j-1)</annotation></semantics></math>. The dominant contribution
therefore changes towards a larger slope at the balance point, which can lead to a failure of log-concavity for multichain counts.
Repeating the period many times reinforces the competition.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Finally, sparse bridges make it costly for a crossing to change tracks.
To pass between the path and ladder within a period, <math id="S5.SS3.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> must contain
an entire bridge. Each additional bridge vertex then has label <math id="S5.SS3.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>
instead of approximately <math id="S5.SS3.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j-1</annotation></semantics></math> later-entry choices. Long enough bridges,
placed only in a few layers, can therefore suppress mixed crossings and
preserve the competition between the two regimes. Whether the resulting
finite graph actually fails log-concavity is decided by the exact
transfer computation, as in the certificate below.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.4. </span>A counterexample</h3>

<div id="S5.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We now fix one graph and leave the heuristic behind. Take</p>
<table id="S5.Ex192" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex192.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G=X\bigl(\mathsf{IOIN},(-1,-1,-1,3);98,68\bigr)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mi>𝖨𝖮𝖨𝖭</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>98</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>68</mn><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G=X\bigl(\mathsf{IOIN},(-1,-1,-1,3);98,68\bigr),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS4.p1.2" class="ltx_p">rooted at <math id="S5.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>, with the terminal vertex and arm included. There are
<math id="S5.SS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="392" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>392</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">392</annotation></semantics></math> layers, <math id="S5.SS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="98" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>98</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">98</annotation></semantics></math> bridges with three new vertices each, and <math id="S5.SS4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="68" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>68</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">68</annotation></semantics></math> arm
vertices. Consequently</p>
<table id="S5.Ex193" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex193.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|V(G)|=2+3\cdot 392+3\cdot 98+68=1540." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mn>392</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mn>98</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>68</mn></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1540</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|V(G)|=2+3\cdot 392+3\cdot 98+68=1540.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS4.p1.3" class="ltx_p">The graph has <math id="S5.SS4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2323" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2323</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2323</annotation></semantics></math> edges. Its toll layers are precisely those with
index <math id="S5.SS4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\equiv 1\pmod{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≡</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mspace style="width:0.949em;" width="0.949em"></mspace><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">mod</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\equiv 1\pmod{4}</annotation></semantics></math>; its bridges lie in layers <math id="S5.SS4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\equiv 3\pmod{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≡</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mspace style="width:0.949em;" width="0.949em"></mspace><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">mod</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\equiv 3\pmod{4}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmthm3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmthm3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 5.3</span></span><span id="S5.Thmthm3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Computer-assisted witness)</h6>
<div id="S5.Thmthm3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmthm3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmthm3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For this rooted graph,</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex194" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex194.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{G}(37)^{2}&lt;Z_{G}(36)Z_{G}(38)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>37</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>36</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>38</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{G}(37)^{2}&lt;Z_{G}(36)Z_{G}(38).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.Thmthm3.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmthm3.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For every integer <math id="S5.Thmthm3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q\geq 23" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>23</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q\geq 23</annotation></semantics></math>, the graph <math id="S5.Thmthm3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{q}(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{q}(G)</annotation></semantics></math> satisfies</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex195" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex195.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{36}(W_{q}(G))^{2}&lt;h_{35}(W_{q}(G))h_{37}(W_{q}(G))." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mn>36</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mn>35</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mn>37</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{36}(W_{q}(G))^{2}&lt;h_{35}(W_{q}(G))h_{37}(W_{q}(G)).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.Thmthm3.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmthm3.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Thus its <math id="S5.Thmthm3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math>-polynomial is neither log-concave nor real-rooted.
The graph <math id="S5.Thmthm3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{2}3(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{2}3(G)</annotation></semantics></math> has <math id="S5.Thmthm3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1+23\cdot 1539=35{,}398" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>23</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mn>1539</mn></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>35,398</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1+23\cdot 1539=35{,}398</annotation></semantics></math> vertices.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<section id="S5.SS4.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">5.4.1. </span>Computational input and its verification</h4>

<div id="S5.SS4.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS4.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The input consists of the integer chain counts <math id="S5.SS4.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{G}(j)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{G}(j)</annotation></semantics></math> for
<math id="S5.SS4.SSS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq j\leq 38" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>38</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq j\leq 38</annotation></semantics></math> and the integer <math id="S5.SS4.SSS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{G}=|\mathcal{B}(G-p)|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{G}=|\mathcal{B}(G-p)|</annotation></semantics></math>. The certificate below uses only coarse inequalities implied by these
computations. Put</p>
<table id="S5.Ex196" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex196.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho=\frac{Z_{G}(37)^{2}}{Z_{G}(36)Z_{G}(38)}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>37</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>36</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>38</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho=\frac{Z_{G}(37)^{2}}{Z_{G}(36)Z_{G}(38)}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS4.SSS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">The required inputs are</p>
<table id="S5.E31" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(31)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E31.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{gathered}t_{G}&lt;2^{881},\qquad Z_{G}(a+1)&gt;10^{12}Z_{G}(a)\quad(1\leq a&lt;38),\\
\rho&lt;\frac{9998}{10000}.\end{gathered}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mi>t</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>G</mi></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>881</mn></msup><mo rspace="4.167em">,</mo><mi>Z</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>G</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>10</mn><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>12</mn></msup><mi>Z</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>G</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo rspace="1.000em" stretchy="false">)</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>38</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mfrac><mn>9998</mn><mn>10000</mn></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{gathered}t_{G}&lt;2^{881},\qquad Z_{G}(a+1)&gt;10^{12}Z_{G}(a)\quad(1\leq a&lt;38),\\
\rho&lt;\frac{9998}{10000}.\end{gathered}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS4.SSS1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">The three central integers have <math id="S5.SS4.SSS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1347" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1347</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1347</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.SS4.SSS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1359" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1359</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1359</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S5.SS4.SSS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1371" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1371</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1371</annotation></semantics></math> decimal
digits, respectively. To indicate their scale, the
calculation gives</p>
<table id="S5.Ex197" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex197.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{aligned} \log_{10}Z_{G}(36)&amp;\approx 1346.6221,\\
\log_{10}Z_{G}(37)&amp;\approx 1358.6728,\\
\log_{10}Z_{G}(38)&amp;\approx 1370.7236,\end{aligned}\qquad\log\rho\approx-0.0002694." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtable columnspacing="0pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>10</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>G</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>36</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≈</mo><mn>1346.6221</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>10</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>G</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>37</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≈</mo><mn>1358.6728</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>10</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>G</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>38</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≈</mo><mn>1370.7236</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mspace style="width:2.167em;" width="2.167em"></mspace><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>ρ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>≈</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>0.0002694</mn></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{aligned} \log_{10}Z_{G}(36)&amp;\approx 1346.6221,\\
\log_{10}Z_{G}(37)&amp;\approx 1358.6728,\\
\log_{10}Z_{G}(38)&amp;\approx 1370.7236,\end{aligned}\qquad\log\rho\approx-0.0002694.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS4.SSS1.p1.4" class="ltx_p">These decimal displays are illustrations, not substitutes for the
integer comparisons in (<a href="#S5.E31" title="Equation 31 ‣ 5.4.1. Computational input and its verification ‣ 5.4. A counterexample ‣ 5. ℎ-polynomials of graph associahedra are not log-concave ‣ Chow and chain polynomials of matroids are not log-concave" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">31</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS4.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS4.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The chain counts were obtained with multiprecision integer
arithmetic by a transfer over the layers. An independently written
program used directed rounding to give upper bounds for all
<math id="S5.SS4.SSS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq j\leq 38" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>38</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq j\leq 38</annotation></semantics></math> and lower bounds for <math id="S5.SS4.SSS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j=36,37,38" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>36</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>37</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>38</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j=36,37,38</annotation></semantics></math>. The resulting brackets,
of relative width at most <math id="S5.SS4.SSS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5.9\cdot 10^{-12}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>5.9</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>12</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5.9\cdot 10^{-12}</annotation></semantics></math>, contained the exact
integers. The number <math id="S5.SS4.SSS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{G}</annotation></semantics></math> was counted by two independent frontier
programs; it has <math id="S5.SS4.SSS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="881" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>881</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">881</annotation></semantics></math> bits. The implementations were also checked
against direct enumeration and generic frontier computations on
smaller test instances.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS4.SSS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS4.SSS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">For the layer transfer, bridge vertices are summed out inside their
layer. The frontier entry times and connectivity data are those
described in Section <a href="#S5.SS2" title="5.2. Computing multichain counts by a transfer matrix ‣ 5. ℎ-polynomials of graph associahedra are not log-concave ‣ Chow and chain polynomials of matroids are not log-concave" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.2</span></a>; the terminal attachment and arm
are included in the final weights. All operations in the exact count
are sums and products of nonnegative integers. In the interval
implementation, the corresponding operations are rounded downwards
and upwards to enclose the count. No asymptotic estimate of a
channel growth rate is used in this computation.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS4.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">5.4.2. </span>A short certificate for every <math id="S5.SS4.SSS2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q\geq 23" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>23</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q\geq 23</annotation></semantics></math></h4>

<div id="S5.SS4.SSS2.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof of <a href="#S5.Thmthm3" title="Theorem 5.3 (Computer-assisted witness) ‣ 5.4. A counterexample ‣ 5. ℎ-polynomials of graph associahedra are not log-concave ‣ Chow and chain polynomials of matroids are not log-concave" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.3</span></a> from the computational input.</h6>
<div id="S5.SS4.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS4.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS4.SSS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text">The last inequality of (<a href="#S5.E31" title="Equation 31 ‣ 5.4.1. Computational input and its verification ‣ 5.4. A counterexample ‣ 5. ℎ-polynomials of graph associahedra are not log-concave ‣ Chow and chain polynomials of matroids are not log-concave" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">31</span></a>) already proves the zeta
failure. It remains to apply the error bound of Theorem <a href="#S2.Thmthm13" title="Theorem 2.13 (coefficient estimate) ‣ 2.5.4. An explicit amplification certificate ‣ 2.5. Face enumerations of graph associahedra ‣ 2. Polynomial invariants and multichain counts ‣ Chow and chain polynomials of matroids are not log-concave" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.13</span></a>.
Set <math id="S5.SS4.SSS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=1540" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1540</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=1540</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS4.SSS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c=n-1+t_{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>G</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c=n-1+t_{G}</annotation></semantics></math>. By (<a href="#S5.E31" title="Equation 31 ‣ 5.4.1. Computational input and its verification ‣ 5.4. A counterexample ‣ 5. ℎ-polynomials of graph associahedra are not log-concave ‣ Chow and chain polynomials of matroids are not log-concave" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">31</span></a>), <math id="S5.SS4.SSS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c&lt;2^{882}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mn>882</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c&lt;2^{882}</annotation></semantics></math>.
For <math id="S5.SS4.SSS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j\in\{35,36,37\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>35</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>36</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>37</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j\in\{35,36,37\}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS4.SSS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m=j-a\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m=j-a\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>, successive multiplication
of the zeta-ratio bounds gives</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex198" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex198.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{Z_{G}(j+1-m)}{Z_{G}(j+1)}&lt;10^{-12m}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo>&lt;</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>12</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{Z_{G}(j+1-m)}{Z_{G}(j+1)}&lt;10^{-12m}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS4.SSS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS4.SSS2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text">Since <math id="S5.SS4.SSS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="23\cdot 2^{882}+37&lt;10^{268}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>23</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mn>882</mn></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>37</mn></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>268</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">23\cdot 2^{882}+37&lt;10^{268}</annotation></semantics></math>, the error at <math id="S5.SS4.SSS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q=23" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>23</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q=23</annotation></semantics></math> satisfies</span></p>
<table id="S5.EGx8" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S5.Ex199"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex199.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle E_{j}(23)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>23</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle E_{j}(23)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex199.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\sum_{m=1}^{j}\left(\frac{Z_{G}(j+1-m)}{Z_{G}(j+1)}\right)^{23}\binom{23c+m}{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>j</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow><mn>23</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mrow><mrow><mn>23</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mi>m</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\sum_{m=1}^{j}\left(\frac{Z_{G}(j+1-m)}{Z_{G}(j+1)}\right)^{23}\binom{23c+m}{m}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S5.Ex200"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex200.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle&lt;\sum_{m=1}^{j}10^{-276m}(23c+m)^{m}&lt;\sum_{m\geq 1}10^{-8m}&lt;2\cdot 10^{-8}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>j</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>276</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>23</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>m</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munder></mstyle><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>8</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle&lt;\sum_{m=1}^{j}10^{-276m}(23c+m)^{m}&lt;\sum_{m\geq 1}10^{-8m}&lt;2\cdot 10^{-8}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS4.SSS2.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS4.SSS2.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text">Writing <math id="S5.SS4.SSS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon=2\cdot 10^{-8}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon=2\cdot 10^{-8}</annotation></semantics></math>, exact rational arithmetic gives</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex201" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex201.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho^{23}\frac{(1+E_{36}(23))^{2}}{(1-E_{35}(23))(1-E_{37}(23))}&lt;\left(\frac{9998}{10000}\right)^{23}\frac{(1+\varepsilon)^{2}}{(1-\varepsilon)^{2}}&lt;1." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>ρ</mi><mn>23</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>36</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>23</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>35</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>23</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>37</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>23</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mn>9998</mn><mn>10000</mn></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><mn>23</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho^{23}\frac{(1+E_{36}(23))^{2}}{(1-E_{35}(23))(1-E_{37}(23))}&lt;\left(\frac{9998}{10000}\right)^{23}\frac{(1+\varepsilon)^{2}}{(1-\varepsilon)^{2}}&lt;1.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS4.SSS2.p1.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS4.SSS2.p1.4.1" class="ltx_text">This proves the <math id="S5.SS4.SSS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math>-inequality for <math id="S5.SS4.SSS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q=23" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>23</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q=23</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS4.SSS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS4.SSS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS4.SSS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">To cover all larger integers, consider an individual error summand
<math id="S5.SS4.SSS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r^{q}\binom{qc+m}{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>r</mi><mi>q</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mi>m</mi></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r^{q}\binom{qc+m}{m}</annotation></semantics></math>, where
<math id="S5.SS4.SSS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=Z_{G}(j+1-m)/Z_{G}(j+1)&lt;10^{-12m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>G</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>12</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=Z_{G}(j+1-m)/Z_{G}(j+1)&lt;10^{-12m}</annotation></semantics></math>. Its ratio at <math id="S5.SS4.SSS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q+1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS4.SSS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> is</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex202" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex202.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\prod_{s=1}^{m}\frac{(q+1)c+s}{qc+s}\leq r\left(1+\frac{1}{q}\right)^{m}&lt;10^{-12m}\left(\frac{24}{23}\right)^{m}&lt;1\qquad(q\geq 23)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∏</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>m</mi></munderover><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>q</mi></mfrac></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mi>m</mi></msup></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>12</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mn>24</mn><mn>23</mn></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><mi>m</mi></msup></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>23</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\prod_{s=1}^{m}\frac{(q+1)c+s}{qc+s}\leq r\left(1+\frac{1}{q}\right)^{m}&lt;10^{-12m}\left(\frac{24}{23}\right)^{m}&lt;1\qquad(q\geq 23).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS4.SSS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS4.SSS2.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text">Thus every <math id="S5.SS4.SSS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{j}(q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{j}(q)</annotation></semantics></math> decreases, as does <math id="S5.SS4.SSS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho^{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ρ</mi><mi>q</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho^{q}</annotation></semantics></math>. The amplification
criterion remains valid for every <math id="S5.SS4.SSS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q\geq 23" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>23</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q\geq 23</annotation></semantics></math>. Newton’s inequalities
exclude real-rootedness, and the vertex count follows from the
definition of a wedge.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS4.SSS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS4.SSS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The value <math id="S5.SS4.SSS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="23" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>23</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">23</annotation></semantics></math> is a certified sufficient number of copies. This
argument does not determine the first <math id="S5.SS4.SSS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> for which the family
fails log-concavity. In particular, a zeta failure for the single
rooted core is not by itself a certificate of an <math id="S5.SS4.SSS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math>-failure for
that core.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS4.SSS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS4.SSS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#S5.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 5.4.2. A short certificate for every 𝑞≥23 ‣ 5.4. A counterexample ‣ 5. ℎ-polynomials of graph associahedra are not log-concave ‣ Chow and chain polynomials of matroids are not log-concave" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> below gives an account of other patterns leading to failures of log-concavity of multichain counts. Each entry describes the ladder pattern, the number of periods used, the size of the arm, the size of the path and ladder graph, the index of the failure, the natural logarithm of <math id="S5.SS4.SSS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho=Z_{X}(k)^{2}/(Z_{X}(k-1)Z_{X}(k+1))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho=Z_{X}(k)^{2}/(Z_{X}(k-1)Z_{X}(k+1))</annotation></semantics></math>, the big length of <math id="S5.SS4.SSS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{X}</annotation></semantics></math>, the number of wedges used to produce a failure for <math id="S5.SS4.SSS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math>. The status entry specifies if the computations have been verified once or twice.</p>
</div>
